window.EXAM_AIDS = {"cards": [{"module": 6, "title": "Sperling: whole and partial report", "pages": [13, 15, 16, 17, 18], "researcher": "Sperling", "aim": "How much does sensory (iconic) memory hold, and how long does it last?", "manipulated": "The report method: whole report (name as many letters as you can), partial report (a tone cues one row right away), and delayed partial report (the tone comes after a delay). The displays were three rows of four letters.", "measured": "How many letters people could report from a briefly flashed letter array.", "found": "In whole report people named about a third of the letters. In partial report they named most of the cued row, for any row. In delayed partial report they named only about one letter, and by about one second partial report fell to near the whole-report level.", "means": "Sensory memory has a large capacity but fast decay; the report limit came from the image fading, not from too little being stored.", "trap": "Whole report makes capacity look like four or five items, but that is a limit on reporting, not on storage."}, {"module": 6, "title": "Brown–Peterson: delay and trial history", "pages": [24, 25, 26, 27, 28, 29, 30], "researcher": "Brown and Peterson and Peterson", "aim": "How fast does short-term memory fade when rehearsal is blocked?", "manipulated": "The delay before recall (a few seconds up to about eighteen seconds) and where a trial fell in the series (first trial, third trial, many trials later).", "measured": "Recall of three letters after counting backward by threes during the delay.", "found": "On the first trial recall stayed high even after the long delay. On later trials recall after the long delay dropped, and after many trials it fell to almost nothing. Averaged over all trials, recall fell with delay.", "means": "Forgetting in short-term memory comes from proactive interference as well as decay; earlier trigrams made the new one harder to keep.", "trap": "The average curve looks like pure decay over time, but it hides that the big drops came from later trials."}, {"module": 6, "title": "Visual change detection and complexity", "pages": [37, 39, 41, 42, 43, 44], "researcher": "Alvarez and Cavanagh", "aim": "Is visual short-term memory capacity set by the number of items or by the amount of information in them?", "manipulated": "Set size (number of colored squares), what changed in colored bars (angle, color, or both), and object complexity (colored squares, Chinese characters, random polygons, shaded cubes).", "measured": "Whether people judged a second display (S2) the same as or different from a briefly flashed first display (S1).", "found": "Accuracy fell dramatically above about four items. Remembering two features of a bar was about as easy as one. Capacity fell as objects got more complex, from about four for colored squares to fewer than two for shaded cubes.", "means": "Visual short-term memory holds about three to four whole objects, and capacity depends on how much information each object carries.", "trap": "Do not say the limit is always seven items; for visual memory the slides give about four, and fewer for complex objects."}, {"module": 6, "title": "Digit-span training and chess expertise", "pages": [45, 46], "researcher": "Ericsson and colleagues; Chase and Simon", "aim": "Can chunking and knowledge let people hold much more in short-term memory?", "manipulated": "For Ericsson: training a college student on digit span. For Chase and Simon: player skill (masters vs beginners) and board type (real-game arrangement vs random arrangement).", "measured": "Digit span for the trained student; number of chess pieces placed correctly after a brief look at a board.", "found": "The trained student's digit span rose to around eighty digits by chunking digits into running times. Masters placed far more pieces than beginners on real-game boards but were no better on random boards.", "means": "Chunking with knowledge from long-term memory stretches capacity; experts do not have bigger short-term memories.", "trap": "The masters' advantage disappears with random boards, so it is knowledge of real patterns, not a larger store."}, {"module": 9, "title": "Conrad · sound-based errors after visual presentation", "pages": [8, 9], "researcher": "Conrad (1964)", "aim": "When letters are seen, are errors based on how they look or how they sound?", "manipulated": "Whether the flashed letters were similar or not similar (half of the presentations each).", "measured": "Writing down the letters after a brief visual flash.", "found": "Errors more often swapped in a letter that sounded like the target (E replaced by B) than one that looked like it (F for E).", "means": "Seen letters are recoded into a sound-based form in working memory (phonological similarity effect).", "trap": "The letters came in through vision, but the confusions followed sound; presentation format is not the same as memory code."}, {"module": 9, "title": "Word length, bilingual digit span, and articulatory suppression", "pages": [10, 11], "researcher": "Baddeley and colleagues (1975, 1984); Ellis and Hennelly (1980)", "aim": "What limits how much verbal material the phonological loop can hold?", "manipulated": "Word length (short vs long words), language of the digits (Welsh vs English for bilingual children), and speaking while memorizing (articulatory suppression).", "measured": "Memory for word lists and digit span.", "found": "People remembered more short words than long words. Welsh-English bilingual children had a shorter digit span in Welsh because Welsh number names take longer to say. Suppression lowered recall and reduced both the word-length and phonological similarity effects.", "means": "Verbal span depends on how much can be rehearsed in about 1.5 to 2 seconds, and speech-based rehearsal is used to maintain verbal information.", "trap": "The Welsh-English gap comes from pronunciation time, not from the numbers' values."}, {"module": 9, "title": "Brooks · sentence and F dual-task comparisons", "pages": [13, 14, 15], "researcher": "Brooks (1968)", "aim": "Does a response interfere more when it uses the same kind of memory as the thing being held?", "manipulated": "Response mode: speaking vs pointing, for two memory tasks (a memorized sentence with noun judgments, and a memorized F with inside/outside corner judgments).", "measured": "How easy each task was under each response mode.", "found": "With the sentence, pointing was easier than speaking. With the F, speaking was easier than pointing.", "means": "Interference depends on the combination of tasks, which supports some independence between verbal and visual or spatial memory.", "trap": "The results reverse across the two tasks, so neither speaking nor pointing is easier overall."}, {"module": 9, "title": "Shepard and Metzler · angle and mental-rotation time", "pages": [17], "researcher": "Shepard and Metzler (1971)", "aim": "Do people mentally rotate objects, and does more rotation take more time?", "manipulated": "The angle of rotation between pairs of block-built objects (picture-plane and depth rotation).", "measured": "Reaction time to judge whether the two objects matched.", "found": "For picture-plane pairs, reaction time increased as the angle increased, approximately linearly.", "means": "Larger angles need a larger mental transformation, consistent with an active operation on a spatial representation.", "trap": "Picture-plane rotation turns within the flat plane; depth rotation turns in three-dimensional space."}, {"module": 9, "title": "Vogel · relevant load, distractors, and filtering efficiency", "pages": [20, 21, 22], "researcher": "Vogel and colleagues (2005)", "aim": "Do people with higher working-memory capacity keep irrelevant items out better?", "manipulated": "Capacity group (higher vs lower) and display type: two relevant red bars, four relevant red bars, or two red bars plus two irrelevant blue bars.", "measured": "A memory-related ERP response during the 900 ms retention interval, plus a change judgment on the relevant orientations.", "found": "High-capacity people showed a larger response for four items than two, and little change when blue distractors were added. For low-capacity people, adding distractors raised the response toward the four-item level. Capacity correlated positively with filtering efficiency.", "means": "Capacity depends on which items get in as well as how many targets there are; better filtering goes with higher capacity.", "trap": "A bigger response means a stronger negative deflection, not a more positive voltage."}, {"module": 10, "title": "Köhler · circle representation and equal diagonals", "pages": [3], "researcher": "Köhler", "aim": "Can reorganizing how a problem is represented make a hidden relationship usable?", "manipulated": "Not a comparison of groups; it is a demonstration of how representing the circle problem as a rectangle changes the solution.", "measured": "Finding the length of line x when the circle's radius is r.", "found": "Seeing the black line as a diagonal of a rectangle makes it equal to the dashed radius (the other diagonal), so x = r.", "means": "Restructuring the representation, not new calculation, makes the answer available.", "trap": "Do not treat x as an isolated unknown inside the circle; the key is spotting the rectangle with equal diagonals."}, {"module": 10, "title": "Metcalfe and Wiebe · insight puzzles and warmth judgments", "pages": [5, 6, 7], "researcher": "Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)", "aim": "Do people feel gradually closer to a solution for insight problems as they do for noninsight problems?", "manipulated": "Problem type: insight problems (triangle and chain) vs noninsight problems (algebra).", "measured": "Warmth ratings (hot means close to the solution) given every 15 seconds while solving.", "found": "For algebra, warmth rose gradually as the solution approached. For insight problems, warmth stayed low until close to the solution and then jumped sharply, from about 3 to 7.", "means": "Insight solutions come with a sudden change in felt progress, unlike methodical noninsight solving.", "trap": "Not all problem solving is sudden; the sudden jump describes insight problems, not algebra."}, {"module": 10, "title": "Candle problem · matchbox presentation and functional fixedness", "pages": [8], "researcher": "", "aim": "Does seeing an object only in its familiar use block its use for something else?", "manipulated": "Whether the matches were inside the box or the matchboxes were empty.", "measured": "Whether participants solved the problem of mounting the candle on a vertical corkboard so it burns without dripping wax on the floor.", "found": "Participants given empty matchboxes were twice as likely to solve the problem.", "means": "Functional fixedness: treating the box only as a container hides its use as a platform for the candle.", "trap": "The materials were enough to solve it; the obstacle was the familiar use of the box."}, {"module": 10, "title": "Two-string problem · pliers as a weight and a movement hint", "pages": [9, 10], "researcher": "", "aim": "What helps people see a new use for an object in the two-string problem?", "manipulated": "Whether an experimenter 'accidentally' set a string in motion (the hint).", "measured": "Whether participants solved the problem of tying together two strings hanging too far apart to reach.", "found": "After the swinging hint, most participants were able to solve the problem. The solution is to tie the pliers on a string as a weight and make it swing like a pendulum.", "means": "The hint helped restructure the representation, so the string became something that can be swung within reach.", "trap": "The pliers' useful property is their weight, not their ability to grip; that is the functional fixedness."}, {"module": 10, "title": "Water-jug problems · practice and a simpler available solution", "pages": [11], "researcher": "", "aim": "Can a method that worked earlier block a simpler solution?", "manipulated": "Practice with the early problems that Practice with early problems solved by B − A − 2C, then new problems (7 and 8) with capacities where a simpler solution exists.", "measured": "Not measured with participants on the slide; it traces which solution method each problem allows.", "found": "Repeated success with B − A − 2C builds a mental set. In problem 7 the goal 18 can be reached by A + C, and in problem 8 the goal 25 by A − C, but the familiar method still works and is brought first.", "means": "A mental set can inhibit a simpler solution even when the familiar one remains possible.", "trap": "The familiar method still works in problems 7 and 8; the set blocks the simpler one, not the correct answer."}, {"module": 10, "title": "Search demonstrations · tile puzzle and Tower of Hanoi subgoals", "pages": [13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18], "researcher": "Newell and Simon", "aim": "How can solving be described as search through a problem space using operators and subgoals?", "manipulated": "Nothing varied; these are worked demonstrations with the Tower of Hanoi and a numbered-tile puzzle.", "measured": "Not measured with participants; the slides trace states, legal moves, and subgoals.", "found": "Operators set which moves are legal; routes pass through intermediate states; a search tree shows branches of alternative states. In the Tower of Hanoi, subgoals free the large disk, free the third peg, then move the large disk.", "means": "Means–end analysis reduces the difference between the current state and the goal by achieving subgoals.", "trap": "A subgoal is an intermediate condition that makes a move possible, not the final answer."}, {"module": 10, "title": "Mutilated checkerboard · labels, pairing, and think-aloud protocols", "pages": [19, 20, 21, 22], "researcher": "", "aim": "Does how a problem is stated change how hard it is to find the pairing constraint?", "manipulated": "Information on the board: blank, colored, labeled black and pink, or labeled bread and butter.", "measured": "How fast participants solved the covering problem, along with think-aloud reports of their thoughts.", "found": "Participants solved the bread-and-butter condition twice as fast as the blank condition. Think-aloud reports showed a shift from squares as positions to the relation between the two kinds of square.", "means": "Labels pointing to the right representation (one of each kind per domino) reduce solving time, matching Gestalt restructuring.", "trap": "Equal total area (62 squares, 31 dominoes) does not mean a covering is possible; the colors are unequal."}, {"module": 11, "title": "Marriage and checkerboard · mapping a shared pairing constraint", "pages": [3, 4, 5], "researcher": "", "aim": "Can a solver see why 31 pairs are impossible when the two groups are unequal?", "manipulated": "The number of men and women (32 and 32, then 30 and 32), and the matching board problem with two same-colored corners removed.", "measured": "Whether 31 heterosexual marriages (or 31 dominoes) can be formed.", "found": "No. With 30 men and 32 women, at most 30 marriages are possible. The checkerboard has the same imbalance between square colors, so 31 dominoes cannot cover it.", "means": "The solution comes from the one-of-each pairing relationship, not from total size or area.", "trap": "Total count (62 people or 62 squares) says 31 pairs, but the pairing rule is what decides it."}, {"module": 11, "title": "Duncker and Gick–Holyoak · radiation, fortress, and reminders", "pages": [6, 7, 8, 9, 10], "researcher": "Duncker; Gick and Holyoak", "aim": "Do people notice and use a solved source story (the fortress) to solve the radiation problem?", "manipulated": "Whether participants read the fortress story first, and whether they were told to think about that story.", "measured": "Whether participants reached the converging-rays solution to the radiation problem.", "found": "About 10% solved radiation without the analogy. After reading the fortress story, about 30% solved it. When told to think about the story, about 75% did.", "means": "Having a useful source in memory is not enough. People must notice it and map it to the target.", "trap": "Many people read the source but do not spontaneously use it. The hint is the difference between 30% and 75%."}, {"module": 11, "title": "Lightbulb transfer · surface and structural similarities", "pages": [11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16], "researcher": "", "aim": "Does a source with similar surface features, or similar structure, help solve a target problem?", "manipulated": "Which source participants knew: radiation, fragile-glass lightbulb, or insufficient-intensity lightbulb. A control group had no source.", "measured": "Whether participants solved the target (lightbulb or radiation).", "found": "81% who knew radiation solved the lightbulb problem, versus 10% in the control group. 69% who read the fragile-glass solution solved radiation, versus 33% after the insufficient-intensity version.", "means": "Matching structure (a strong effect at the target plus protected surroundings) helps more than matching only the objects.", "trap": "Both lightbulb sources use converging lasers, but only the fragile-glass one shares the reason (damage to surroundings)."}, {"module": 11, "title": "In vivo observation · analogies in scientific work", "pages": [17], "researcher": "", "aim": "How often do real scientists use analogies during ordinary work?", "manipulated": "Nothing was manipulated. Researchers observed scientists in real-world lab meetings.", "measured": "The number of analogies used in a one-hour laboratory meeting.", "found": "Scientists use about 3-15 analogies in a one-hour laboratory meeting.", "means": "Analogies are common in real work even though they are hard to transfer in lab tasks (the analogical paradox).", "trap": "In vivo gives real context but little control of variables, and it can be time-consuming."}, {"module": 11, "title": "Chase and Simon · game positions, random layouts, and chunks", "pages": [19], "researcher": "Chase and Simon (1973)", "aim": "Is expert chess recall due to general memory or to organized knowledge?", "manipulated": "Skill (chess master versus a beginner with under 100 hours) and the arrangement type (actual game positions versus random arrangements).", "measured": "Reproducing the piece positions after viewing the board for five seconds.", "found": "The expert did much better than the beginner on actual game positions. The same familiar chunks were not available for random arrangements.", "means": "Expert memory depends on chunks stored in long-term memory, not on a general memory advantage.", "trap": "Do not say experts remember everything better. The advantage is tied to meaningful positions."}, {"module": 11, "title": "Chi and colleagues · surface-based and principle-based sorting", "pages": [20, 21], "researcher": "Chi et al. (1982)", "aim": "Do experts and novices organize physics problems in the same way?", "manipulated": "Group: experts (physics professors) versus novices (students with one semester of physics).", "measured": "Sorting 24 physics problems into groups by similarity.", "found": "Novices grouped by surface features, such as inclined planes. Experts grouped by structural features, such as conservation of energy.", "means": "Expertise changes which relationship is used as the basis for similarity.", "trap": "The same problem 24 pairs with 23 (novice, inclined planes) or with 21 (expert, conservation of energy)."}, {"module": 12, "title": "Availability judgments · letter positions and causes of death", "pages": [6, 7, 8], "researcher": "", "aim": "Do people judge how likely something is by how easily examples come to mind?", "manipulated": "The letter question (r first versus r third) and paired causes of death (for example, botulism versus asthma).", "measured": "Choosing which alternative is more frequent or more likely.", "found": "About 70% say more words begin with r, though words with r third are three times more common. Many pick the dramatic cause of death over the more common one such as asthma.", "means": "Ease of remembering is used as a cue for probability, and it is affected by things other than frequency.", "trap": "Memorable or newsworthy is not the same as common."}, {"module": 12, "title": "Representativeness · Robert, Jack, and Linda", "pages": [9, 10, 11], "researcher": "", "aim": "Do people use resemblance instead of base rates and probability rules?", "manipulated": "The description given (Robert, Jack, Linda) and whether base-rate information was available.", "measured": "Judging occupation (librarian or farmer; engineer or lawyer) or choosing between bank teller and feminist bank teller.", "found": "Many call Robert a librarian despite about 2.2 million farms versus 120,000 libraries. People use the 70/30 base rate alone but ignore it once Jack is described. About 82% choose the conjunction for Linda.", "means": "A fitting description can override base rates and the conjunction rule.", "trap": "Feminist bank teller is a subset of bank teller, so it cannot be more probable."}, {"module": 12, "title": "Hospital samples · extreme proportions and sample size", "pages": [12, 13, 14], "researcher": "", "aim": "Do people consider sample size when judging how often extreme proportions occur?", "manipulated": "Hospital size: about 45 births per day versus about 15 births per day.", "measured": "Choosing which hospital has more days with over 60% boys (larger, smaller, or about the same).", "found": "56% chose about the same. The correct answer is the smaller hospital, because small samples vary more.", "means": "Small samples are more variable, which is why the law of large numbers matters.", "trap": "The question is about days over 60%, not total boys born."}, {"module": 12, "title": "Lord and colleagues · myside bias in article evaluation", "pages": [16], "researcher": "Lord and colleagues (1979)", "aim": "Does an existing opinion change how the same evidence is evaluated?", "manipulated": "Participants' opinion on capital punishment: in favor versus opposed.", "measured": "How each participant interpreted the same article.", "found": "Each side saw the article as favorable to its own position.", "means": "Myside bias is evaluating evidence in a way that favors one's own opinions and attitudes.", "trap": "Both groups read the same article. The difference is in the readers."}, {"module": 12, "title": "Hidden number rule · confirmation and diagnostic tests", "pages": [17], "researcher": "", "aim": "Do people look for evidence that could prove their hypothesis wrong?", "manipulated": "Nothing varied between groups. Participants wrote their own number triples starting from 2, 4, 6.", "measured": "Their triples and the rule they proposed, with feedback on whether each fit the experimenter's rule.", "found": "The most common hypothesis was increasing intervals of two. The actual rule was three numbers in increasing order.", "means": "Testing only triples that fit the hypothesis keeps a too-narrow belief alive. Testing for contradictions is more useful.", "trap": "Repeated 'yes' feedback does not separate the narrow rule from the broader one."}, {"module": 12, "title": "Jellybeans · favorable counts versus winning probability", "pages": [20], "researcher": "", "aim": "Do people choose by the number of winning items or by the probability of winning?", "manipulated": "Two bowls: 1 red bean out of 10 versus 7 red beans out of 100.", "measured": "Which bowl participants chose before drawing.", "found": "Most chose the 7-out-of-100 bowl, though 10% beats 7%.", "means": "A larger count of favorable items draws attention away from the total number of draws.", "trap": "Compare ratios, not counts."}, {"module": 12, "title": "Ritov and Baron · convoy rerouting and lives saved", "pages": [22], "researcher": "Ritov and Baron (1999)", "aim": "Do people choose the option that saves the most lives when it requires causing harm?", "manipulated": "Rerouting a food convoy (saves 1,000 at the second camp, 100 die at the first) versus keeping the route.", "measured": "Whether participants chose to reroute.", "found": "63% did not choose to reroute. Some would keep the route even if only one person would die after the change.", "means": "Reluctance to cause harm can outweigh a larger benefit, so choices are not just about the biggest number.", "trap": "Net lives saved (900) favors rerouting, yet most refused."}, {"module": 12, "title": "Tetlock and colleagues · taboo and tragic outcomes", "pages": [23, 24, 25], "researcher": "Tetlock, Kristel, Elson, Green, and Lerner (2000)", "aim": "How do taboo and tragic tradeoffs affect moral outrage?", "manipulated": "Tradeoff type (taboo: Johnny's life versus hospital money; tragic: two children, one liver) and outcome (Johnny saved or not saved).", "measured": "Ratings of moral outrage.", "found": "In the taboo tradeoff, not saving Johnny produced much greater outrage than saving him. In the tragic tradeoff, outrage was similar whether he was saved or not.", "means": "The effect of the decision depends on the type of value conflict (an interaction).", "trap": "A tragic tradeoff compares two sacred values. A taboo tradeoff puts a sacred value against a secular benefit."}, {"module": 12, "title": "Ginges and colleagues · payment, recognition, and sacred commitments", "pages": [26, 27, 28, 29], "researcher": "Ginges, Atran, Medin, and Shikaki (2007)", "aim": "How do people react to compromise deals over contested issues, and does money help?", "manipulated": "Deal type: taboo, Taboo+ (one billion dollars a year for ten years), and symbolic (recognition and an apology). Participants were also classified as moral absolutists or not.", "measured": "Anger, disgust, and support for violence.", "found": "For moral absolutists, adding money increased anger, disgust, and support for violence. Non-absolutists responded the other way. Symbolic recognition lowered anger or disgust and violence support.", "means": "Money can backfire when the issue is sacred, and symbolic reciprocity can help.", "trap": "Samples were 601 settlers, 535 refugees, and 719 students."}, {"module": 12, "title": "Iranian online study · sacred values, funds, sanctions, and energy results", "pages": [30, 31, 32, 33], "researcher": "Dehghani, Atran, and colleagues (2010); Dehghani, Iliev, and colleagues (2009)", "aim": "Do incentives or sanctions make Iranians who hold a sacred value more willing to give up the nuclear program?", "manipulated": "A 2 x 3 design: program type (energy or weapons) by deal type (Taboo, Taboo+ with 40 billion dollars, Taboo- with the threat of sanctions).", "measured": "Personal approval, anticipated public anger if the government approved, and whether the deal seems misleading.", "found": "For sacred-value participants in the energy condition, approval was lower with funds or sanctions than with the basic deal, and anger was highest with funds. Participants without a sacred value rated approval higher and anger lower.", "means": "For a sacred commitment, improving the material balance does not make a deal more acceptable.", "trap": "Sacred means refusing the exchange no matter how great the consequences. About 14% (energy) and 13% (weapons) did so."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Kermer and colleagues · expected and actual emotions after a coin flip", "pages": [4], "researcher": "Kermer and colleagues (2006)", "aim": "Do people predict their emotions after a win or a loss accurately?", "manipulated": "Outcome of a coin flip: win an extra $5 or lose $3 (after being given $5). Predicted feelings (before the flip) were compared with actual feelings (after).", "measured": "Happiness ratings: a baseline rating before the experiment, then predicted and actual changes from baseline.", "found": "Before the flip, people expected losing $3 to drop happiness about 4 points and winning $5 to raise it about 2 points. After the flip, the real drop was about 1 point and the real rise about 1.2 points.", "means": "People overestimate how bad a loss will feel, because they ignore their coping mechanisms; this feeds risk aversion.", "trap": "The loss was the smaller amount of money ($3 vs $5) but was expected to hurt about twice as much as the gain helped."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Simonsohn · cloudy days, admissions, and enrollment", "pages": [5], "researcher": "Simonsohn (2007); Simonsohn (2009)", "aim": "Does the weather, an incidental factor, carry into important decisions?", "manipulated": "Weather: cloudy vs sunny days (on the day of the admissions decision, or the day of the campus visit).", "measured": "Admissions decisions (what was weighted) and whether visiting students enrolled.", "found": "Simonsohn (2007): applicants' academic attributes were weighted more heavily on cloudy days than sunny days. Simonsohn (2009): visitors to a highly rated university were more likely to enroll if they visited on a cloudy day.", "means": "Weather is an incidental factor that can change important decisions even though it is unrelated to the applicant or the school.", "trap": "Do not mix up the two years: 2007 is admissions decisions, 2009 is enrollment by visitors."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Lerner and colleagues · mood and highlighter prices", "pages": [6], "researcher": "Lerner and colleagues (2004)", "aim": "Can a mood unrelated to the decision change how much an object seems worth?", "manipulated": "Film clip watched first: sad (a person dying), disgust (a person using a dirty toilet), or neutral (fish at the Great Barrier Reef).", "measured": "Selling price and buying price for a set of highlighters.", "found": "Selling: neutral about $4.50, disgust about $2.70, sad about $3.00. Buying: neutral about $3.50, disgust about $3.00, sad about $4.50.", "means": "Incidental mood influences decisions: disgust lowered both prices, and sadness lowered selling but raised buying.", "trap": "Sad lowers the selling price but RAISES the buying price; disgust lowers both."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Physicians · earlier cases and cesarean recommendations", "pages": [7], "researcher": "", "aim": "Does the context of earlier cases change a decision about the same case?", "manipulated": "What came before the same test case: nothing (control), four serious-complication cases, or four fairly routine (not serious) cases.", "measured": "Whether physicians recommended a cesarean section for the test case.", "found": "Significantly more doctors recommended cesarean after reading the non-serious cases first.", "means": "Context, including recently considered cases, changes how the same patient is judged.", "trap": "The test case never changed; only the cases before it did."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Organ donation · opt-in and opt-out consent", "pages": [8], "researcher": "", "aim": "Does the way the choice is presented change how many people become organ donors?", "manipulated": "Opt-in procedure (must take a step to be a donor) vs opt-out procedure (a donor unless you ask not to be).", "measured": "Consent rate in countries using each procedure.", "found": "Opt-in countries have under 28% consent; opt-out countries have over 99% consent.", "means": "The status quo bias, the tendency to do nothing when a decision is difficult, makes the default decide the result.", "trap": "Doing nothing means non-donor under opt-in but donor under opt-out."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Tversky and Kahneman · the disease problem in gain and loss frames", "pages": [9, 11, 12], "researcher": "Tversky and Kahneman (1981)", "aim": "Does describing the same outcomes as gains or as losses change risk-taking?", "manipulated": "Frame: gain frame (A: 200 saved for certain; B: 1/3 chance all 600 saved, 2/3 chance none) vs loss frame (C: 400 die for certain; D: 1/3 chance no one dies, 2/3 chance all 600 die).", "measured": "Which program people chose.", "found": "Gain frame: 72% chose A (sure), 28% chose B. Loss frame: 78% chose D (gamble), 22% chose C.", "means": "The framing effect: gains push people to risk aversion (sure thing), losses push them to risk taking (gamble), though A=C and B=D.", "trap": "A and C are the same outcome, and B and D are the same gamble; only the wording changed."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Sanfey and colleagues · the ultimatum game and the anterior insula", "pages": [14, 15], "researcher": "Sanfey and colleagues (2003)", "aim": "Do emotion and brain activity explain why people reject unfair offers that cost them money?", "manipulated": "Offer size ($5/$5, $7/$3, $8/$2, $9/$1) and partner type (human vs computer); each participant played 20 games, 10 with each partner type.", "measured": "Whether the responder accepted or rejected, plus activation of the right anterior insula.", "found": "$5/$5 and $7/$3 were accepted almost always. $8/$2 was accepted about 53% (human) vs 85% (computer); $9/$1 about 37% (human) vs 68% (computer). More right anterior insula activation meant rejecting more offers.", "means": "Emotion (anger at unfairness) is important in decision making; utility theory says accept any offer, but people do not.", "trap": "Utility theory says accept any offer because any money beats nothing; rejecting costs both players."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Belief bias · judging invalid believable conclusions", "pages": [18], "researcher": "", "aim": "Does a believable conclusion make people accept a syllogism as valid?", "manipulated": "Invalid syllogisms with believable conclusions.", "measured": "Whether participants judged the syllogism valid.", "found": "71% judged invalid syllogisms with believable conclusions as valid.", "means": "Belief bias: people judge by whether the conclusion sounds true instead of whether it follows.", "trap": "Example: All of the students are tired / Some tired people are irritable / Some of the students are irritable sounds fine but is not valid."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Conditional syllogisms · four forms and accuracy", "pages": [22, 23, 24, 25], "researcher": "", "aim": "Which kinds of if-then syllogisms do people judge correctly?", "manipulated": "The four forms, all with If p, then q: p (modus ponens), not q (modus tollens), q, and not p.", "measured": "Whether participants judged each form valid or invalid correctly.", "found": "Modus ponens (valid) 97% correct; modus tollens (valid) 60%; syllogism 3 (invalid) 40%; syllogism 4 (invalid) 40%.", "means": "Both valid forms were judged correctly more often than both invalid ones, but modus tollens is much harder than modus ponens even though both are valid.", "trap": "Modus tollens is valid; syllogisms 3 and 4 are not."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Wason four-card problem · abstract and concrete versions", "pages": [26, 27, 28], "researcher": "", "aim": "Does using real-world items make conditional reasoning easier?", "manipulated": "Abstract version (cards E, K, 4, 7; rule: vowel then even number) vs concrete version (cards Beer, Soda, 16 years old, 21 years old; rule: drinking beer then 21 years old).", "measured": "Which cards participants chose to turn over.", "found": "Abstract: 46% chose 4 (error), only 7% chose 7 (correct). Concrete: 73% answered correctly.", "means": "Following the falsification principle, look for cases that break the rule; permission schemas in familiar rules make this easier.", "trap": "Correct cards are E and 7 (abstract) and beer and 16 years old (concrete); the 4 and the 21 cannot break the rule."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Phonological Similarity Effect", "pages": [1], "researcher": "Conrad (1964); Murray (1968)", "aim": "Show that people recode silently read items into a speech-based form.", "manipulated": "Lists of letters that sound alike versus sound different. On some trials people also said 'one, two, three, four' out loud (articulatory suppression) and on others stayed quiet.", "measured": "How well people clicked the letters back in the order shown.", "found": "Sound-alike lists were recalled worse, even when read silently. With suppression on visual lists, the cost disappeared.", "means": "People turn what they see into a sound code, which supports the phonological loop.", "trap": "It is about similar sound, not similar look (E and F would be a visual mix-up)."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Neighborhood Size Effect", "pages": [2], "researcher": "Roodenrys et al. (2002)", "aim": "Test whether long-term knowledge of words affects short-term recall.", "manipulated": "Words from large neighborhoods (like cat) versus small neighborhoods (like hippopotamus).", "measured": "How well people recalled the words in order.", "found": "Large neighborhood words were recalled better in short-term memory tasks.", "means": "What you know in long-term memory helps short-term recall, so memory span is not a pure measure of short-term capacity.", "trap": "A neighbor differs by one letter or one sound; do not confuse it with a word that merely means the same thing."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Brown-Peterson Task", "pages": [3], "researcher": "Peterson and Peterson (1959)", "aim": "Find out what happens to short-term memory when rehearsal is blocked.", "manipulated": "How long people did a distractor task (counting backwards) before recall.", "measured": "How well people recalled the consonants.", "found": "The longer the distraction, the worse the recall.", "means": "The researchers concluded short-term memory loses information through decay unless it is rehearsed.", "trap": "Decay is the researchers' conclusion, and interference (Keppel and Underwood, 1962) was cited as an alternative."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Modality Effect", "pages": [4], "researcher": "", "aim": "Test whether hearing a list gives better memory than reading it.", "manipulated": "Lists that were heard versus read silently (digits in random order).", "measured": "Memory for the last one or two items.", "found": "Heard lists gave better memory for the last items. Lip-read or mouthed lists show it too.", "means": "Hearing adds information about how the list sounded (Neath and Surprenant, 2003), and the effect shaped ideas about sensory memory.", "trap": "The effect is for the last items of the list, not the whole list."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Partial Report", "pages": [5], "researcher": "Sperling (1960)", "aim": "Test whether the small amount people report from a brief display is a recall limit or a perception limit.", "manipulated": "Whether a cue telling which part to report came right away or after a delay.", "measured": "How much of the cued part people reported.", "found": "With an immediate cue, people reported the cued part almost perfectly. With a delayed cue, performance fell toward the usual limit.", "means": "A sensory (iconic) store briefly held nearly all the display, and it fades very fast. The limit is reporting time, not perception.", "trap": "The limit is in recall, not in what people saw."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Position Error Effect", "pages": [6], "researcher": "", "aim": "Show how order errors in recall are patterned.", "manipulated": "Nothing was compared across groups. Each recalled letter's placed position was compared with its true position.", "measured": "Where each recalled item was placed in the list.", "found": "Items were usually placed correctly. Errors were systematic: the item landed in an adjacent position.", "means": "People keep approximate position information, and percent correct alone hides this.", "trap": "Order errors are not random."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Mental Rotation", "pages": [7], "researcher": "Shepard and Metzler (1971)", "aim": "Test whether mental images behave like real images.", "manipulated": "How much one block shape was turned relative to the other (same shape or mirror image).", "measured": "How long people took to judge same or mirror image.", "found": "The larger the turn, the longer the response, at a constant rate.", "means": "People mentally rotate images, so mental images are much like real images.", "trap": "Self-reports of imagery are unreliable, so researchers use tasks that require imagery."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Typical Reasoning", "pages": [8], "researcher": "Tversky and Kahneman (1983); Shafir, Smith, and Osherson (1990)", "aim": "Show that people judge probability with heuristics.", "manipulated": "People rated how likely descriptions of a person were to fit single professions or activities versus combinations.", "measured": "Ratings of how likely each statement was.", "found": "People sometimes rated a combination as more likely than one of its parts (conjunction fallacy).", "means": "People use heuristics, not the rules of probability.", "trap": "A combination can never be more likely than either part alone."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Wason Selection", "pages": [9], "researcher": "Wason and Shapiro (1971)", "aim": "Compare how well people test abstract rules and familiar thematic rules.", "manipulated": "Abstract rules (letters and numbers) versus thematic rules (like borrowing a car).", "measured": "Which cards people chose to turn over.", "found": "Thematic rules were much easier. On the abstract rule most people chose D and 3 instead of D and 7.", "means": "With relevant experience people test rules well; with abstract rules they must use logic alone.", "trap": "The 3 can only confirm the rule; the 7 could falsify it."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Decision Making", "pages": [10], "researcher": "Kahneman and Tversky (1982); Tversky and Kahneman", "aim": "Show that wording or context can change choices between equivalent options.", "manipulated": "The same choice framed with gain wording versus loss wording.", "measured": "Which option people chose.", "found": "Gain wording led to the sure option and loss wording to the gamble.", "means": "Choices can follow the framing and not the substance.", "trap": "The situations are otherwise equivalent."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Monty Hall", "pages": [11], "researcher": "", "aim": "Show poor intuition about the probability of combined events.", "manipulated": "Whether people stayed with their first door or switched after the host opened a no-prize door.", "measured": "How often staying and switching won.", "found": "Switching wins about two-thirds of the time and staying about one-third. Many people think it makes no difference.", "means": "The first pick stays at 1 in 3, so the other 2 in 3 moves to the remaining door.", "trap": "The two remaining doors are not equally likely."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Risky Decisions", "pages": [12], "researcher": "Kahneman and Tversky", "aim": "Show that risky choices depend on whether outcomes are seen as gains or losses.", "manipulated": "Whether the sure outcome was a gain or a loss, with a gamble as the alternative.", "measured": "Whether people took the sure thing or the gamble.", "found": "People avoided risk with gains (sure thing) and sought risk with losses (gamble).", "means": "Risk decisions depend on how outcomes are perceived, not only on objective odds.", "trap": "Losses lead to gambling, not caution."}, {"module": 14, "title": "German & Defeyter (2000): Immunity to functional fixedness in young children", "pages": [13], "researcher": "German and Defeyter (2000)", "aim": "Test whether young children get stuck on an object's usual function (functional fixedness).", "manipulated": "Whether the box had first been shown as a container (preutilization) or not, in 5-, 6- and 7-year-olds.", "measured": "How long children took to offer the box as a support.", "found": "Children aged 6 and 7 were slower after seeing the box as a container. 5-year-olds were not slowed and were even faster than the older children after seeing the box as a container.", "means": "Younger children have a more fluid idea of function, so one shown use did not lock the object in.", "trap": "Younger children did better here, not worse."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Leist et al. (2022): Irrelevant speech impairs serial recall of verbal but not spatial items", "pages": [14], "researcher": "Leist et al. (2022)", "aim": "Find out whether ignored speech hurts only verbal items or spatial items too.", "manipulated": "Verbal versus spatial items; quiet, steady-state or changing-state speech to ignore; third graders versus adults.", "measured": "Serial recall of the order of the items.", "found": "Speech hurt verbal recall but not spatial recall, in children and adults.", "means": "The effect is specific to verbal material, which fits phonological interference better than attention capture or changing-state accounts.", "trap": "Changing-state speech hurts more than steady-state, but only for verbal items."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Morrison & Richmond (2020): Offloading items from memory", "pages": [15], "researcher": "Morrison and Richmond (2020)", "aim": "Find out who offloads items from memory and whether it helps.", "manipulated": "A no-choice block (memory only) versus a choice block where pen and paper were allowed, with lists of different lengths.", "measured": "Recall and how much people wrote things down.", "found": "Offloading improved memory, especially at high loads, and people offloaded more as load grew.", "means": "Memory ability did not predict who offloaded, so offloading may be a compensatory strategy across abilities.", "trap": "Low-capacity people were expected to offload more, but capacity did not predict it."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Hoover & Healy (2019): The Bat-and-Ball Problem", "pages": [16], "researcher": "Hoover and Healy (2019)", "aim": "Test whether the bat-and-ball error comes from unconsciously ignoring 'more than'.", "manipulated": "A standard problem with 'more than' versus a control version without it.", "measured": "Confidence, and later recall and recognition of the problem wording.", "found": "Wrong reasoners mostly remembered 'more than', so it was not simply overlooked.", "means": "Substitution cannot be confirmed as the cause, so the authors prefer the term error sensitivity.", "trap": "The intuitive 10 cents is wrong; the answer is 5 cents."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Giuliani et al. (2023): The joint effect of framing and defaults on choice behavior", "pages": [17], "researcher": "Giuliani et al. (2023)", "aim": "Test the framing effect and the default effect together.", "manipulated": "Gain versus loss wording, and no default, a sure default or a risky default, in life-or-death and money scenarios.", "measured": "Whether people chose the sure or the risky option.", "found": "A risky default raised risk taking, a sure default did not raise risk aversion, and framing and default effects were additive.", "means": "People who thought the default was most rational followed it most, though that question came after the choice.", "trap": "The belief question was exploratory and may be a justification."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Chein et al. (2011) and Albert, Chein & Steinberg (2013): Peers and the adolescent brain", "pages": [18], "researcher": "Chein et al. (2011); Albert, Chein and Steinberg (2013)", "aim": "Explain why adolescents take more risks with peers around.", "manipulated": "Playing a driving game alone versus watched by friends, in adolescents, young adults and adults.", "measured": "Risky driving choices and brain activity in reward and cognitive-control regions.", "found": "Adolescents took more risks when watched, with greater reward-region activity. Cognitive-control regions did not change with peers.", "means": "The peer effect works mainly through reward circuitry, not weaker reasoning.", "trap": "Adolescents reason about risk like adults in the lab; the peer context is what changes."}], "contrasts": [{"module": 6, "title": "Whole report vs partial report", "a": {"term": "Whole report", "definition": "The person names as many letters as possible from the flashed array."}, "b": {"term": "Partial report", "definition": "A tone cues one row to report, so only that row is named."}, "tell": "Whole report gave about a third of the letters, partial report gave most of the cued row. The gap shows that more was stored than people could report.", "example": "Sperling's three rows of four letters: the top row was cued by a tone."}, {"module": 6, "title": "Maintenance rehearsal vs elaborative rehearsal", "a": {"term": "Maintenance rehearsal", "definition": "Repeating information silently so it stays in short-term memory longer."}, "b": {"term": "Elaborative rehearsal", "definition": "Linking new information to knowledge already in long-term memory."}, "tell": "Maintenance only keeps information alive while the repeating continues. Elaborative adds meaning and helps move information into long-term memory.", "example": "Saying a phone number over and over is maintenance; connecting it to something you already know is elaborative."}, {"module": 6, "title": "Retroactive vs proactive interference", "a": {"term": "Retroactive interference", "definition": "A new memory interferes with remembering old information."}, "b": {"term": "Proactive interference", "definition": "An old memory interferes with remembering new information."}, "tell": "Ask which memory is doing the interfering. New hurting old is retroactive; old hurting new is proactive.", "example": "A new phone number crowding out the old one is retroactive; earlier trigrams harming the current one in Brown-Peterson is proactive."}, {"module": 6, "title": "Decay vs interference", "a": {"term": "Decay", "definition": "A memory trace vanishes simply because time passes."}, "b": {"term": "Interference", "definition": "Forgetting caused by one memory competing with or replacing another."}, "tell": "Decay depends on time alone; interference depends on what else is in memory. Brown and Peterson and Peterson meant to test decay but found interference as well.", "example": "Recall after the long delay was fine on the first trial but near zero many trials later."}, {"module": 6, "title": "Icon vs echo", "a": {"term": "Icon", "definition": "A fleeting mental image; the visual form of sensory memory."}, "b": {"term": "Echo", "definition": "The brief continuation of a sound in the auditory system."}, "tell": "Icon is for seeing and echo is for hearing; both are sensory memory lasting under a second.", "example": "Sperling studied iconic memory; replaying the last few words someone said is an echo."}, {"module": 6, "title": "Episodic vs semantic memory", "a": {"term": "Episodic memory", "definition": "Memory for personal events we lived through."}, "b": {"term": "Semantic memory", "definition": "Memory for facts and general knowledge."}, "tell": "Episodic ties to a particular time and place; semantic is general knowledge.", "example": "Remembering one particular birthday is episodic; knowing what a birthday is is semantic."}, {"module": 9, "title": "Phonological loop vs visuospatial sketchpad", "a": {"term": "Phonological loop", "definition": "Maintains verbal and auditory information, with a phonological store and articulatory rehearsal."}, "b": {"term": "Visuospatial sketchpad", "definition": "Holds visual and spatial information such as appearance and arrangement."}, "tell": "Ask whether the content is sounds and words or shapes and locations. Brooks's reversed results show they are partly separate.", "example": "A remembered sentence uses the loop; the remembered F uses the sketchpad."}, {"module": 9, "title": "Word-length effect vs phonological similarity effect", "a": {"term": "Word-length effect", "definition": "Lists of short words are remembered better than lists of long words."}, "b": {"term": "Phonological similarity effect", "definition": "Items that sound alike are confused."}, "tell": "Word length is about rehearsal time; similarity is about overlapping sounds. Articulatory suppression reduces both.", "example": "Welsh digits take longer to say, so span is shorter; E is confused with B because they sound alike."}, {"module": 10, "title": "Functional fixedness vs mental set", "a": {"term": "Functional fixedness", "definition": "Restricting an object to its familiar functions."}, "b": {"term": "Mental set", "definition": "A preconceived notion about how to approach a problem, based on past experience with that problem or similar problems."}, "tell": "Fixedness is about an object's use; a set is about a method of solving.", "example": "Seeing the matchbox only as a container is fixedness; repeating B − A − 2C in the water-jug problems is a set."}, {"module": 10, "title": "Insight vs noninsight problems", "a": {"term": "Insight problem", "definition": "Solved by a sudden realization after restructuring, as in the triangle and chain problems."}, "b": {"term": "Noninsight problem", "definition": "Solved by successive operations that bring the solver closer, as in algebra."}, "tell": "Look at warmth: it rises gradually for noninsight problems but jumps late, from about 3 to 7, for insight problems.", "example": "Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987) compared the triangle and chain problems with algebra."}, {"module": 12, "title": "Availability heuristic vs representativeness heuristic", "a": {"term": "Availability heuristic", "definition": "Judging how likely something is by how easily examples come to mind."}, "b": {"term": "Representativeness heuristic", "definition": "Judging whether A belongs to class B by how much A resembles B."}, "tell": "Ask what supplies the shortcut. Remembered examples means availability. Similarity to a type means representativeness.", "example": "Botulism in the news (availability) versus Robert resembling a librarian (representativeness)."}, {"module": 11, "title": "Surface features vs structural features", "a": {"term": "Surface features", "definition": "The specific elements of a problem, such as a fortress, a tumor, or a ray."}, "b": {"term": "Structural features", "definition": "The underlying principle that governs the solution."}, "tell": "Surface is what the objects are. Structure is why a solution works.", "example": "Lightbulb and laser are surface. A strong beam harming the surroundings is structural."}, {"module": 11, "title": "Noticing vs mapping vs applying an analogy", "a": {"term": "Noticing", "definition": "Recognizing that a source problem may be relevant to the target."}, "b": {"term": "Mapping and applying", "definition": "Linking elements of the source to the target, then using that link to build a solution."}, "tell": "Noticing is finding the source. Mapping is lining up the parts. Applying is using them.", "example": "About 30% solved radiation after the fortress story, and about 75% when told to think about it."}, {"module": 11, "title": "Analogical source vs target", "a": {"term": "Source problem", "definition": "A problem with relevant similarities whose solution guides a new problem."}, "b": {"term": "Target problem", "definition": "The problem currently being solved."}, "tell": "The target is what you need to solve now. The source is what you already know.", "example": "Russian marriage is the source and the mutilated checkerboard is the target."}, {"module": 12, "title": "Base rate vs description information", "a": {"term": "Base rate", "definition": "The background prevalence of categories, such as 70 lawyers and 30 engineers."}, "b": {"term": "Description information", "definition": "Personal characteristics, such as Jack's hobbies, that resemble a category."}, "tell": "Base rate says how common the category is. The description says how well the person fits it.", "example": "People use 70/30 alone but call Jack an engineer once he is described."}, {"module": 12, "title": "Conjunction rule vs representativeness", "a": {"term": "Conjunction rule", "definition": "The probability of two events together cannot exceed either one alone."}, "b": {"term": "Representativeness", "definition": "Seeing a more detailed, better-fitting story as more probable."}, "tell": "The rule is about nested sets. Representativeness is about fit.", "example": "About 82% chose feminist bank teller over bank teller for Linda."}, {"module": 12, "title": "Confirmation bias vs myside bias", "a": {"term": "Confirmation bias", "definition": "Seeking information that fits a hypothesis and overlooking what argues against it."}, "b": {"term": "Myside bias", "definition": "Generating and evaluating evidence, and testing hypotheses, in ways biased toward one's own opinions and attitudes."}, "tell": "Myside bias is one form of confirmation bias, tied to one's own opinions and attitudes.", "example": "2, 4, 6 task (confirmation) versus capital punishment article (myside)."}, {"module": 12, "title": "Taboo tradeoff vs tragic tradeoff", "a": {"term": "Taboo tradeoff", "definition": "Mixes sacred and secular domains, such as a child's life against money."}, "b": {"term": "Tragic tradeoff", "definition": "Compares two sacred values with each other."}, "tell": "Ask whether the competing item is a secular benefit (taboo) or another sacred value (tragic).", "example": "Johnny's liver costing one million dollars versus two children and one liver."}, {"module": 12, "title": "Moral absolutists vs non-absolutists", "a": {"term": "Moral absolutists", "definition": "Treat the issue as a sacred value and reject compromise regardless of benefit."}, "b": {"term": "Non-absolutists", "definition": "Have strong preferences against compromise but do not treat the issue as sacred."}, "tell": "Ask whether benefits can enter the decision at all, not how much the person cares.", "example": "Adding money to Taboo+ raised anger for absolutists and lowered it for non-absolutists."}, {"module": 12, "title": "Material incentive vs symbolic recognition", "a": {"term": "Material incentive (Taboo+)", "definition": "Money added to the deal, such as one billion dollars a year for ten years."}, "b": {"term": "Symbolic recognition", "definition": "Recognition of the other group's right and an apology, with no added cash."}, "tell": "Money changes the economic benefit. Symbolic terms address the status of a group's claim.", "example": "Taboo+ raised absolutists' anger, while the symbolic offer lowered anger and support for violence."}, {"module": 12, "title": "Incentive (Taboo+) vs disincentive (Taboo-)", "a": {"term": "Incentive", "definition": "A benefit offered for agreeing, such as 40 billion dollars in funds."}, "b": {"term": "Disincentive", "definition": "A cost imposed for refusing, such as additional sanctions."}, "tell": "Incentives change the benefit of accepting. Disincentives change the cost of refusing.", "example": "Iran study: Taboo+ gave funds and Taboo- threatened sanctions."}, {"module": 11, "title": "Expert vs novice problem organization", "a": {"term": "Experts", "definition": "Group problems by structural features, such as conservation of energy."}, "b": {"term": "Novices", "definition": "Group problems by surface features, such as inclined planes."}, "tell": "Novices go by what the problem looks like. Experts go by the principle that solves it.", "example": "Chi et al. (1982): problems 23 and 24 (novice) versus 21 and 24 (expert)."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Valid vs true", "a": {"term": "Valid", "definition": "The conclusion follows logically from the two premises; depends on the form of the reasoning."}, "b": {"term": "True", "definition": "The statements match the world."}, "tell": "Ask two separate questions: does it follow? and are the statements correct? A valid syllogism with a false premise can have a false conclusion.", "example": "All birds are animals / All animals have four legs / All birds have four legs is valid but its conclusion is false because premise 2 is false."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Modus ponens vs modus tollens", "a": {"term": "Modus ponens", "definition": "If p, then q; p; therefore q. Valid; 97% correct."}, "b": {"term": "Modus tollens", "definition": "If p, then q; not q; therefore not p. Valid; 60% correct."}, "tell": "Ponens affirms p and gets q. Tollens denies q and gets not p. Both are valid; tollens just feels harder.", "example": "If I study, I'll get a good grade; I didn't get a good grade; therefore I didn't study."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Modus tollens vs syllogism 3 (q, therefore p)", "a": {"term": "Modus tollens", "definition": "Second premise is not q; conclusion not p. Valid."}, "b": {"term": "Syllogism 3", "definition": "Second premise is q; conclusion p. Not valid."}, "tell": "Valid forms move from p to q or from not q to not p. Going from q back to p is not valid because there may be other ways to get q.", "example": "If I live in Tucson, then I live in Arizona; I live in Arizona; therefore I live in Tucson does not follow."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Syllogism 3 vs syllogism 4", "a": {"term": "Syllogism 3", "definition": "If p then q; q; therefore p. Not valid; 40% correct."}, "b": {"term": "Syllogism 4", "definition": "If p then q; not p; therefore not q. Not valid; 40% correct."}, "tell": "Both are invalid. In 3 the second premise is q; in 4 it is not p.", "example": "Syllogism 4: If I study, I'll get a good grade; I didn't study; therefore I didn't get a good grade (a good grade could come another way)."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Deductive reasoning vs belief bias", "a": {"term": "Deductive reasoning", "definition": "Determining whether a conclusion logically follows from premises."}, "b": {"term": "Belief bias", "definition": "Thinking a syllogism is valid because its conclusion is believable."}, "tell": "Deduction asks if the conclusion follows. Belief bias answers by asking if the conclusion sounds true.", "example": "71% judged invalid syllogisms with believable conclusions as valid."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Abstract vs concrete Wason problem", "a": {"term": "Abstract version", "definition": "Cards E, K, 4, 7 with the rule vowel then even number; correct cards E and 7."}, "b": {"term": "Concrete version", "definition": "Cards Beer, Soda, 16 years old, 21 years old with the rule drinking beer then 21 years old; correct cards beer and 16."}, "tell": "Same if p, then q form. Concrete, familiar content calls up a permission schema and correct answers jump (73% answered the beer version correctly; only 7% chose the 7 in the abstract version).", "example": "7% chose the 7 in the abstract version; 73% answered the beer version correctly."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Mental models vs belief bias", "a": {"term": "Mental model approach", "definition": "Build a model of the premises, make a tentative conclusion, search for exceptions, then decide validity."}, "b": {"term": "Belief bias", "definition": "The tendency to think a syllogism is valid if its conclusion is believable."}, "tell": "The mental model approach decides validity by searching for an exception; belief bias decides by whether the conclusion sounds believable.", "example": "A conclusion is valid only if no model of the premises refutes it."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Gain frame vs loss frame", "a": {"term": "Gain frame", "definition": "Outcomes stated as people saved; people prefer the sure option (risk aversion)."}, "b": {"term": "Loss frame", "definition": "Outcomes stated as people who die; people prefer the gamble (risk taking)."}, "tell": "Same facts, different wording. Gains lead to the sure thing, losses lead to the gamble.", "example": "72% chose sure Program A; 78% chose gamble Program D."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Opt-in vs opt-out", "a": {"term": "Opt-in", "definition": "You must take a step to be a donor; under 28% consent."}, "b": {"term": "Opt-out", "definition": "You are a donor unless you request not to be; over 99% consent."}, "tell": "Look at what doing nothing gives you. Status quo bias: many people do nothing.", "example": "Doing nothing leaves you a non-donor under opt-in and a donor under opt-out."}, {"module": 13, "title": "Expected emotions vs incidental emotions", "a": {"term": "Expected emotions", "definition": "Emotions people predict they will feel about an outcome."}, "b": {"term": "Incidental emotions", "definition": "Emotions not specifically related to the decision (disposition, recent experience, surroundings)."}, "tell": "Expected emotions are predictions about the outcome itself. Incidental emotions come from something unrelated.", "example": "Kermer: predicted feelings about a coin flip. Lerner: mood from a film clip."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Decay vs interference", "a": {"term": "Decay", "definition": "Information fades from short-term memory over time unless it is rehearsed."}, "b": {"term": "Interference", "definition": "Forgetting caused by other material getting in the way of the memory."}, "tell": "Decay depends on time passing; interference depends on competing material. Peterson and Peterson (1959) chose decay because counting backwards is so unlike remembering letters.", "example": "Recall got worse the longer the counting lasted, which they took as decay."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Phonological code vs visual code", "a": {"term": "Phonological code", "definition": "A speech-based form of the items."}, "b": {"term": "Visual code", "definition": "A memory of how items look."}, "tell": "With a sound code, sound-alike items get confused; with a visual code, look-alike items would. The sound-alike cost on silently read lists shows a sound code.", "example": "E and F look alike, but similar-sounding letters were the ones recalled worse."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Sensory store vs short-term store", "a": {"term": "Sensory (iconic) store", "definition": "A very brief back-up store of raw, unanalyzed input that held nearly all of a display."}, "b": {"term": "Short-term store", "definition": "Holds information briefly for use, and loses it unless rehearsed."}, "tell": "The sensory store fades very quickly, while rehearsal can keep short-term traces.", "example": "In Sperling's (1960) partial report, an immediate cue let people report the cued row almost perfectly."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Phonological similarity vs neighborhood size", "a": {"term": "Phonological similarity effect", "definition": "Sound-alike items are recalled worse."}, "b": {"term": "Neighborhood size effect", "definition": "Words with many neighbors are recalled better."}, "tell": "One shows sound similarity hurting recall; the other shows long-term word knowledge helping recall.", "example": "Lists of sound-alike letters do worse; cat is recalled better than hippopotamus."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Framing effect vs default effect", "a": {"term": "Framing effect", "definition": "Choice changes with the wording or context of equivalent options (gain wording gives the sure option, loss wording the gamble)."}, "b": {"term": "Default effect", "definition": "A tendency to choose the preselected option."}, "tell": "Framing is about how options are described; the default is about which option is already chosen. Giuliani et al. (2023) found the two effects were additive.", "example": "A risky default raised risk taking on top of the framing effect."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Substitution vs error sensitivity", "a": {"term": "Unconscious attribute substitution", "definition": "Answering an easier question because 'more than' is neglected."}, "b": {"term": "Error sensitivity", "definition": "The term Hoover and Healy (2019) prefer because substitution cannot be confirmed as the cause of the error."}, "tell": "Substitution claims a cause; error sensitivity only says people notice they may be wrong. Wrong reasoners mostly remembered 'more than'.", "example": "Hoover and Healy found 'more than' was mostly recalled, so it was not just overlooked."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Reward system vs cognitive control", "a": {"term": "Reward system", "definition": "Ventral striatum and orbitofrontal cortex, primed by peers."}, "b": {"term": "Cognitive-control system", "definition": "Lateral prefrontal cortex, which matures slowly."}, "tell": "In Chein et al. (2011) reward regions rose when friends watched; cognitive-control regions did not change.", "example": "Adolescents watched by friends took more risks, with more reward-region activity."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Offloading vs rehearsal", "a": {"term": "Offloading", "definition": "Using actions like writing things down to reduce mental demands."}, "b": {"term": "Rehearsal", "definition": "Repeating information mentally to keep it from being lost."}, "tell": "Offloading puts the load outside the head; rehearsal keeps it in the head. Offloading helped most at high loads (Morrison and Richmond, 2020).", "example": "With pen and paper allowed, memory improved when lists got long."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Verbal vs spatial items", "a": {"term": "Verbal items", "definition": "Word-like items, such as pictured objects."}, "b": {"term": "Spatial items", "definition": "Locations, such as dot positions."}, "tell": "Ignored speech hurt verbal recall only (Leist et al., 2022), which points to interference with phonological codes.", "example": "Third graders and adults were hurt on objects but not on dot locations."}, {"module": 14, "title": "Falsify vs confirm", "a": {"term": "Falsify", "definition": "Choose the cards that could show the rule is broken (D and 7)."}, "b": {"term": "Confirm", "definition": "Choose a card that can only agree with the rule (the 3)."}, "tell": "The 7 could break the rule; the 3 cannot, since the rule says nothing about what is behind a 3.", "example": "Most people add the 3 to D on the abstract rule; the correct pair is D and 7."}], "ask": [{"module": 6, "topic": "Memory systems and control processes", "questions": ["Which stage of the modal model holds about five to seven items for roughly fifteen to twenty seconds?", "What is the difference between maintenance rehearsal and elaborative rehearsal?", "Why is rehearsal called a control process?", "What is the role of attention between sensory memory and short-term memory?", "Which concept is this an example of: placing items along an imagined building to make them memorable?"], "remember": "The modal model runs sensory, then short-term, then long-term memory; attention gets items into short-term memory, and rehearsal and other control processes are chosen by the person.", "answers": ["That is short-term memory, the middle stage of the modal model proposed by Atkinson and Shiffrin (1968). Sensory memory comes first and holds everything for a fraction of a second, and long-term memory comes last and can hold a huge amount for years or even decades. Short-term memory is the small, active store, holding only five to seven items for about fifteen to twenty seconds unless you keep rehearsing them.", "Maintenance rehearsal is just repeating the information silently, like saying a phone number over and over, and it keeps the information alive in short-term memory only for as long as you keep repeating. Elaborative rehearsal links the new information to things you already know in long-term memory, like connecting a fact to an example or a personal experience. Those connections give it meaning and create several paths back to it, so it transfers into long-term memory much better.", "Rehearsal is a control process because it is something the person chooses to do with the contents of memory, rather than something the memory system does automatically. You decide whether to repeat a number, how much, and for how long, and that choice changes how much you keep. Repeating information keeps it active in short-term memory and also helps move it into long-term memory. Chunking and attention strategies are other examples of control processes.", "Attention is the gateway between the two stores. Sensory memory briefly takes in everything that reaches the senses, like a whole screen full of phone listings, but it fades almost at once. Attention picks out the one thing you need, like the single number, and only what attention selects passes into short-term memory. Because short-term memory is so small, this selection matters, and anything attention ignores is simply lost.", "That is a memory palace, an ancient strategy that works as a control process. You picture a familiar place, like a building with many rooms, and put each item to remember at a spot along a route through it. Those places are already stored in long-term memory, so they act as ready-made cues. Then you just walk through the building in your mind, and the items come back in order."]}, {"module": 6, "topic": "Sensory memory and Sperling", "questions": ["What did Sperling find about whole report compared with partial report?", "What was manipulated in Sperling's experiment?", "Why did delayed partial report produce only about one letter?", "What did people believe about iconic memory before Sperling, and why was it wrong?", "How does an icon differ from an echo?"], "remember": "Sensory memory has large capacity but fast decay; whole report underestimates it because the image fades while reporting.", "answers": ["Sperling (1960) found that whole report gave much less than partial report. In whole report, people named about 4.5 of the 12 letters, which is 37.5 percent. In partial report, where a tone told them which row to give, they named about 3.3 of the 4 letters in the cued row, or 82 percent, and they could do it for any row. So most of the letters were available at first, but the report was too slow to get them out.", "What Sperling (1960) manipulated was the report method, meaning what participants had to report and when they found out. In whole report they named as many letters as they could. In partial report a tone came after the display and told them which row to report, high for the top row, medium for the middle, low for the bottom. In delayed partial report, there was a short delay before that tone. The display was three rows of four letters.", "In delayed partial report the tone comes only after a short wait, and by then the icon has already faded. All the letters were registered when the display flashed, but visual sensory memory decays very fast, so only about one of the four letters in the cued row was left, around 25 percent. By about a second, the advantage of partial report was nearly gone, down to the whole report level.", "People believed visual sensory memory could hold only four or five items, because that is how many letters people managed in whole report. They took the report score as the size of the store, and that was wrong. Sperling (1960) showed with partial report that nearly every row was available at first, so the whole scene was registered. The limit was in how much could be reported before the image faded, not in how much was stored.", "An icon is the visual form of sensory memory, a fleeting mental image of what you just saw. An echo is the auditory form, the brief continuation of a sound in the auditory system after you hear it. They work the same way, each holding raw input for less than a second so there is time to process it. The difference is just the sense involved: icon for seeing, echo for hearing."]}, {"module": 6, "topic": "Duration, forgetting, and interference", "questions": ["Why did Brown and Peterson and Peterson have participants count backward by threes?", "What happened to recall after a long delay on the first trial versus many trials later?", "Which concept is this an example of: earlier trigrams making new letters harder to recall?", "How does retroactive interference differ from proactive interference?", "Why does the average over all trials give a misleading impression?"], "remember": "Counting blocks rehearsal; the trial-by-trial pattern shows proactive interference, so forgetting is both decay and interference.", "answers": ["They had participants count backward by threes to block maintenance rehearsal. Participants saw three letters and a number, then counted backward from the number during a delay of 3 to 18 seconds before recalling the letters. Counting takes steady attention and keeps the inner voice busy, so the letters cannot be repeated or refreshed. Without it, people would just repeat the trigram and recall it perfectly, and the delay would tell you nothing about duration.", "On the first trial, recall stayed very high even after 18 seconds, only slightly below recall after 3 seconds. Many trials later, recall after 18 seconds had dropped to close to zero, while recall after 3 seconds was still high. The delay and the counting task were exactly the same each time. The difference is that many earlier trigrams had piled up in memory and were competing with the new one.", "That is proactive interference. Older information, the earlier trigrams, disrupts memory for the newer information, the current letters. The trigrams are all similar, so they are easy to confuse, and each new trial adds one more competitor. The longer the delay, the more the older trigrams win out. Brown (1958) and Peterson and Peterson (1959) were looking for decay, but this build-up across trials showed interference too.", "The difference is the direction of the disruption. In retroactive interference, a new memory interferes with remembering old information, like a new phone number crowding out your old one. In proactive interference, an old memory interferes with remembering new information, like last week's parking spots making it harder to find where you parked today. Retro means backward and pro means forward. In both cases the more similar the memories, the stronger the competition.", "The average over all trials makes it look like a steady loss with time, as though short-term memory simply decays. But the average mixes very different trials together. Early trials have high recall even at 18 seconds, and later trials have very low recall at 18 seconds. The steep drop comes mostly from the later trials, where proactive interference is strongest, so averaging hides interference behind something that looks like decay."]}, {"module": 6, "topic": "Capacity, visual memory, and chunking", "questions": ["What did Miller propose about short-term memory capacity?", "What did Alvarez and Cavanagh find as object complexity increased?", "What was manipulated in Chase and Simon's chess study, and what was found?", "What did Ericsson's trained student do to reach a digit span of around eighty?", "How does chunking increase capacity without enlarging the store?"], "remember": "Capacity is about units, not raw items: visual memory holds about three to four objects, fewer if complex, and chunking uses long-term knowledge to pack more into each slot.", "answers": ["Miller (1956) proposed that short-term memory can hold seven, plus or minus two, units. The key word is units, not raw items. A unit can be a digit, a word, a multi-digit number, or even a phrase, and each one counts as a single slot. The plus or minus two allows for differences between people and kinds of material. That became the classic estimate of short-term memory capacity.", "Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004) found that capacity depends on how complex the objects are. Visual memory held about 4.4 colored squares, but only about 2.8 Chinese characters, about 2.0 random polygons, and about 1.6 shaded cubes. So the more complex the object, the fewer you can hold. Each object still counts as one item, but more detail uses up more of the limited capacity, which supports capacity depending on the amount of information.", "Chase and Simon (1973) compared chess masters and beginners, and they also compared two kinds of boards: arrangements that could occur in a real game and random arrangements. With real positions, the master placed about three times as many pieces correctly as the beginner. With random boards, they did about the same and both recalled few. So the master's advantage comes from chunking with chess knowledge, not from a bigger short-term memory.", "Ericsson and colleagues (1980) trained a college student who was a runner, and his digit span rose to 79. He did it by chunking the digits into meaningful running times. His short-term memory still held only a few units, as everyone's does, but each unit was a race time packed with several digits. Normal span is near seven, so knowledge from long-term memory is what stretched it.", "A chunk is a meaningful unit that combines smaller pieces, and however many pieces it contains, it takes up a single slot. So the store itself stays the same size, but each slot carries more. What makes a group meaningful is knowledge in long-term memory, so the more someone knows, the larger the chunks they can form. That is why a skilled chess player holds familiar clusters of pieces as single chunks."]}, {"module": 9, "topic": "Working-memory model and component functions", "questions": ["What did Baddeley and Hitch (1974) propose?", "What does the central executive do, compared with the phonological loop?", "Why does mental multiplication of 43 times 6 need both storage and processing?", "What component did Baddeley add in 2000?", "How does the working-memory model differ from Atkinson and Shiffrin's short-term memory?"], "remember": "Working memory stores and manipulates information using a central executive, phonological loop, and visuospatial sketchpad, with the episodic buffer added later.", "answers": ["Baddeley and Hitch (1974) proposed working memory, which resembles short-term memory but covers much more. It is a limited-capacity system for the temporary storage and manipulation of information in complex tasks like comprehension, learning, and reasoning. The old view treated short-term memory as passive storage, a kind of waiting room. Working memory instead has parts that hold information while you work on it, with storage and manipulation sharing one limited capacity.", "The central executive stores nothing itself. It directs and maintains attention, coordinates the other components, pulls in knowledge from long-term memory, and suppresses irrelevant information. It also does things like calculations. The phonological loop, by contrast, is a store for verbal and auditory information, with a phonological store and an articulatory rehearsal process that silently repeats items like an inner voice so they do not fade.", "Multiplying 43 times 6 in your head means holding information while also working on it. You multiply 3 by 6 to get 18, hold the 8 in memory, and carry the 1 to the 4. Then you multiply 6 by 4 to get 24, add the carried 1 to get 25, and combine that with the held 8 to make 258. Holding the 8 is storage, and carrying and calculating are processing.", "Baddeley added the episodic buffer in 2000. It is a fourth component with a limited capacity of about four chunks, and it binds information from the phonological loop, the visuospatial sketchpad, and long-term memory into integrated episodes. It was needed because people can recall about 16 words of a meaningful sentence, and because some amnesic patients can recall a story they just heard. The original model had nowhere to keep those.", "In Atkinson and Shiffrin's model, short-term memory is a single component for all types of information, used mainly for holding things briefly. Working memory is built from several cooperating parts: a central executive, a phonological loop, and a visuospatial sketchpad, with the episodic buffer added later. It is for manipulating information during reasoning, comprehension, and learning. With separate stores, a verbal task and a visual task can run together with little interference."]}, {"module": 9, "topic": "Verbal maintenance, recoding, and interference", "questions": ["What did Conrad (1964) find about errors with seen letters?", "What was manipulated in Conrad's study?", "What is the word-length effect, and who found it?", "Why did Welsh-English bilingual children have a shorter digit span in Welsh (Ellis and Hennelly, 1980)?", "What does articulatory suppression do to the word-length and phonological similarity effects?"], "remember": "Seen letters are recoded into sound, and verbal span depends on rehearsal time; speaking while memorizing disrupts the loop.", "answers": ["Conrad (1964) found that when people saw letters briefly and wrote them down, their errors followed sound, not appearance. They were unlikely to replace a letter with one that looked like it, such as writing F for E, and likely to replace it with one that sounded like it, such as writing B for E. The letters came in through the eyes, but they were held as sound codes in the phonological loop.", "Conrad (1964) manipulated whether the flashed letters were similar or not similar, with half of the presentations each. Participants saw target letters quickly flashed and then wrote them down. The letters vanished so fast that writing them depended on memory. Lists of similar-sounding letters led to more errors than lists of distinct-sounding letters, and the errors that did happen were sound-alike substitutions.", "The word-length effect is that memory for lists is better for short words than for long words. Baddeley and colleagues (1975, 1984) found it. Long words take more time to say, so fewer of them can be rehearsed before they fade. The idea is that the loop holds roughly what you can say in about 1.5 to 2 seconds, so the limit is one of time rather than a fixed number of items.", "Ellis and Hennelly (1980) found that the children had a shorter digit span in Welsh than in English. The children knew the same numbers in both languages, so the difference was not about knowledge. Welsh number names simply take longer to pronounce, and so fewer of them can be rehearsed in the roughly 1.5 to 2 seconds the phonological loop can hold. The limit is speaking time, not the numbers themselves.", "Articulatory suppression, speaking while you memorize, keeps the phonological loop busy, and it makes memory worse. It also reduces both the word-length effect and the phonological similarity effect. The word-length effect shrinks because rehearsal is blocked, so short words lose their advantage. The similarity effect shrinks because seen letters are no longer converted into sound codes. One manipulation removes both, which suggests rehearsal and recoding share the same speech-based system."]}, {"module": 9, "topic": "Spatial representations and Brooks’s response reversal", "questions": ["What did Brooks (1968) find about the sentence task and the F task?", "What was manipulated in Brooks's experiments?", "Why was pointing easier for the sentence but speaking easier for the F?", "What does the reversal suggest about verbal and visuospatial memory?", "Which component is used when holding the shape of an F in mind?"], "remember": "A response is harder when it uses the same kind of memory as the task: sentence with speaking, F with pointing.", "answers": ["Brooks (1968) found a reversal. In the sentence task, people held a sentence and judged each word as a noun or not, and pointing was easier than speaking. In the F task, people held the image of a block F and judged whether each corner was an inside or outside corner, and speaking was easier than pointing. So neither response was easier overall. What mattered was whether the response used the same kind of memory as the task.", "Brooks (1968) manipulated the response mode, speaking versus pointing, across two memory tasks. In the sentence task, participants memorized a sentence and indicated yes or no for whether each word was a noun. In the F task, they memorized a block letter F and moved around its corners, indicating inside or outside corners. The task stayed the same within each experiment, and only the way of responding changed.", "With the sentence, the phonological loop was already busy holding and working through the words, and speaking also needs the loop, so the two competed. Pointing relies on the sketchpad, which was free. With the F, it was the other way around. The image sat in the sketchpad, and pointing means looking and moving the hand to places on a sheet, which uses those same spatial resources, while speaking used the free loop.", "It suggests that verbal and visuospatial memory are somewhat independent, with at least separate capacities. Tasks were easier when the memory and the response used different systems, and harder when they used the same one. Filling the phonological loop leaves the sketchpad room to spare, and the reverse. Each part interferes with itself far more than with the other, which a single store could not produce.", "Holding the shape of an F uses the visuospatial sketchpad, which holds visual and spatial information such as shape, appearance, and layout. That is why, in Brooks's (1968) F task, pointing was harder, since it needed the same spatial resources, and speaking was easier, because the phonological loop was free to help."]}, {"module": 9, "topic": "Mental rotation and executive control", "questions": ["What did Shepard and Metzler (1971) find about angle and reaction time?", "What is the difference between picture-plane and depth rotation?", "What are Miyake and colleagues' (2000) updating, shifting, and inhibition?", "Why is the central executive sometimes criticized as a homunculus?", "Which concept is this an example of: suppressing irrelevant information to keep the current goal?"], "remember": "Reaction time rises with rotation angle, and the central executive controls attention through updating, shifting, and inhibition.", "answers": ["Shepard and Metzler (1971) found that reaction time grew steadily with the angle of rotation between two objects. Pairs at 0 degrees took about 1 second, and pairs at 180 degrees took about 4.5 seconds, with reaction time rising in a straight line in between. Participants mentally rotated one object to line it up with the other, so a bigger difference in angle needed a longer rotation, as though the image turned at a steady speed.", "Picture-plane rotation turns the object flat within the page, much like the hand of a clock. Depth rotation turns the object as if it were spun around a vertical axis, which is a rotation in three-dimensional space. Shepard and Metzler (1971) found the same straight-line increase in reaction time with angle for both, so the mental image is handled as a three-dimensional object, with the work done in the visuospatial sketchpad.", "These are the three functions the central executive is often split into, following Miyake and colleagues (2000). Updating replaces old contents of working memory with new, relevant information. Shifting means moving between tasks or mental sets. Inhibition means holding back unwanted thoughts or responses. Splitting the executive this way gives specific functions that can be measured separately and that vary between people.", "A homunculus is a little person in the head who makes every decision. Saying that a central executive directs attention and controls everything names the control without explaining how it works, so it can look like an empty explanation. Breaking the executive into updating, shifting, and inhibition answers that criticism, because those are specific functions that can be measured separately.", "That is inhibition, one of the central executive's functions from Miyake and colleagues (2000). It means holding back unwanted thoughts or responses, so distractions do not use up working memory. The executive suppresses irrelevant information so the limited space stays available for what the current task needs, which keeps attention on the goal."]}, {"module": 9, "topic": "Integrated episodes and filtering individual differences", "questions": ["What does the episodic buffer do and what is its capacity?", "What did Vogel and colleagues (2005) find for high-capacity versus low-capacity participants when distractors were added?", "What was manipulated in Vogel's task?", "How is filtering efficiency related to working-memory capacity?", "Why can some amnesic patients immediately recall a just-heard story?"], "remember": "The buffer binds loop, sketchpad, and long-term information into episodes; higher capacity goes with better filtering of distractors.", "answers": ["The episodic buffer is a fourth component of working memory, added by Baddeley in 2000. It binds information from the phonological loop, the visuospatial sketchpad, and long-term memory into integrated episodes, joining separate pieces into one combined memory. Its capacity is limited, about four chunks, where a chunk is a meaningful unit that may contain several pieces. It sits between the loop and the sketchpad so their contents can be combined.", "Vogel and colleagues (2005) found that high-capacity people showed greater brain activity for four relevant items than for two, and adding irrelevant blue bars had no effect, so they kept distractors out. Low-capacity people were different. When distractors were added, their activity rose to nearly the level for four targets, so they let the distractors in, where they took up space meant for the targets.", "Vogel and colleagues (2005) first sorted participants into high-capacity and low-capacity groups, then manipulated the display. There were three conditions: two relevant red bars, four relevant red bars, and two red bars with two irrelevant blue bars to ignore. They measured an ERP, electrical brain activity from the scalp, during the blank delay, which tracks how much a person is holding. People judged whether a red bar's angle had changed.", "They are positively related. Filtering efficiency is an EEG measure of how well someone keeps irrelevant items out of working memory, and it correlates with capacity, so people with higher capacity are less distracted by irrelevant items. The people who hold more are the same people who screen out what they do not need. So effective capacity reflects both the size of the store and the strength of the filter.", "Amnesia is a severe loss of long-term memory, so these patients cannot store the story in long-term memory. Yet they can recall a story they just heard, which means it was held in working memory as a meaningful episode. That needs a component large enough to contain it, and that is the episodic buffer, which binds information into integrated episodes. The original model had nowhere to keep it."]}, {"module": 10, "topic": "Problem representation, restructuring, and insight", "questions": ["What are the present state, goal, and obstacle in a problem?", "What did Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987) find about warmth ratings for algebra versus insight problems?", "What was manipulated in Metcalfe and Wiebe's study?", "What is restructuring, and how does it solve Köhler's circle problem?", "How does the triangle problem or chain problem require a change in representation?"], "remember": "A problem has a present state, goal, and obstacle; insight follows restructuring and feels sudden, unlike gradual algebra.", "answers": ["A problem is an obstacle between a present state and a goal, where the solution is not immediately obvious. The present state is the way things are now, the goal is the way we want them to be, and the obstacle is whatever stops us from going straight there. In the sliding-tile puzzle, for example, the scrambled board is the present state, the ordered board is the goal, and the rule that a tile can only move into the empty space is the obstacle.", "Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987) found different patterns. For algebra problems, warmth rose gradually, climbing steadily over the last minute until the solution, so those were solved step by step. For insight problems, warmth stayed low until just before the answer and then jumped sharply, from about 3 to 7. People felt cold only seconds before solving them, so their sense of closeness was a poor guide.", "Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987) manipulated the type of problem. Insight problems, the triangle problem and the chain problem, were compared with noninsight problems, which were algebra problems. Participants gave warmth judgments every 15 seconds, rating from cold to hot how close they felt to the answer. The warmth ratings track the feeling of closeness over time, which shows whether the answer came gradually or suddenly.", "Restructuring is reorganizing the problem's representation so it is seen in a new way. The elements stay the same, but the way the solver arranges them changes. In Köhler's circle problem, you are asked for the length of line x when the radius is r. Seen as a lone line, x is hard. Seen as a diagonal of a rectangle, whose other diagonal is a radius, x equals r, because a rectangle's two diagonals are equal.", "In both problems the obvious approach fails and the answer comes only when the parts are seen differently. In the chain problem, opening one link on each of four pieces costs 20 cents, over the 15 cent budget. You have to treat one whole piece as a set of connectors, opening all three of its links to join the other three pieces. In the triangle problem, you have to see how three dots can be moved so the same triangle points down."]}, {"module": 10, "topic": "Fixation, functional fixedness, and mental set", "questions": ["What did the empty matchbox condition change in the candle problem?", "What is functional fixedness, and how does it apply to the pliers in the two-string problem?", "What is a mental set, and how does the water-jug procedure B − A − 2C illustrate it?", "Which concept is this an example of: failing to see a simpler solution because a familiar method worked before?", "How does fixation differ from a mental set?"], "remember": "Functional fixedness is about an object's familiar use; mental set is about a familiar method. Both hide a workable solution.", "answers": ["The empty matchbox condition removed the cue that made the box look like a container. In the candle problem, full matchboxes looked only like containers for matches, and that familiar function kept people from using them as supports. When participants were given empty matchboxes, they were twice as likely to solve the problem. An empty box no longer looks like a container in use, so its flat bottom is easier to notice as a shelf for the candle.", "Functional fixedness is restricting the use of an object to its familiar functions, so you see one purpose and overlook other possible uses. In the two-string problem, people think of pliers as a gripping tool, and that hides the fact that they are heavy. The solution needs that weight, tying the pliers to one string to make a pendulum and catching it as it swings. The pliers are in plain view the whole time.", "A mental set is a preconceived notion about how to approach a problem, based on past experience with that problem or similar ones. In the water-jug problems, the formula B minus A minus twice C solved each early problem, so people kept using it automatically. Later, in problem 7 the answer 18 is simply A plus C, and in problem 8 the answer 25 is simply A minus C, but the set inhibited people from seeing those simpler solutions.", "That is a mental set. A method that worked before gets used again, even when a simpler one is available. In the water-jug problems, repeated success with B minus A minus twice C builds a habit, and the familiar method still works on the later problems, so nothing signals that anything is wrong. But it hides the easier route. Past success becomes an obstacle in its own right.", "Both are kinds of obstacles to solving, but they differ in what is stuck. Functional fixedness is about an object, restricting it to its familiar use, like seeing the matchbox only as a container or the pliers only as a gripper. A mental set is about a method, a way of solving that worked before and gets used again, like repeating B minus A minus twice C. Fixation is the broader tendency, and fixedness and set are two forms of it."]}, {"module": 10, "topic": "Problem spaces, operators, and means–end analysis", "questions": ["What are the initial state, intermediate states, and goal state in a problem space?", "What do operators do in the Tower of Hanoi?", "What is a subgoal, and what are the Tower of Hanoi subgoals?", "What does means–end analysis try to reduce?", "What does a search tree show for the tile puzzle?"], "remember": "Newell and Simon describe solving as search through states using operators; means–end analysis reduces the gap to the goal by achieving subgoals.", "answers": ["Newell and Simon describe every problem space as having three kinds of states. The initial state holds the conditions at the beginning of the problem. Intermediate states are the conditions partway through, after some moves but before the answer. The goal state is the solution. To solve a problem is to travel through this space, starting at the initial state and making moves that reach intermediate states, until the search arrives at the goal state.", "Operators are the rules that specify which moves are allowed and which are not, and they take the problem from one state to another. In the Tower of Hanoi there are three rules: disks are moved one at a time from one peg to another, a disk can be moved only when nothing is on top of it, and a larger disk cannot go on a smaller one. Every legal move applies one of these operators.", "A subgoal is a smaller goal that must be met before the main goal can be reached, and it creates an intermediate state closer to the goal. In the Tower of Hanoi, the first subgoal is to free the large disk, the second is to free the third peg, and the third is to move the large disk onto the third peg. Each subgoal removes one obstacle to moving the largest disk.", "Means-end analysis tries to reduce the difference between the current state and the goal state. At each point the solver asks how the current state differs from the goal and which move would shrink that difference. When no move can close the main difference directly, the solver sets a subgoal that removes the obstacle. This steers the search toward the goal, so the solver does not have to try every possible move.", "A search tree lays out the problem space of the tile puzzle. Each state is a point in the tree, and each legal move links one state to the next. The starting board allows three moves, so the tree splits three ways, and then keeps branching at every level. It shows how quickly the possibilities multiply while only one path leads to the goal, which is why a good strategy follows the branches that bring the board closer to the goal."]}, {"module": 10, "topic": "Checkerboard representations and think-aloud evidence", "questions": ["Why can 31 dominoes not cover the mutilated checkerboard?", "What conditions were compared in the checkerboard experiment, and what was found?", "How did the bread-and-butter labels help solvers?", "What is a think-aloud protocol, and what does it reveal?", "Which concept is this an example of: a change in representation that makes a constraint available?"], "remember": "Opposite corners are the same color, so colors are unequal; labels like bread and butter made the pairing constraint easier to see, and solving was twice as fast as blank.", "answers": ["The key is color. Each domino covers two neighboring squares, which are always different colors, so every domino covers one dark square and one light one. Opposite corners share the same color, so removing them leaves 32 squares of one color and 30 of the other. Thirty-one dominoes would need 31 squares of each color. The numbers seem to fit, since 62 squares match 31 dominoes, but the colors do not.", "The conditions differed in what information was on the squares: a blank board, a colored board, a board labeled black and pink, and a board labeled bread and butter. The board and dominoes were identical in all four. Participants solved the bread and butter condition twice as fast as the blank condition. So wording that pointed toward the right representation made the problem easier, even though nothing about the problem itself had changed.", "The labels bread and butter emphasized the difference between the two kinds of squares, since bread and butter naturally come in pairs, one of each. That helped solvers see each domino as pairing one square of each kind, which is the constraint that makes the covering impossible. A small change in wording guided people toward the representation that solves the problem, and they solved it twice as fast as with a blank board.", "A think-aloud protocol is a method where participants say aloud what they are thinking while they solve a problem, putting new thoughts into words as they come. It reveals what information the person is attending to, so researchers can follow what the solver notices and when. With the checkerboard, solvers first talked about laying down dominoes, then about pairing the two kinds of squares. That shows restructuring while it happens.", "That is restructuring, the Gestalt idea of a change in representation. In the mutilated checkerboard, the board and the rules do not change, but the solver stops thinking about laying down dominoes and starts thinking about pairing the two kinds of squares. Once each domino is seen as covering one square of each kind, the unequal counts of the two colors become available, and the answer follows from a simple count."]}, {"module": 11, "topic": "Analogical sources, targets, and convergence", "questions": ["What is the difference between a source problem and a target problem?", "In the Russian marriage problem, what do the marriages and the groups of people correspond to in the checkerboard problem?", "What solution did Duncker's radiation problem require, and what percentage solved it without the analogy?", "Which concept is this an example of: sending small groups along different roads so they arrive together at the fortress?", "How did the percentage solving radiation change after reading the fortress story, and after being told to think about it?"], "remember": "Both the fortress and radiation solutions use weak approaches that converge to give a strong effect at one target. Noticing the source is the hard step.", "answers": ["The source problem is the one you already understand, and the target problem is the one you are trying to solve right now. Analogical transfer goes from source to target, and it succeeds when the reasoning you worked out on the source carries over to the new problem. In the example, the Russian marriage problem is the source, because its structure is easy to see, and the mutilated checkerboard is the target.", "The marriages correspond to the dominoes, because each one pairs exactly one of each kind. The men and the women correspond to the two colors of squares, so the dark squares are like one group and the light squares are like the other. The two bachelors who died match the two missing corners, and the two women left without partners match the two leftover squares of one color.", "Duncker's radiation problem needed you to send several low-intensity rays from different directions so they converge on the tumor at the same time. Each ray is weak enough to pass safely through healthy tissue, but where they meet their combined strength destroys the tumor. Only about 10 percent of people reached that solution without any help, which is why it makes such a good test of analogy.", "That is the converging solution, which is the same idea behind the radiation problem. A strong force is needed at one target, but only weak forces can travel along any single road safely, so you split the force into small groups, send them along separate paths, and have them arrive together. Each group is harmless on its own, and the full strength exists only at the fortress.", "Without any story, about 10 percent solved radiation. After reading the fortress story, about 30 percent solved it, which is three times better but still leaves most people stuck. When they were then told to think about the story they had read, the rate jumped to about 75 percent. So the knowledge was in their heads; what was missing was noticing that it applied."]}, {"module": 11, "topic": "Noticing, surface similarity, and structural correspondence", "questions": ["What did 81% versus 10% show in the lightbulb study?", "What is the difference between surface features and structural features?", "Why did the fragile-glass source (69%) help more than the insufficient-intensity source (33%) on radiation?", "What is analogical encoding?", "Which concept is this: noticing, mapping, or applying: connecting the central target to the separate approaches?"], "remember": "Matching structure (a strong effect at the target and protected surroundings) matters more than matching objects, though similar surfaces help noticing.", "answers": ["It showed that a source with similar surface features gets noticed and used. Of the people who knew the radiation problem, 81 percent solved the lightbulb problem, compared with only 10 percent of a control group with no source. Rays and lasers, tumors and filaments, and tissue and glass look alike, so the link comes to mind easily. That is far better than the fortress story managed, which helped only about 30 percent.", "Surface features are the particular objects and details in a problem, like a fortress, a tumor, a laser, or a filament. They are what the problem looks like. Structural features are the underlying principle that governs the solution, the reason the solution works. In radiation and the lightbulb, the structure is that a strong beam would harm what surrounds the target, so the beam has to be split.", "Because the fragile-glass version shares the reason for using several weak beams. In both problems a strong beam would damage the surroundings, the glass or the healthy tissue, so the structure matches, and 69 percent solved radiation. In the insufficient-intensity version the glass is never at risk; the lasers were just too weak, so readers had the steps without the principle, and only 33 percent solved it. Structure mattered more than surface.", "Analogical encoding is comparing two problems or stories and working out what they have in common. It is an effective way to get people to pay attention to structural features. When you compare, say, a soldier story and a laser story, the details that belong to only one of them fade, and the shared idea of weak forces combining at a target stands out. Then you can recognize it in a problem that looks completely different.", "That is mapping. Noticing is recognizing that a source problem might be relevant at all, and applying is using the mapping to build a concrete solution. Connecting the central target to the separate approaches means lining up the parts of the source with the parts of the target, like the fortress with the tumor and the small groups with the weak rays, so it is the mapping step."]}, {"module": 11, "topic": "Real-world analogy and research conditions", "questions": ["What is the analogical paradox?", "How many analogies do scientists use in a one-hour lab meeting?", "What is in vivo problem-solving research?", "What is one tradeoff of in vivo research compared with a lab experiment?"], "remember": "Analogies are hard to transfer in the lab but common in real work (about 3-15 per meeting). In vivo trades control for real context.", "answers": ["The analogical paradox is that analogies are hard to apply in the laboratory, yet people use them all the time in real settings. People miss a useful source in an experiment when they are handed an unfamiliar story, but in their own work they come up with analogies from knowledge they understand well. Those analogies tend to share both surface and structural features with the problem, so they are easy to notice and use.", "Scientists use about 3 to 15 analogies in a one-hour lab meeting. That shows analogies are common in real work even though people often miss them in lab tasks. They draw on deep knowledge of related experiments, so their analogies fit the problem in both surface and structure, which is part of why they come to mind so readily.", "In vivo problem-solving research studies problem solving where it naturally happens. People are observed to see how they actually solve problems in the real world, such as scientists in their own lab meetings. In vivo means in the living, meaning real life as opposed to the lab. Its advantage is the naturalistic setting, which shows how people really solve problems at work.", "One tradeoff is that researchers cannot isolate and control variables the way they can in an experiment. You get real context, but you lose the control that lets you say what caused what. It is also time-consuming. So you gain a naturalistic setting and give up the tight control of the lab."]}, {"module": 11, "topic": "Expert knowledge, chunks, and problem organization", "questions": ["What did Chase and Simon (1973) find about experts and random chess arrangements?", "What is a chunk?", "How did novices and experts sort the physics problems in Chi et al. (1982)?", "Which concept explains why an expert groups problems 21 and 24 together?", "Do experts outperform novices outside their field?"], "remember": "Experts rely on organized knowledge (chunks, principles) within their field. Outside the field they are no better than novices.", "answers": ["Chase and Simon (1973) compared a chess master with a beginner who had under 100 hours of play. After seeing a board for five seconds, the master reproduced real game positions far better, because those positions were stored in long-term memory and accessed in chunks. With random arrangements, those chunks were not available, so the master's advantage mostly vanished. The expert's edge is organized knowledge, not a general memory advantage.", "A chunk is a group of pieces that forms a familiar pattern from real games and is stored in long-term memory. An expert sees a position as a few meaningful chunks instead of many separate pieces, so five seconds is enough to recall it. A beginner has few stored patterns and has to remember each piece on its own.", "Chi and colleagues (1982) gave 24 physics problems to experts, who were physics professors, and to novices, who were students with one semester of physics, and asked them to sort the problems by similarity. Novices sorted by surface features, what the objects looked like, so blocks on slopes went together. Experts sorted by structural features, the underlying principles, so problems needing conservation of energy went together even when they looked different.", "The expert is using structural features, in particular the underlying physics principle, here conservation of energy. Problem 21 is a compressed spring pushing a block along a flat surface, with no slope, so it looks nothing like problem 24. But both are solved with conservation of energy, and the expert files problems under the principle that solves them. A novice would pair 24 with 23 because both have inclined planes.", "No. Experts are no better than novices when given problems outside their field, because expertise belongs to a particular domain. A chess master has no special edge in physics, and a physicist has none in chess. The advantage comes from knowledge of the field, organized in long-term memory, and not from some general problem-solving ability."]}, {"module": 11, "topic": "Creativity, divergent thinking, and implementing solutions", "questions": ["What is divergent thinking?", "What stages come after generating ideas in creative problem solving?", "How can too much knowledge hinder creative problem solving?", "What does thinking flexibly require when a familiar procedure does not fit?"], "remember": "Ideas are necessary but not enough. Evaluation, planning, and implementation are separate parts. Familiar procedures can block flexibility.", "answers": ["Divergent thinking is open-ended thinking that produces a large number of potential solutions. Instead of narrowing straight to one answer, you generate many possible answers to one question, which raises the odds that one of them turns out to be good. It is a key part of creativity, along with novel ideas and new connections between existing ideas.", "After ideas are generated, they still have to be evaluated, planned, and implemented. Creative problem solving is a process with stages: problem finding and fact finding, then problem definition and idea finding, then evaluation and planning, and finally solution implementation, which means taking action. Ideas are necessary, but having an idea and producing a solution are two different achievements.", "Too much knowledge can hinder creativity because problems often need flexible thinking and the rejection of accepted procedures. Someone who knows the usual approach very well may struggle to set it aside, much like the danger experts face. Knowledge gives the raw material for ideas, but it also supplies familiar procedures that can crowd out new approaches.", "It requires being willing to reject the usual procedure and use another one that is used less often. A well-practiced method tends to come to mind first and can work like a mental set, blocking alternatives. So when a problem calls for an unusual approach, you have to drop the familiar method, and the same deep knowledge that makes experts fast can make them rigid."]}, {"module": 12, "topic": "Judgments, decisions, and inductive evidence", "questions": ["What is the difference between decisions and reasoning?", "What is inductive reasoning?", "What three things determine the strength of an inductive argument?", "How does the black swan example show the weight of one high-quality observation?", "Why are inductive conclusions only probably true?"], "remember": "Decisions choose between alternatives. Reasoning draws conclusions. Induction goes from observations to probable conclusions.", "answers": ["Decisions are the process of making choices between alternatives, while reasoning is the process of drawing conclusions. They are closely linked, because decisions are based on the judgments we make, and reaching those judgments involves reasoning. For example, judging that rain is likely is reasoning, and deciding to bring an umbrella is the decision that follows from it.", "Inductive reasoning is reasoning based on observations. You reach conclusions from evidence by going from particular cases to a general conclusion. It is one of the primary mechanisms behind judgments, and it is the basis of scientific investigations, where observations are made, data are collected, and conclusions are drawn. We also use it in everyday life to predict the future from past experience.", "The strength of an inductive argument depends on three things. First is how representative the observations are of the whole category. Second is how many observations there are, since more give stronger support. Third is the quality of the observations, because careful, accurate ones count for more. Sometimes one very compelling, high-quality observation can carry a great deal of weight.", "Many white swans support the claim that all swans are white, but one clearly observed black swan contradicts it. An inductive conclusion is a general claim built from particular cases, so a single clear counterexample can prove it false no matter how many cases supported it before. That is how much one high-quality observation can weigh.", "Because the conclusions go beyond the observations we have, and a new observation can always overturn them. In induction, conclusions are probably, but not definitely, true, and they are suggested with various degrees of certainty. They are always open to revision, unlike a conclusion that must follow from its premises."]}, {"module": 12, "topic": "Availability, representativeness, and sample size", "questions": ["What did people choose in the r-first versus r-third word question, and what is the truth?", "What is base-rate neglect, and how does the Jack problem show it?", "Why is choosing feminist bank teller for Linda a conjunction error?", "What is the correct answer to the hospital question, and why?", "What does the law of large numbers say?"], "remember": "Availability uses ease of remembering. Representativeness uses resemblance. Small samples vary more than large ones.", "answers": ["About 70 percent of people said more words begin with r, but in reality three times more words have r in the third position. That is the availability heuristic. Words starting with r are easy to retrieve, because memory lets us search by first letter, while third-letter words are hard to retrieve even though there are far more of them. People answer by ease of recall, not by true frequency.", "Base-rate neglect is ignoring how common each category is in the population when a description is available. In the Jack problem, a group has 70 lawyers and 30 engineers. With no description, people correctly say the chance of an engineer is 30 percent. But once Jack is described as careful, conservative, and fond of carpentry and math puzzles, people say engineer, even though the group still has far more lawyers.", "Because feminist bank tellers are a smaller group inside the group of all bank tellers, so the combination cannot be more probable than one part alone. This is the conjunction rule. About 82 percent picked the feminist option, since it sounds more like Linda. That sense of fit overrides the logic, which is representativeness producing an error.", "The correct answer is the smaller hospital, with about 15 births a day. Sample size controls how far a percentage can drift from the true value by chance. With few births, the share of boys swings widely, so days over 60 percent boys happen more often, while the larger hospital stays nearer 50 percent. Yet 56 percent said about the same, and only 22 percent chose the smaller hospital.", "The law of large numbers says that the larger the number of individuals randomly drawn from a population, the more representative the resulting group will be of the whole population. A large sample evens out chance variation, while a small one can be thrown off by a few unusual cases. People wrongly expect small samples to look representative, which is the error in the hospital problem."]}, {"module": 12, "topic": "Illusory correlation, myside bias, and hypothesis testing", "questions": ["What is an illusory correlation?", "How does a stereotype connect to the availability heuristic?", "What did Lord and colleagues (1979) find about people who favored or opposed capital punishment?", "What is the difference between confirmation bias and myside bias?", "What was the actual rule in the 2, 4, 6 task, and what hypothesis was most common?"], "remember": "People look for and favor information that fits what they already believe, and rarely test what could disprove it.", "answers": ["An illusory correlation is when a correlation appears to exist, but either does not exist or is much weaker than assumed. It tends to happen when we expect two things to be related, so what we expect guides what we notice, and what we notice guides what we believe. We fool ourselves into thinking they are related even when they are not.", "A stereotype leads people to pay particular attention to behaviors associated with that stereotype. That selective attention makes those behaviors more available in memory, so they come to mind easily. People then take those easily recalled examples as proof of the stereotype, creating an illusory correlation that reinforces it. They notice the cases that fit and miss the many that do not.", "Lord and colleagues (1979) had people in favor of capital punishment and people against it read the same article, which presented evidence for both sides. Each group found that the article supported its own view. The identical information strengthened opposite beliefs, so the difference came entirely from how each group judged it. This is the myside bias, favoring evidence that agrees with your own opinions.", "Confirmation bias is the tendency to look for information that fits a hypothesis and overlook information that argues against it. Myside bias is a type of confirmation bias, specifically generating and evaluating evidence and testing hypotheses in a way biased toward your own opinions and attitudes. So the 2, 4, 6 task shows confirmation bias in general, while the capital punishment article shows myside bias.", "The actual rule was three numbers in increasing order of magnitude, so any rising sequence fit. The most common hypothesis was increasing intervals of two. People kept testing sets that matched their own guess, and every such set also fit the real rule, so the yes feedback made them confident in the wrong rule. Only a set that breaks your own guess could reveal that the real rule is broader."]}, {"module": 12, "topic": "Utility, probabilities, and choices that cause harm", "questions": ["What does expected utility theory assume people do?", "Why do utility procedures not fully describe real choices (for example, casino gambling)?", "In the jellybean choice, which bowl did most people pick and which has the higher probability?", "What did Ritov and Baron (1999) find about rerouting the convoy?", "How did avoiding air travel after September 11 increase harm?"], "remember": "People do not always maximize expected benefit. They are swayed by counts, harm avoidance, and fear.", "answers": ["Expected utility theory assumes that people are rational. If they have all the relevant information, they will make the decision that results in the maximum expected utility, which combines how good each outcome is with how likely it is. In its early form, utility meant maximum monetary payoff. You multiply each outcome's value by its probability, add them up, and choose the largest total.", "Because utility is not necessarily money, and people find value in other things, like safety, fairness, or how a choice makes them feel. Many decisions do not maximize the probability of the best outcome. People gamble in casinos all the time, even though they know the odds are very much against them. So the approach is a strong standard but a weak description of how people really choose.", "Most people chose the bowl with 7 red jellybeans out of 100. But the other bowl, with 1 red out of 10, has the higher probability, 10 percent compared with 7 percent. Their choice was influenced by considerations other than probability. The second bowl has more red beans, so it seems to offer more chances, but people should compare ratios, not counts.", "Ritov and Baron (1999) asked about a food convoy that could be rerouted to a second camp, saving 1,000 people but causing 100 in the first camp to die. Rerouting saves 900 more lives, so expected utility says to reroute, yet 63 percent did not. Twelve percent would not change the route even if only one person in the first camp would die. Causing harm by acting weighed more than the larger benefit.", "After the September 11 attacks, air travel decreased and driving increased, even though the probability of dying in a car accident is far greater than dying in a plane. More Americans lost their lives on the road than the total number of passengers killed on the four hijacked flights. The choice that felt safer was actually more dangerous. Fear and vivid, heavily reported events made flying feel riskier than it was."]}, {"module": 12, "topic": "Sacred values, taboo and tragic tradeoffs", "questions": ["What is a sacred value, and how is it kept separate from secular values?", "What is the difference between a taboo tradeoff and a tragic tradeoff?", "What did Tetlock and colleagues (2000) find about outrage when Johnny was not saved in each tradeoff?", "Which tradeoff produced similar outrage whether Johnny was saved or not?"], "remember": "Taboo mixes sacred with secular and causes outrage. Tragic compares two sacred values, so outcome matters less.", "answers": ["A sacred value is something people treat as beyond price, such as human life, loyalty, or justice, and people want to protect it. In the sacred value protection model of Tetlock, Kristel, Elson, Green, and Lerner (2000), sacred values are kept in a separate compartment from secular values like money and time. When the two are mixed together, people feel threatened and respond with moral outrage.", "A taboo tradeoff mixes the sacred and secular domains, such as trading a child's life for money, and it provokes moral outrage because putting a price on something sacred treats it as a commodity. A tragic tradeoff compares two sacred values with each other, such as choosing between two lives. It is painful, but people are less morally outraged because the choice does not cross the line between sacred and secular.", "Tetlock and colleagues (2000) had a hospital administrator decide whether to save Johnny, a boy needing a liver transplant. In the taboo scenario, his life was weighed against a million dollars. Not saving him produced very high outrage, about 5.4 on a scale topping out at 6, compared with about 2.5 when he was saved. So the choice mattered a great deal when a life was traded for money.", "The tragic tradeoff, where one child's life was set against another's because there was only one liver. Outrage was moderate and nearly the same whether or not Johnny was saved. Either way a life was saved, so neither option broke the line between sacred and secular. Outcome matters less when two sacred values are compared."]}, {"module": 12, "topic": "Sacred values in intergroup negotiation", "questions": ["Who were the participants in Ginges, Atran, Medin, and Shikaki (2007)?", "What was added in Taboo+, and what was added in the symbolic version?", "How did moral absolutists and non-absolutists differ in response to money?", "What percentage of settlers held the sacred-value position on the Land of Israel?", "How did symbolic recognition compare with money for anger and support for violence?"], "remember": "For sacred issues money can backfire (more anger and support for violence), while symbolic reciprocity helps.", "answers": ["The participants were Israeli settlers, 601 of them, Palestinian refugees, 535, and Palestinian students affiliated with various political groups, 719. All were directly involved in the Israeli and Palestinian conflict, so the study extended the earlier findings from imagined scenarios to a realistic setting with real stakes. They were offered deals as possible solutions to the conflict.", "Taboo+ added money to the taboo compromise: Israel would pay Palestine 1 billion dollars a year for 10 years. The symbolic version added a symbolic concession instead. Israel would recognize the historic and legitimate right of the Palestinians to their own state and apologize for the wrongs done to them. Recognition and an apology address what the other side holds sacred, so the deal becomes a tragic tradeoff.", "They responded in opposite ways. For non-absolutists, adding money slightly lowered anger and disgust and lowered support for violence, just as a cost and benefit view predicts. For moral absolutists, adding money raised anger and disgust and raised support for violence. For them, money turned a hard compromise into an insulting trade of the sacred for cash. They judge a deal by what it says about their values, not its payoff.", "Among the settlers, 46 percent were moral absolutists. They believed it was never permissible for the Jewish people to give up part of the Land of Israel, no matter how great the benefit. For Palestinian students, the figure was 54 percent, who treated both the right of return and Jerusalem as sacred values.", "Symbolic recognition did better than money. Adding recognition and an apology lowered anger and disgust and also lowered support for violence, so a symbolic gesture succeeded where money failed. For moral absolutists, money raised anger and support for violence. When a value is sacred, material incentives backfire, while a symbolic concession answers a sacred value with a sacred value, which feels less like a betrayal."]}, {"module": 12, "topic": "Nuclear-program study, manipulations, measures, and energy results", "questions": ["What is the design of the Iranian online study (Dehghani and colleagues)?", "What do Taboo, Taboo+, and Taboo- each involve?", "How was a sacred value classified, and how many participants did so?", "What did sacred-value participants show for approval and anger with funds or sanctions in the energy condition?", "What three judgments did participants make?"], "remember": "Funds and sanctions did not raise approval for sacred-value participants and raised anticipated anger. Non-sacred participants barely changed.", "answers": ["It was an online study conducted in Farsi with 1,997 Iranian participants, using a 2 by 3 design. One factor was the nuclear program, energy or weapons, and the other was the type of deal, Taboo, Taboo+, or Taboo-. This was by Dehghani, Atran, and colleagues (2010) and Dehghani, Iliev, and colleagues (2009). It asked whether Iranians who hold the program as sacred would be open to material incentives or disincentives.", "In the Taboo deal, Iran gives up its program and surrenders its nuclear facilities to the UN, and in return Israel does the same. Taboo+ adds an incentive, 40 billion dollars in economic funds from the United Nations to Iran. Taboo- adds a disincentive instead, so that if Iran does not accept the deal, the UN would impose additional sanctions. Incentives change the benefit of accepting, and disincentives change the cost of refusing.", "Participants were asked what they thought about Iran giving up its program. Those who chose the final answer, that it should not be done no matter how great the consequences, were classified as holding a sacred value. That refuses every tradeoff, whatever is offered. It was 14 percent of participants for nuclear energy and 13 percent for nuclear weapons.", "For sacred-value participants in the energy condition, approval was lower than for the others, and adding funds or sanctions lowered it even further. Anger was higher, and adding money or sanctions raised it, with anger highest with funds. So sweeteners and threats made the deal less acceptable. Participants without a sacred value approved highly and expected little anger, because to them money and sanctions are ordinary costs and benefits.", "Participants gave three judgments about each deal. They rated their own personal approval of the deal, how angry people would be if the Iranian government approved it, and whether they would find the deal misleading if it were really proposed to Iran. Approval measured personal support, anger measured the expected public reaction, and the misleading question asked about distrust of the deal's intentions."]}, {"module": 13, "topic": "Emotions, expected emotions, and risk aversion", "questions": ["What did Kermer and colleagues (2006) find about expected versus actual emotions after a loss?", "What was manipulated in Kermer and colleagues (2006)?", "How do expected emotions relate to risk aversion?", "Why do people overestimate how bad a loss will feel?", "How do flattened emotions, anxiety, and optimism each affect decision making?"], "remember": "People expect a loss to hurt more than an equal gain helps, but coping mechanisms make the real feeling smaller; this raises risk aversion.", "answers": ["They found that people badly overestimate how bad a loss will feel. Before the coin flip, participants expected losing three dollars to drop their happiness by about 4 points and winning five dollars to raise it by about 2 points. After the flip, the real drop was only about 1 point and the real rise about 1.2. So the loss hurt far less than predicted, because people forget about the coping mechanisms that switch on after a loss.", "The outcome of a coin flip was what varied. Participants were given five dollars and told the flip would decide whether they won an extra five dollars or lost three. They rated their happiness first to set a baseline, then predicted how they would feel after winning or losing, and later reported how they actually felt, so the researchers could compare expected emotions with actual emotions.", "Expected emotions are one of the things that determine risk aversion. Risk aversion is the tendency to avoid taking risks, and when you predict that a loss will make you feel terrible, you become less willing to take a chance that could lead to it. Because people believe a loss will hit harder than an equal gain would help, they avoid risks more than they should.", "People overestimate it because they forget about their coping mechanisms. After a loss, we usually have ways of coping that get activated and soften how we really feel, but when we predict our emotions ahead of time, we do not take those into account. So the prediction misses by a lot, as in Kermer and colleagues (2006), where the real drop was far smaller than expected.", "Flattened emotions make decisions worse. People with damage to part of the prefrontal cortex feel much less than usual, and their decision making is impaired, so emotion is part of deciding well. Anxiety leads people to avoid choices that could lead to large negative consequences, which is risk avoidance. Optimism leads people to ignore negative information and focus on the positive, and too much of it leads to poor decisions."]}, {"module": 13, "topic": "Incidental emotions and mood", "questions": ["What did Simonsohn (2007) find about cloudy days and admissions?", "What did Simonsohn (2009) find about campus visits?", "What was manipulated in Lerner and colleagues (2004)?", "What did Lerner and colleagues (2004) find about buying and selling prices for sad and disgust groups?", "Which concept is this an example of: a film clip changes how much a highlighter seems worth?"], "remember": "Incidental emotions come from unrelated things (weather, clips) yet change decisions.", "answers": ["They found that cloudy weather changed how applicants were judged. Simonsohn (2007) looked at university admissions decisions and found that applicants' academic attributes were weighted more heavily on cloudy days than on sunny days. The weather had nothing to do with the applicants, yet it shifted what the decision makers paid most attention to. That makes weather an incidental factor from the surroundings.", "He found that prospective students visiting a highly rated university were more likely to enroll if they had visited the campus on a cloudy day. Simonsohn (2009) is about enrollment, not admissions. The weather on a single visit says nothing about how good the school is, yet it carried into one of the biggest decisions a student makes.", "What was manipulated was the mood participants were in before deciding, using a film clip. Lerner and colleagues (2004) had people watch one of three clips: a sad one showing a person dying, a disgust one showing a person using a dirty toilet, or a neutral one showing fish at the Great Barrier Reef. Then everyone set prices for a set of highlighters.", "Both groups differed from the neutral group, but in different ways. For selling, the neutral group asked about 4.50 dollars, the disgust group about 2.70 and the sad group about 3.00. For buying, neutral offered about 3.50, disgust about 3.00 and sad about 4.50. So disgust lowered both prices, while sadness lowered the selling price but raised the buying price.", "This is an example of an incidental emotion. The mood comes from a film clip, which has nothing to do with the highlighters, yet it changes what the highlighters seem worth. Incidental emotions are not specifically related to the decision itself. They can come from a person's disposition, a recent experience, or the surroundings, and they still influence what people decide."]}, {"module": 13, "topic": "Context, defaults, and the status quo bias", "questions": ["What was manipulated in the physician cesarean study?", "Which group of physicians recommended cesarean more often, and why?", "How do opt-in and opt-out differ, and what consent rates result?", "Which concept is this an example of: people leaving the default organ donor choice unchanged?"], "remember": "Context and defaults shape choices; status quo bias is doing nothing, so the default wins.", "answers": ["The context that came before the same test case was manipulated. Physicians read a hypothetical case of a possible cesarean candidate, and the case itself never changed. In the control condition it came first, in another it came after four cases with serious complications, and in the third it came after four fairly routine, not serious cases. The question was whether the earlier cases changed the recommendation.", "The physicians who had read the non-serious cases first recommended cesarean significantly more often. The test case was identical for everyone, so the cases they had just considered must have changed their judgment. After four routine cases, the test case looked serious by comparison, so more doctors recommended surgery. Even experts get swayed by the context their decision is made in.", "In an opt-in procedure you have to take an active step to become an organ donor, and countries using it have less than a 28 percent consent rate. In an opt-out procedure you are a donor unless you request not to be, and those countries have over a 99 percent consent rate. The difference comes from what happens when people simply do nothing.", "That is the status quo bias, which is the tendency to do nothing when faced with a difficult decision. If people leave the default organ donor choice unchanged, the default wins. Under opt-in, doing nothing means you are not a donor, and under opt-out, doing nothing means you are one. Many people do nothing either way, which is why the consent rates are so far apart."]}, {"module": 13, "topic": "Framing gains and losses", "questions": ["What did Tversky and Kahneman (1981) find in the gain frame?", "What did Tversky and Kahneman (1981) find in the loss frame?", "How are Programs A and C related?", "Which strategy does a gain frame encourage, and which does a loss frame encourage?", "How does risk aversion differ from risk taking?"], "remember": "Same outcomes, different words: gain frame gives sure option (72% A), loss frame gives gamble (78% D).", "answers": ["In the gain frame, people mostly took the sure option. The disease was expected to kill 600 people, and the outcomes were described as people saved. Program A saved 200 for certain, and Program B gave a one in three chance of saving all 600 and a two in three chance of saving none. Seventy-two percent chose Program A, and 28 percent chose Program B.", "In the loss frame, people mostly took the gamble. The same disease problem was worded in terms of people who die. Program C had 400 people dying for certain, and Program D gave a one in three chance that no one dies and a two in three chance that all 600 die. Seventy-eight percent chose Program D, and only 22 percent chose Program C.", "They are the same outcome described in different words. Saving 200 of 600 people means that 400 die, so Program A and Program C come to exactly the same thing. Program B and Program D are also the same gamble. Only the wording changed, from people saved to people who die, and yet most people switched from the sure option to the gamble.", "A gain frame encourages a risk-aversion strategy, meaning people prefer the sure option. A loss frame encourages a risk-taking strategy, meaning people prefer the gamble. This is the framing effect: the way a decision is stated highlights what might be gained or what might be lost, and the same people can switch strategies depending only on the wording.", "Risk aversion is the tendency to avoid taking risks, which in the disease problem means choosing the sure option. Risk taking is going for the gamble, accepting uncertainty in the hope of a better result. Tversky and Kahneman (1981) showed that the same people can show either one, depending on whether the outcomes are framed as gains or as losses."]}, {"module": 13, "topic": "Neuroeconomics and the ultimatum game", "questions": ["What did Sanfey and colleagues (2003) find about human versus computer offers?", "What does utility theory say the responder should do?", "What did Sanfey and colleagues (2003) find about the right anterior insula?", "What is neuroeconomics?"], "remember": "Responders reject unfair offers out of anger, even at a cost; insula activity tracks rejecting.", "answers": ["People rejected unfair offers much more often when they came from a human than from a computer. Sanfey and colleagues (2003) found that fair offers, five and five or seven and three, were accepted almost always from either partner. But an eight and two offer was accepted about 53 percent of the time from a human and about 85 percent from a computer, and nine and one about 37 percent versus 68 percent. People were less angry with an unfair computer.", "Utility theory says the responder should accept any offer. It judges a choice by the value of its outcome, and even one dollar is worth more than the nothing you get by rejecting. If the responder rejects, neither player gets paid, so a responder following utility theory would never turn down money. The finding was that real people do not behave that way.", "They found that people with more activation of the right anterior insula were more likely to reject more offers. The insula is an area connected with emotional states. People often rejected low offers because they became angry that the offers were unfair, and that anger cost them money. So the brain activity fits the idea that emotion is important in decision making.", "Neuroeconomics combines research from psychology, neuroscience and economics. Psychology contributes how people think and feel, neuroscience contributes what happens in the brain, and economics contributes how choices about value are analyzed. Putting them together lets researchers study a decision and the brain activity behind it at the same time, including how emotions tied to specific brain areas influence choices."]}, {"module": 13, "topic": "Deductive reasoning, validity, and belief bias", "questions": ["What is a syllogism and what are its parts?", "How does validity differ from truth?", "What is belief bias, and what percent judged invalid believable syllogisms as valid?", "Which concept is this an example of: All birds are animals, All animals have four legs, All birds have four legs?", "Is this valid: All of the students are tired, Some tired people are irritable, Some of the students are irritable?"], "remember": "Valid means it follows from the premises, not that it is true. Belief bias confuses believable with valid.", "answers": ["A syllogism is the basic form of deductive reasoning, introduced by Aristotle. It has two statements called premises, which are the statements the reasoning starts from, and a third statement called the conclusion, which is what you arrive at. The only question you ask is whether the conclusion follows from the two premises. For example, all birds are animals and all animals eat food, so all birds eat food.", "Validity is about whether the conclusion follows logically from the premises, and it depends on the form of the reasoning. Truth is about whether the statements match the world. They can come apart. If the premises of a valid syllogism are true, the conclusion must be true, but a valid form with a false premise can still give a false conclusion.", "Belief bias is the tendency to think a syllogism is valid just because its conclusion is believable. It means you judge by whether the ending sounds true instead of whether it actually follows from the premises. Seventy-one percent of participants judged invalid syllogisms with believable conclusions as valid, which shows how easily good reasoning and truth get confused.", "This is an example of a valid syllogism with a false conclusion, which shows that validity is not the same as truth. It has the same form as the birds, animals, eat food example, so it is valid. The conclusion that all birds have four legs is false only because the second premise, that all animals have four legs, is false.", "No, this is not valid, even though every statement sounds reasonable. The problem is the word some. Saying some tired people are irritable leaves open which tired people they are, so all the students being tired does not place any of them among the irritable ones. The conclusion sounds believable, which is exactly why belief bias makes this easy to get wrong."]}, {"module": 13, "topic": "Mental models", "questions": ["What is a mental model according to Johnson-Laird (1999)?", "What are the steps of the mental model approach?", "When is a conclusion valid under the mental model approach?", "Why does imagining the pool table help?"], "remember": "Build a picture, draw a tentative conclusion, search for an exception; one exception means not valid.", "answers": ["According to Johnson-Laird (1999), a mental model is a specific situation represented in a person's mind that can be used to help determine the validity of syllogisms in deductive reasoning. You build a picture of what the premises describe and reason from that picture. A model is one concrete way the premises could be true.", "There are four steps. First, you create a model of the situation the premises describe. Second, you generate tentative conclusions about the model, meaning conclusions that might hold. Third, you look for exceptions that would falsify the model, searching for a case where the premises are true but the conclusion is not. Fourth, you determine the validity of the syllogism based on whether an exception turned up.", "A conclusion is valid only if it cannot be refuted by any model of the premises. To refute it means showing it can be false. So if you can find even one model that fits the premises but not the conclusion, the conclusion does not follow and the syllogism is not valid. If none of the ways the premises could be true refutes it, then it is valid.", "It helps because it shows how people reason by imagining a situation. In the pool table problem, you picture the black ball above the cue ball, the green ball to the right, and the red ball between them, and from that picture you work out that the cue ball is to the left of the line of sight. Most people solve it by imagining the arrangement, and that is the basis of the mental model proposal."]}, {"module": 13, "topic": "Conditional syllogisms", "questions": ["What is modus ponens and how many judged it correctly?", "What is modus tollens and why is it harder?", "Why is If p then q, q, therefore p not valid?", "Why is If p then q, not p, therefore not q not valid?", "Which two of the four conditional syllogisms are valid?"], "remember": "Valid: p so q (97%), not q so not p (60%). Invalid: q so p and not p so not q (40% each).", "answers": ["Modus ponens is the conditional syllogism that goes from p to q. If I study, I'll get a good grade, I studied, therefore I'll get a good grade. Since the condition is met, what follows from it has to follow too. It is valid, and 97 percent of participants judged it correctly. It moves forward, in the same direction as the rule, which is why it is the easiest of the four.", "Modus tollens goes from not q to not p. If I study, I'll get a good grade, I didn't get a good grade, therefore I didn't study. It is valid, because if studying always leads to a good grade, a missing grade means the studying did not happen. It is harder because it runs backward, against the direction of the rule, and only 60 percent judged it correctly.", "Because it runs from the result back to the condition, and the first premise never says that p is the only way to get q. If I live in Tucson, then I live in Arizona, and I live in Arizona, but that does not mean I live in Tucson. Someone can live in Arizona without living in Tucson, so the conclusion does not follow. Only 40 percent judged it correctly.", "Because if p then q tells you what happens when p is true, but it says nothing about what happens when p is false. If I study, I'll get a good grade, and I didn't study, but I could still get a good grade some other way. So denying p settles nothing about q. This is the same kind of error as the previous one, from the other side, and again only 40 percent got it right.", "The two valid ones are modus ponens and modus tollens. Modus ponens goes from p to q, and modus tollens goes from not q to not p. The two invalid ones go from q to p, and from not p to not q. The valid types confirm p or deny q, while the invalid types confirm q or deny p. People judged both valid ones correctly more often than both invalid ones, at 97 and 60 percent versus 40 percent each."]}, {"module": 13, "topic": "The Wason four-card problem and permission schemas", "questions": ["Which cards should be turned over in the E, K, 4, 7 problem, and why?", "What is the falsification principle?", "What happened in the concrete beer version?", "What is a permission schema?", "How does the abstract version differ from the concrete version?"], "remember": "Test a rule by looking for what could break it: E and 7; beer and 16. Permission schemas make the concrete version easier.", "answers": ["You should turn over E and 7. To test the rule that a vowel on one side means an even number on the other, you look for cards that could break it. The E might hide an odd number, and the 7 might hide a vowel, and either would break the rule. The K is not a vowel, and the 4 can only fit the rule, so turning those two tests nothing.", "The falsification principle says that to test a rule, you must look for situations that falsify the rule, which means situations that would prove it false. Most people do not do this. Instead of looking for what could break the rule, they go for cards that seem to fit it, which is why in the abstract problem 46 percent chose the 4 and only 7 percent chose the correct 7.", "The rule was that if someone is drinking beer, then they must be 21 years old, and the cards read beer, soda, 16 years old and 21 years old. When the problem was put in concrete everyday terms, correct responses greatly increased, and 73 percent answered correctly. The right cards are beer and 16 years old, because someone drinking beer while under 21 is the only case that breaks the rule.", "A permission schema is the idea that if A is satisfied, B can be carried out. It is a familiar pattern of knowledge about rules that grant permission once a condition is met. In the beer rule, being 21 is the condition and drinking beer is what can then be carried out. It tells people exactly what to check: the age of the beer drinker, and the drink of the sixteen-year-old.", "The two versions have the same if-then form, but the abstract one is about letters and numbers and the concrete one is about familiar things. In the abstract version only 7 percent chose the correct 7 and 46 percent chose the 4. In the concrete version, 73 percent were correct. The concrete rule calls up a permission schema, and context matters, though familiarity alone does not explain the improvement."]}, {"module": 14, "topic": "Phonological Similarity Effect", "questions": ["What is the phonological similarity effect?", "What was manipulated when people said 'one, two, three, four' out loud?", "Which concept is this an example of: mix-ups between E and F would show a visual code, but sound-alike mix-ups show...?"], "remember": "Sound-alike items hurt recall even when read silently, so we recode into speech.", "answers": ["The phonological similarity effect is the finding that people recall a list worse when its items sound alike than when they sound different. What makes it striking is that it still happens when people read the items silently, with no sound at all. That tells us the sound is what matters, which is why acoustic similarity effect is arguably the better name.", "What was manipulated there was whether people kept their speech-based recoding available. On some trials they said one, two, three, four out loud over and over, which is articulatory suppression, and on others they stayed quiet. Suppression keeps the articulatory control process busy, so visual items cannot be recoded into speech, and then sound-alike and sound-different lists were recalled equally well.", "This is an example of a phonological code, meaning a speech-based form of the items. If memory held only a visual code, the mix-ups would be between letters that look alike, such as E and F. Because sound-alike letters are the ones recalled worse, even when read silently, people must be recoding what they see into speech."]}, {"module": 14, "topic": "Neighborhood Size Effect", "questions": ["What did Roodenrys et al. (2002) find about large neighborhood words?", "What is a word's neighborhood?", "Why is memory span not a pure measure of short-term capacity?"], "remember": "Long-term word knowledge helps short-term recall.", "answers": ["Roodenrys and colleagues (2002) found that in short-term memory tasks, words from large neighborhoods are recalled better than words from small neighborhoods. People saw a sequence of words and then gave them back in order, and a word like cat did better than a word like hippopotamus. So what a person knows about the language helps them hold words briefly.", "A word's neighborhood is every word you can make by changing one letter, which is the orthographic neighborhood, or one sound, which is the phonological neighborhood. Cat has many neighbors, such as bat, cot and can, so it is a large neighborhood word. Hippopotamus has none, so it is a small neighborhood word.", "Memory span is not a pure measure of short-term capacity because long-term memory affects it. Roodenrys and colleagues (2002) showed that words with many neighbors are recalled better, so knowledge stored in long-term memory changes short-term performance. If span depends partly on what you already know, it cannot be telling you only about short-term capacity."]}, {"module": 14, "topic": "Brown-Peterson Task", "questions": ["What did Peterson and Peterson (1959) conclude about forgetting in short-term memory?", "What was manipulated in the Brown-Peterson task?", "Which concept is this an example of: forgetting because information fades unless rehearsed?"], "remember": "Longer distraction, worse recall: the authors said decay.", "answers": ["Peterson and Peterson (1959) concluded that short-term memory holds information only briefly, and that without rehearsal it is lost through decay. The longer people counted backwards before recall, the less likely they were to remember the letters. Because counting backwards is so different from remembering letters, they judged interference an unlikely cause. Decay is their own conclusion, and the results have also been given an alternative explanation.", "What was manipulated was how long people did a distractor task before they had to recall. People saw three consonants, then a number, and counted backwards out loud by threes for several seconds. The counting stopped them from rehearsing the letters, and the length of that delay was what varied. Recall got worse as the delay got longer.", "That is decay, meaning information fades from short-term memory over time unless it is rehearsed. In the Brown-Peterson task, recall dropped the longer the counting lasted, with rehearsal blocked. Peterson and Peterson (1959) took that as decay, since time passing was the thing that mattered and the counting task was too different from the letters to interfere."]}, {"module": 14, "topic": "Modality Effect", "questions": ["What is the modality effect?", "What was compared in the modality effect?", "What explanation do Neath and Surprenant (2003) favor?"], "remember": "Heard lists give better memory for the last items.", "answers": ["The modality effect is better memory for the last one or two items of a list when the list is heard than when it is read silently. It is about those final items, not the whole list. It also shows up for lists that are lip-read or silently mouthed, so it is not only about hearing.", "The comparison was between lists that were heard and lists that were read silently. People either heard or saw the digits one through nine in a random order and then gave them back in the order presented. Half the trials were auditory and half were visual, and the difference showed up in memory for the last items.", "Neath and Surprenant (2003) favor the idea that hearing provides an additional type of information compared with reading. When you recall the last items of a list, you do better if you also have information about how the list sounded. That extra sound information is what gives the heard lists their advantage at the end."]}, {"module": 14, "topic": "Partial Report", "questions": ["What did Sperling (1960) find with the cue right after the display?", "What was manipulated in partial report?", "Which concept is this an example of: a very brief store holding nearly all of a display?"], "remember": "The limit is reporting time because the sensory store fades fast.", "answers": ["Sperling (1960) found that when the cue came right after the display disappeared, observers reported the cued part almost perfectly, whichever part it was. That means a sensory, or iconic, store briefly held nearly a complete copy of the display. The usual small amount people report comes from the time needed to report, not from what they saw.", "What was manipulated was when the cue arrived. A grid of letters flashed very briefly, and then a tone told the observer which row to report, with the tone coming either right away or after a delay. With a delayed cue, performance fell back toward the usual limit, which shows how briefly the store lasts.", "That is the sensory, or iconic, store, a very brief store of raw, unanalyzed input. In Sperling's (1960) partial report, an immediate cue let people report the cued row almost perfectly, so the store must have held nearly all of the display. It fades very fast, which is why a delayed cue lost most of the advantage."]}, {"module": 14, "topic": "Position Error Effect", "questions": ["What is the position error effect?", "Where do misplaced items usually end up?", "Why does scoring only percent correct hide something?"], "remember": "Order errors are systematic, not random.", "answers": ["The position error effect is the finding that when we remember a list, order mistakes are systematic and not random. Most of the time an item is recalled in its correct position. When a mistake does happen, the item usually comes out close to where it belongs, so forgetting a position is not the same as losing the item.", "Misplaced items usually end up in an adjacent position. The third item is more likely to show up second or fourth than first or last, and rarely far from where it really belongs. So the errors are patterned, and people still remember roughly where each item went, even when they get the exact spot wrong.", "Percent correct alone makes forgetting look like lost information. A response that is one place off counts as simply wrong, even though the person still knows roughly where the item belonged. Looking at where each recalled item was placed shows that approximate position information is kept, and percent correct hides that."]}, {"module": 14, "topic": "Mental Rotation", "questions": ["What did Shepard and Metzler (1971) find about turning the shape?", "What was manipulated in the mental rotation task?", "Why do researchers use tasks that require imagery instead of self-reports?"], "remember": "Bigger turn, longer response: mental images are like real images.", "answers": ["Shepard and Metzler (1971) found that the larger the turn between two block shapes, the longer people took to respond, and the extra time grew at a constant rate. People were judging whether the shapes were the same or mirror images. Rotating a mental image takes time, just as turning a real object does, which suggests mental images are much like real images.", "What was manipulated was how much one block shape was turned relative to the other. The two shapes appeared side by side, either identical or mirror images, rotated by different angles. People judged same or different as quickly as possible, and their reaction time was recorded. Reaction time rose with the size of the turn.", "Descriptions of mental images are unreliable, so researchers cannot trust what people say about their images. Instead they use tasks that require imagery and vary one critical part of the task. That lets them work out objectively what images are like, which is what Shepard and Metzler (1971) did by varying how far one block shape was turned."]}, {"module": 14, "topic": "Typical Reasoning", "questions": ["What did Tversky and Kahneman (1983) show about probability estimates?", "What did Shafir, Smith, and Osherson (1990) find?", "Which concept is this an example of: judging A and B together as more likely than A alone?"], "remember": "A combination is never more likely than a part: the conjunction fallacy shows heuristics.", "answers": ["Tversky and Kahneman (1983) showed that people's estimates of probability often differ greatly from objective probability. A striking example is the conjunction fallacy, judging that A and B together are more likely than A alone or B alone. By the rules of probability a combination can never be more likely than either part alone, so these judgments point to heuristics, which trade accuracy for speed.", "Shafir, Smith and Osherson (1990) had people read short descriptions of a person and rate how likely it was that the person had certain professions or took part in certain activities. People sometimes rated the combination as more likely than one part of it. That points to reliance on heuristics rather than the rules of probability.", "That is the conjunction fallacy. Judging A and B together as more likely than A alone breaks the rules of probability, because a combination can never be more likely than either part on its own. It shows that people use heuristics, which are usually good enough day to day but can lead to incorrect judgments."]}, {"module": 14, "topic": "Wason Selection", "questions": ["Which two cards best test the rule 'If a card has a D on one side, it has a 3 on the other', and why?", "What did Wason and Shapiro (1971) compare?", "Why is the car and gas tank rule easier?"], "remember": "Look for what could falsify the rule; experience helps.", "answers": ["The best cards are D and 7, because those are the ones that could show the rule is broken. A D without a 3 would falsify the rule, and so would a 7 with a D behind it. Most people pick D and 3, but the 3 can only confirm the rule, since the rule says nothing about what must be behind a 3.", "Wason and Shapiro (1971) compared abstract rules, made of letters and numbers, with thematic rules, like borrowing a car. People saw a rule and four cards and chose the two cards to turn over to see if the rule was true. Thematic rules were much easier, while on the abstract rule most people chose D and 3 instead of D and 7.", "Because it uses familiar content, so people can draw on relevant experience. The right cards are the borrowed car and the empty gas tank, because those could reveal someone who borrowed the car and did not fill it. With an abstract rule, people have to rely solely on logic, and that is much harder."]}, {"module": 14, "topic": "Decision Making", "questions": ["What is a framing effect?", "What was manipulated in classic framing research?", "Which concept is this an example of: gain wording leading to the sure option?"], "remember": "Same choice, different wording, different decision.", "answers": ["A framing effect is when the perceived context or wording of a choice makes a big difference, even when the situations are otherwise equivalent. The same choice can be presented in two different ways, and people choose differently depending on the version. The choice follows the framing and not the substance.", "In classic framing research, as summarized by Kahneman and Tversky (1982), the same choice was presented in two different ways, with gain wording or with loss wording. People read situations and chose between two options, in one of two question sets that framed the same choices differently. Their choices depended on which wording they saw.", "That is a framing effect. Gain wording typically pushes people toward the sure option, while loss wording pushes them toward the gamble. The situations are otherwise equivalent, so the fact that the choice changes with the wording shows that decisions are shaped by how a choice is framed and not only by what the options are."]}, {"module": 14, "topic": "Monty Hall", "questions": ["Should you stay or switch in the Monty Hall problem, and why?", "What is wrong with thinking the two remaining doors are equal?", "What chance does the first pick keep?"], "remember": "Switching wins about two-thirds of the time.", "answers": ["You should switch, because switching wins about two-thirds of the time and staying wins only about one-third. Your first pick has a one in three chance of being right, so there is a two in three chance the prize is behind one of the other two doors. When the host opens an empty door, that whole two in three moves to the remaining door.", "The mistake is treating the two remaining doors as equally likely. It feels like a fifty-fifty choice, which is why many people think it does not matter whether they stay or switch. That intuition is incorrect, because your own door stays at one in three while the other door now carries two in three.", "The first pick keeps a one in three chance of being right. Opening a no-prize door does not change that, so your door stays at one in three. The other two in three chance is what moves to the remaining door, and that is why switching is the better choice."]}, {"module": 14, "topic": "Risky Decisions", "questions": ["What do people typically do with gains in risky choices?", "What do people typically do with losses?", "What was varied in the risky decisions task?"], "remember": "Gains lead to the sure thing; losses lead to the gamble.", "answers": ["With gains, people typically avoid risk and take the sure thing. When the choice is about something they stand to win, a guaranteed outcome is preferred over a gamble, even when the gamble has a stated chance of winning more. This is the gain side of how risky choices depend on how outcomes are perceived, not only on the objective odds.", "With losses, people typically seek risk and take the gamble. When the sure outcome is a loss, they are more willing to gamble than to accept it, hoping to avoid the loss altogether. So losses lead to gambling, not caution, and this is the loss side of how risky choices depend on how outcomes are perceived.", "What was varied was whether the guaranteed outcome was a gain or a loss, with a gamble as the alternative. People played a wheel-of-fortune game with a stated chance of winning and losing if they gambled, and a stated amount guaranteed if they did not. That showed risky decisions depend on how outcomes are perceived, not just on the objective odds."]}, {"module": 14, "topic": "German & Defeyter (2000): Immunity to functional fixedness in young children", "questions": ["What did German and Defeyter (2000) find about 5-year-olds?", "What was manipulated in German and Defeyter (2000)?", "Which concept is this an example of: being stuck on an object's usual function?"], "remember": "Younger children are more fluid, so one shown use did not lock the box in.", "answers": ["German and Defeyter (2000) found that 5-year-olds were immune to functional fixedness. They were not slowed by having seen the box as a container, and they were even faster than the older children in that condition. So the younger children did better here, not worse, which is the opposite of what you might expect since problem solving normally improves with age.", "German and Defeyter (2000) manipulated whether the box had first been shown as a container, which is preutilization. Children aged 5, 6 and 7 helped a toy bear reach a toy on a high shelf, and the solution was to turn the box over and use it as a support. In one condition the other objects started inside the box, and in the other they sat beside it. Time to offer the box solution was measured.", "That is functional fixedness, being stuck on an object's usual function, so that a new use is slow or missed. The 6- and 7-year-olds were slower to use the box as a support after seeing it as a container. The authors explained this by saying younger children have a more fluid idea of function, while older children hold it more rigidly and must restructure it."]}, {"module": 14, "topic": "Leist et al. (2022): Irrelevant speech impairs serial recall of verbal but not spatial items", "questions": ["What did Leist et al. (2022) find about spatial recall?", "What was manipulated in Leist et al. (2022)?", "Which concept is this an example of: speech that hurts recall of ordered items?"], "remember": "Ignored speech hurts verbal, not spatial, recall.", "answers": ["Leist and colleagues (2022) found that irrelevant speech did not impair spatial recall. It impaired verbal recall but not spatial recall, in both third-grade children and adults. So the effect is specific to verbal material. That is hard for the attention-capture and changing-state accounts, which predict disruption of spatial items too, and it fits similarity-based interference with phonological representations.", "Leist and colleagues (2022) varied the type of item, the speech people heard, and the age group. Items were either verbal, pictured objects, or spatial, dot locations. People heard quiet, a repeated syllable, which is steady-state, or different syllables, which is changing-state, and were told to ignore it. Third graders and adults were tested, and they had to recall the order of the items.", "That is the irrelevant speech effect, which is that speech you are told to ignore impairs serial recall. Changing-state speech, with different syllables, hurts more than steady-state speech, with one syllable repeated. Leist and colleagues (2022) found it happened for verbal items only, which points to interference with phonological representations."]}, {"module": 14, "topic": "Morrison & Richmond (2020): Offloading items from memory", "questions": ["What did Morrison and Richmond (2020) find about offloading and high loads?", "What was manipulated in Morrison and Richmond (2020)?", "Did memory ability predict who offloaded?"], "remember": "Offloading helps, mostly at high loads, across abilities.", "answers": ["Morrison and Richmond (2020) found that offloading improved memory, especially at high loads, meaning the longer lists. People also offloaded more as the load rose. So having pen and paper to write things down helped most exactly when the memory demands were highest, and people used it more when they needed it.", "Morrison and Richmond (2020) compared a no-choice block, where people relied on memory alone, with a choice block, where they had pen and paper and could write anything down if they wished. Students heard lists of letters of different lengths and said them back in order. They also completed working memory span tasks to measure their capacity.", "No, memory ability did not predict who offloaded, and capacity did not predict the benefit either. The researchers had expected people with lower working memory capacity to offload more. Instead, offloading may be a compensatory strategy that helps across a wide range of memory ability."]}, {"module": 14, "topic": "Hoover & Healy (2019): The Bat-and-Ball Problem", "questions": ["What did Hoover and Healy (2019) find about 'more than'?", "What was manipulated in Hoover and Healy (2019)?", "Why do they call it error sensitivity?"], "remember": "Wrong reasoners mostly remembered 'more than'; substitution is not confirmed.", "answers": ["Hoover and Healy (2019) found that incorrect reasoners mostly recalled and recognized the phrase more than, so it was not simply overlooked. They apparently did not use its meaning. Some reasoners did lose the phrase and many stayed very confident, so substitution may still occur for some people.", "Hoover and Healy (2019) compared a standard bat-and-ball problem containing more than with a control version without it. People answered each version and rated their confidence, and later were asked to recall the problem and recognize its wording. Simple math problems were placed in between as interference for most participants.", "Because they cannot be sure the error was unconscious attribute substitution, where people neglect more than and answer an easier question. Since the wrong reasoners mostly remembered the phrase, Hoover and Healy (2019) cannot confirm substitution as the cause. They prefer the term error sensitivity, which only says people notice they may be wrong."]}, {"module": 14, "topic": "Giuliani et al. (2023): The joint effect of framing and defaults on choice behavior", "questions": ["What did Giuliani et al. (2023) find about a risky default?", "What was manipulated in Giuliani et al. (2023)?", "What did the authors find about framing and default effects together?"], "remember": "Risky default raised risk taking; the two effects added up.", "answers": ["Giuliani and colleagues (2023) found that a risky default raised risk taking. A sure default, however, did not raise risk aversion. So a preselected option pushed people toward risk when the default was the gamble, but not toward caution when the default was the sure thing.", "Giuliani and colleagues (2023) varied the wording, gain or loss, and the default, which was none, a sure default or a risky default. Each person was randomly assigned to one of the conditions and chose in a life-or-death scenario and a money scenario involving their own money. They chose between a sure option and a risky one.", "Giuliani and colleagues (2023) found that the framing effect and the default effect were additive. They found a classic framing effect, and a risky default raised risk taking on top of it. Afterward, people who thought the default was the most rational choice followed it most, though that question came after the choice, so it may partly be a justification."]}, {"module": 14, "topic": "Chein et al. (2011) and Albert, Chein & Steinberg (2013): Peers and the adolescent brain", "questions": ["What did Chein et al. (2011) find when friends watched the driving game?", "What did the cognitive-control regions do with peers?", "What do Albert, Chein and Steinberg (2013) say raises adolescent risk taking?"], "remember": "The peer context works through reward circuitry.", "answers": ["Chein and colleagues (2011) found that adolescents took significantly more risks in the driving game when friends were watching. Reward-related regions, the ventral striatum and orbitofrontal cortex, were more active in adolescents when peers watched, and this went with risky decisions. The age-by-context difference on behavior was not itself significant in this small sample.", "The cognitive-control regions did not change with peers. Whether friends were watching or not, those regions stayed the same, so the peer context did not alter them. Chein and colleagues (2011) therefore concluded that the peer effect works mainly through reward circuitry, not through weaker reasoning, though the two systems may interact.", "Albert, Chein and Steinberg (2013) say it is the peer context. Adolescents reason about risk as well as adults in the lab, but with peers present they take more risks and adults do not. Peers prime the reward system while the cognitive-control system matures slowly, and that maturational gap raises risk taking."]}], "explainers": [{"module": 9, "title": "Mental multiplication: 43 times 6", "plain": "This shows storage and processing working together: you hold one number while you work on another.", "steps": ["3 x 6 = 18.", "Hold the 8 in memory and carry the 1 over the 4.", "6 x 4 = 24, then add the carried 1 to get 25.", "Combine the 25 with the held 8 to get 258."], "example": "Holding the 8 is storage; carrying the 1 and doing the next multiplication and addition is processing.", "watchOut": "Do not say working memory only holds numbers; the 8 must survive while other operations are done.", "explain": ["Start with what working memory is supposed to be. Baddeley and Hitch (1974) proposed it because the older picture of short-term memory was too simple. In that older picture, short-term memory was mostly a waiting room where information sat until it was used or lost. Working memory is a limited-capacity system for the temporary storage and manipulation of information for complex tasks such as comprehension, learning and reasoning. Storage keeps something on hand briefly, and manipulation works on it. The hard part to grasp is that both have to happen at the same moment and draw on one shared, limited capacity.", "Multiplying 43 times 6 in your head is a small, concrete case of exactly that. Walk through it slowly. First you picture the whole problem, 43 times 6, and keep it in mind so later steps can return to it. Then you multiply 3 by 6, which gives 18. Now comes the moment that matters: you hold the 8 in memory as the ones digit, and you carry the 1 over to the 4. Next you multiply 6 by 4 to get 24, and you add the carried 1 to get 25. Finally you combine that 25 with the 8 that has been held all along, and the answer is 258.", "Look at what each piece of that work is doing. Keeping the 8 alive while everything else goes on is storage. Carrying, multiplying and adding are active processes, because each one changes the information. The trap is to think working memory only holds things, as if it were a shelf. But the 8 has to last through several steps while other operations run, so the answer depends on storage and processing working together, which is exactly the job of working memory.", "That is why losing any stored piece breaks the answer. If the 8 slips away while you work on the tens, or if the carried 1 is never added, the result comes out wrong. So mental arithmetic is a direct test of working memory."], "another": ["Think of it like pushing a cart through a grocery store while adding up your bill in your head. You pick up a bag of rice at 6 dollars, then a jar of sauce at 4, and you say to yourself, ten so far. That running total is the thing you are holding on to. Then you pick up cheese at 5, and now you have to do something with the number you are holding: add 5 to it and replace it with 15.", "Notice that two jobs are going on at once. One job is keeping the total alive between aisles, and the other is working on it every time a new price shows up. If all you did was keep it, you would just be staring at a number. If all you did was add, you would have nothing to add to. And the two jobs share one small amount of mental room. That is why a friend calling out a question halfway down the aisle makes the total vanish. Your limited capacity got used for something else, and the held number slipped away.", "So when you explain the 43 times 6 problem to a friend, you can say it works the same way. The 8 is like the running total you keep carrying, and the multiplying and adding are like the new prices you keep folding in. Working memory is not a shelf where numbers sit. It is the shared workspace where you keep something and work on it at the very same time, and losing the kept piece ruins the answer."]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Tower of Hanoi with subgoals", "plain": "You cannot move the big disk until it is free and the target peg is free, so you set up those conditions first.", "steps": ["Goal: move the large disk to the third peg.", "Subgoal 1: free the large disk by moving the smallest disk to the third peg and the next disk to the middle peg.", "Subgoal 2: free the third peg by moving the smallest disk from the third peg onto the smaller stack on the middle peg.", "Subgoal 3: move the largest disk from the starting peg to the third peg.", "Then move the smaller disks onto it, never putting a larger disk on a smaller one."], "example": "The slide's example: the smallest disk goes to the third peg, the next disk to the middle peg, then the smallest goes onto the middle stack.", "watchOut": "Achieving a subgoal is not the finished puzzle; the smaller disks still have to reach the third peg.", "explain": ["Newell and Simon (1956) looked at problem solving as a search that happens between the posing of the problem and its solution. Every problem has an initial state, intermediate states along the way, and a goal state, and operators are the rules that say which moves are allowed and take you from one state to another. In the Tower of Hanoi, the stack of three disks starts on the first peg and must end in the same order on the third. Disks move one at a time, only when nothing is on top, and never onto a smaller disk.", "The goal is easy to picture, and that is what makes people go wrong. The stack cannot move all at once, so you cannot head straight for the goal. Smaller disks keep having to get out of the way, and the middle peg serves as a temporary parking spot. The moves explode as disks are added: with 64 disks it would take a trillion years, even at one correct move per second.", "Means-end analysis is the strategy that tames this. It aims to reduce the differences between the initial state and the goal state, and it relies on subgoals, which are smaller goals that create intermediate states closer to the goal. Here the main difference is that the whole stack is on the wrong peg, and the largest disk cannot move until the disks above it are cleared. So the first subgoal is to free the large disk: the smallest disk goes from the first peg to the third, and the middle disk goes from the first peg to the middle peg. The second subgoal is to free the third peg: the smallest disk moves from the third peg onto the middle disk, so the two smaller disks sit legally together on the middle peg. The third subgoal is to move the large disk onto the third peg, where it is in its final place.", "Notice that putting the smallest disk on the third peg and then taking it off looks wasteful. That detour is what frees the space the large disk needs, and means-end analysis accepts such detours when they remove an obstacle. Reaching the third subgoal is not finishing: the same strategy still has to move the two smaller disks onto the large one."], "another": ["Imagine you are moving a heavy piano into the back bedroom. The piano is in the living room, but a couple of chairs are stacked on top of it, and the back bedroom is full of boxes. You cannot just go straight at the goal, because the piano will not budge while it is buried and the room it is headed for is not clear.", "So you stop asking how to move the piano and start asking what is stopping you. First, the chairs have to come off, so you set them to the side in the hallway. Second, the bedroom has to be emptied, so the boxes get shoved temporarily into the hall too. Only then does the piano roll in, and that is the real prize. Along the way you did things that looked pointless, like lifting chairs just to put them down somewhere else, but each one removed a specific obstacle. And when the piano is in place, you are not done, because the chairs and boxes still have to be arranged around it.", "That is how you can tell a friend what subgoals do in the Tower of Hanoi. You look at the gap between where you are and where you want to be, you spot what blocks the biggest move, and you make clearing that block its own small goal. Parking a small disk somewhere temporarily is just like setting the chairs in the hall. Getting the big disk into place is the piano landing in the bedroom, and the rest of the work still follows."]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Mutilated checkerboard", "plain": "Each domino covers one square of each color, so the board must have equal numbers of the two colors.", "steps": ["A full board has 64 squares and takes 32 dominoes.", "Remove two opposite corners and 62 squares remain, matching 31 dominoes in area.", "Every domino covers one square of each color.", "Opposite corners are the same color, so one color now has fewer squares.", "Equal numbers are needed, so no covering is possible."], "example": "The bread-and-butter labels make it clear that each domino needs one bread and one butter.", "watchOut": "Equal area (62 squares, 31 dominoes) does not guarantee a legal covering.", "explain": ["A checkerboard has 64 squares and can be completely covered by 32 dominoes, with each domino covering 2 neighboring squares. The mutilated checkerboard problem removes two opposite corners and asks whether the remaining 62 squares can be covered with 31 dominoes. This is hard because the numbers seem to fit: 31 dominoes at two squares each cover exactly 62 squares. The arithmetic alone suggests a covering exists, so people start laying dominoes down one at a time, and that could take a very long time and could never prove that a covering is impossible.", "The answer is no, and the colors of the squares hold the key. Neighboring squares are always different colors, so every domino covers one dark square and one light square. Opposite corners share the same color, so removing them leaves 32 squares of one color and 30 of the other. Thirty-one dominoes would need 31 squares of each color, so a full covering is impossible. Once the board is represented by its colors, a single count settles the question before any domino is placed.", "The key, then, is understanding two things: each domino covers two squares, and those squares must be of different colors. Conditions in the experiment differed in how much information was given about the squares. Condition 1 was a blank board, condition 2 a colored board, condition 3 wrote the words black and pink on the squares, and condition 4 wrote the words bread and butter. The bread and butter condition emphasized the difference between the squares, since bread and butter naturally come in pairs, one of each, and participants solved it twice as fast as the blank condition. The board and dominoes were identical throughout, so only the representation changed.", "Think-aloud protocols show this happening. Participants get the problem after realizing that the words bread and butter are important; before that they talk about laying down dominoes, and afterward they talk about pairing the two kinds of squares. That is similar to the Gestalt idea of a representational shift. Equal area, 62 squares and 31 dominoes, is therefore never enough: the covering also needs equal numbers of the two colors."], "another": ["Picture a school dance with 62 students in the gym, and the teacher says every single student must dance in a pair made of one leader and one follower. It sounds fine at first, because 62 people can form 31 pairs. Without looking any closer, you might start matching up students and shuffling them around, trying pair after pair, and you could be at it all evening.", "Now suppose someone looks at the name tags and sees that 32 students are wearing leader tags and only 30 are wearing follower tags. The question is settled in a second. Each pair needs exactly one of each kind, so thirty-one pairs would call for thirty-one of each, and the room simply does not have that. No amount of shuffling could fix it, because the problem was never about the total head count. It was about the two kinds.", "This is the same move that makes the checkerboard click. The two removed corners are the same color, which is like sending home two followers and leaving the leaders short of partners. A domino is a pair that always needs one dark square and one light square, so once you label the squares by color and count, the answer appears before you place a single domino. You can tell your friend that the numbers matching up is not enough. What matters is whether the kinds match up, and the way you describe the squares is what lets you see that."]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Chain problem", "plain": "Treat a whole section of chain as the source of connectors instead of opening one link at a time.", "steps": ["Opening a link costs 2 cents and closing it costs 3 cents, so opening and closing one link costs 5 cents.", "The budget is 15 cents, so you can open and close three links.", "Open the three links of one three-link section.", "Use each opened link to join the other three sections into a closed loop, closing each once."], "example": "Four short sections become one closed loop for exactly 15 cents.", "watchOut": "Opening and closing one link costs 5 cents, so 15 cents allows only three links; use one whole section as the connectors.", "explain": ["The chain problem was one of the insight problems used by Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987), who designed an experiment to distinguish insight from noninsight problems. The triangle problem and the chain problem were the insight problems, and algebra problems were the noninsight ones. Participants gave warmth judgments every 15 seconds, rating how close they felt to the answer. Metcalfe and Wiebe expected that in insight problems people would not be very good at predicting how near they were to a solution, because if insight comes suddenly the solver should feel far away right until the answer arrives.", "Here is the problem. You have four short pieces of chain, each with three links, and you must join them into a single closed loop. Opening a link costs 2 cents, closing a link costs 3 cents, and you have only 15 cents to spend. The obvious plan is to open one link on each piece and use it to join the next piece. Work it out slowly: that is four links, each costing 2 to open and 3 to close, which is 20 cents, and that goes over budget. Opening and closing one link costs 5 cents, so 15 cents allows only three links, and the obvious plan needs four.", "The answer comes from seeing the parts differently. Open all three links of one piece, and use those three open links to join the other three pieces into a loop, closing each once. That costs 3 times (2 plus 3), which is exactly 15 cents. The shift is treating one whole piece of chain as a set of connectors instead of as a piece to be joined.", "Neither this answer nor the triangle answer comes from gradual improvement. Each requires leaving the obvious approach behind, and both problems look simple, which makes the obvious approach hard to give up. This is the kind of restructuring that Gestalt psychologists linked to insight. Steadily rising warmth would mean gradual progress, while low warmth followed by a sudden jump means insight, and that is the pattern these problems were chosen to produce."], "another": ["Say you are making one big closed bracelet loop out of four short strands of beads, and each strand ends in a little metal ring. A jeweler will open and close rings for you, but your budget only covers opening and closing three rings. The first idea that comes to mind is to open a ring on the end of every strand and hook each one to the next strand. That is four rings, and you can only afford three, so the plan fails when you count it up.", "The way out is to stop treating every strand the same. Most strands are things to be joined, but one strand can be sacrificed as a supply of parts. You take the three rings of a single strand, open each one once, and use them as connectors to link the other three strands into the loop. The strand you gave up stops being a strand with beads in the way you expected and becomes a bag of connectors, and the count now comes to exactly three.", "That is the whole trick behind the chain problem, and it is a good way to explain it aloud. Your first idea was to open one link on every piece, which costs too much. The better idea is to open all the links of a single piece and let it serve as the connectors for the others. Nothing about it comes from trying the first plan slightly harder. You have to change what you think of one piece as, and that sudden change in how you see the parts is what insight feels like."]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Triangle problem", "plain": "Move three dots from a ten-dot upward triangle so it points down, keeping seven in place.", "steps": ["Start with ten dots in four rows, with the point on top.", "Move the original top dot to a new bottom point beneath the center of the original bottom row.", "Move the two outer dots of the original bottom row to the outer ends of the original two-dot row.", "That row now has four dots, with three, two, and one below it."], "example": "Only three dots move and seven stay put, giving a downward-pointing triangle.", "watchOut": "", "explain": ["The triangle problem is the second of the insight problems used by Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987). They compared insight problems, the triangle and chain problems, with noninsight algebra problems, and they asked participants for warmth judgments every 15 seconds, rating from cold to hot how close they felt to the answer. Their expectation was that with noninsight problems, which involve a more methodical process, people know how close they are because every finished step brings the answer visibly closer. With insight problems, people should not be very good at predicting how near they are, because the solution arrives suddenly.", "The task is to move three of the blue dots to make the triangle point to the bottom of the page. The triangle has ten dots arranged in rows of one, two, three and four, with the point on top, and the rule is that seven of the dots stay where they are while only three move. This is hard because the problem looks simple, which makes the obvious approach hard to give up, and the obvious approach does not work.", "Walk through the answer slowly. The top dot, the one at the point, goes below the middle of the bottom row. The two ends of the bottom row go up beside the row of two dots. Count what you have now. The row that held two dots now has four, and below it sit the rows of three, two and one. The rows run four, three, two and one, so the triangle points down, and only three dots moved.", "Nothing about this comes from gradual improvement. You cannot get nearer by small adjustments; you have to give up the picture of an upward triangle and see the dots as parts that can be regrouped. That is the restructuring that Gestalt psychologists linked to insight, and it explains why warmth stays low and then jumps instead of rising steadily."], "another": ["Imagine a coach arranges a team photo with players standing in rows of one, two, three and four, ten players in all, narrow at the front and wide at the back, so the formation points forward. Now the coach says to flip it so it points the other way, but only three players are allowed to move and the other seven must stay put. The natural instinct is to nudge everybody a little, one step this way, one step that way, hoping it slowly turns into the new shape. It never gets there, because small nudges keep the old shape intact.", "What works is to stop looking at it as a fixed shape and start seeing it as a set of people who can be regrouped. Most of the team can stay where they are, because they already form part of a row that will still be needed. The few who move are the ones who were forming the old point and the old base. They go to the opposite end, and the row that was tiny becomes the wide one, while the wide one becomes tiny.", "This is the same shift as in the triangle problem, and you can tell a friend that is why it feels sudden. Until you let go of the picture of the formation pointing one way, you do not feel any closer, even though you are working hard. Then the regrouping appears all at once, and you jump from feeling cold to knowing you have it. That jump, rather than steady progress, is the signature of insight."]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Water-jug problems and mental set", "plain": "A rule that worked on early problems can stop you seeing a shorter route later.", "steps": ["Early problems use B − A − 2C, for example 127 − 21 − 3 − 3 = 100.", "Problem 7 (A 15, B 39, C 3, goal 18): the familiar way is 39 − 15 − 6 = 18, but A + C gives 18 directly.", "Problem 8 (A 28, B 59, C 3, goal 25): A − C gives 25 directly, although B − A − 2C also works."], "example": "Problem 8: 28 − 3 = 25 is simpler than the familiar method.", "watchOut": "The familiar method still works in problems 7 and 8; the issue is that it blocks the simpler one.", "explain": ["A mental set is a preconceived notion about how to approach a problem, based on a person's past experiences with the problem or with similar problems. It is a broader form of fixation, and the idea is that a method that worked before gets used again, even when a better method is available. The water-jug problem reveals how a mental set works. The task is to figure out how to obtain a required volume of water, given three empty jars for measuring. Each problem gives the capacities of jugs A, B and C and a final desired quantity, in quarts, and only filling, pouring and emptying are allowed.", "Take problem 1, where the jugs hold 21, 127 and 3 quarts and the target is 100. Fill jug B with 127 quarts and pour from B into A until A is full, which leaves 106 quarts in B. Then pour from B into C until C is full, empty C and fill it once more, which leaves exactly 100 quarts in B. So the formula is B minus A minus twice C, which is 127 minus 21 minus 2 times 3, or 100. The same formula solves each of the early problems.", "The trouble is what that success does to you. Participants started applying the formula automatically, and that automatic habit is the mental set. It forms because the formula keeps working. Then the later problems test the habit. Problem 7 has jugs of 15, 39 and 3 quarts and a target of 18. The long formula still works, giving 39 minus 15 minus 6, but simply adding A and C, 15 plus 3, gives 18 directly. Problem 8 has jugs of 28, 59 and 3 and a target of 25, which A minus C solves directly, 28 minus 3, even though the familiar formula also works.", "What the experiment shows is that the mental set inhibited participants from using the simpler solutions, and people who had not worked through the earlier problems found the short methods far more often. A set speeds up solving when problems are alike, but once the problems change, the same set hides the easier paths. Past success can therefore become an obstacle in its own right. The familiar method is not wrong in problems 7 and 8; it is just that it blocks the simpler one."], "another": ["Think about walking to class. For weeks you have taken the main road, and it has never failed you. You stop even thinking about it, and your feet just take you there. Then one morning a new footpath opens through the park, and it would get you there in half the time. But you are on autopilot, and you walk right past the entrance without noticing it.", "What is interesting is that the main road did not stop working. It still gets you to class every time. The trouble is that because it worked so many times, you stopped looking around for anything else. Your past success made one way feel like the only way, so the shorter way was sitting in plain view and you missed it. Someone new to campus, with no habit, would probably spot the footpath at once.", "That is what a mental set is, and it is how you can explain the water-jug problems to a friend. The early problems all got solved by the same longer recipe, so people kept running it by habit. When later problems arrived where a quick one-step answer was available, the old recipe still worked, but it kept them from noticing the easier route. A habit that serves you well when problems look alike can quietly get in the way once the problems change."]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Pairing problems: marriage and checkerboard", "plain": "Count the two kinds of pieces that must pair up. If the counts differ, a full pairing is impossible.", "steps": ["Find what each pair must contain (one man and one woman, or one square of each color).", "Count each kind.", "Compare the counts, not the total.", "If unequal, some are left over, however you rearrange."], "example": "30 men and 32 women give at most 30 marriages. Removing two same-colored corners leaves unequal colors, so 31 dominoes cannot cover the board.", "watchOut": "Do not use total size (62 divided by 2 = 31).", "explain": ["Start with the checkerboard, because it is the problem that tricks people. A checkerboard has 64 squares, and 32 dominoes, each covering two neighboring squares, cover it completely. Now cut off two opposite corners. That leaves 62 squares, and 31 dominoes cover 62 squares, so the numbers seem to fit and people start laying dominoes down. But no number of failed attempts would ever prove a covering impossible.", "The way out is to change what you pay attention to, and the colors are the key. Neighboring squares are always different colors, so every domino, wherever you put it, covers exactly one dark square and one light square. Opposite corners are the same color, so removing them takes away two squares of one color. What remains is 32 squares of one color and 30 of the other. Thirty-one dominoes would need 31 squares of each color, and that is simply not there.", "The Russian marriage problem shows the same structure plainly. A village has 32 bachelors and 32 unmarried women, and the matchmaker has arranged 32 marriages. Then two bachelors die, and the question is whether she can arrange 31 marriages among the 62 survivors. It sounds possible until you notice that every marriage pairs one man with one woman. There are now 30 men and 32 women, so at most 30 marriages are possible and two women are left over. The smaller group sets the limit.", "Now map one problem onto the other. Men and women are the two colors of squares, marriages are dominoes, and the two dead bachelors are the two missing corners. Rearranging cannot help, because the imbalance rules out the last pair before any piece is placed. This is analogical transfer, with the marriage story as the source problem and the checkerboard as the target. Presentation matters too: when squares were labeled bread and butter, which come in pairs, participants solved it twice as fast as with a blank board."], "another": ["Think of a shoe store that sells shoes only as matched pairs, one left and one right. Someone wheels in a cart with 30 left shoes and 32 right shoes and asks how many pairs you can sell. You would not say 31 just because there are 62 shoes in all. A pair needs one of each kind, so you count the lefts and the rights separately, and the smaller pile decides. Thirty pairs, and two right shoes stay on the cart no matter how you shuffle them around.", "What makes this feel so obvious in the shoe store is that you instinctively look at the two kinds instead of the total. Nobody tries to build a pair out of two left shoes, so the only question is whether one kind runs out first. The moment you see that one kind runs out, you can stop trying, because there is nothing left to try. Rearranging, pairing in a different order, or starting from the other end cannot create a right shoe that is not there.", "Now say it back the way you would to a friend. Left and right shoes are the two colors of squares, or the men and the women. A pair is a domino, or a marriage. The total number of pieces tells you nothing, because every pair must use one of each kind, and when the counts are unequal some pieces are left over however cleverly you arrange them. The whole trick is asking what each pair must contain and then counting those things."]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Converging solution (radiation, fortress, lightbulb)", "plain": "Split a strong effect into weak parts that travel separately and meet at the target.", "steps": ["Find the target that needs a strong effect.", "Find the limit on the paths (healthy tissue, mines, fragile glass).", "Use several weak approaches from different directions.", "Make them arrive together."], "example": "Several low-intensity rays hit the tumor at once. Each path is safe, and the combined intensity destroys it.", "watchOut": "Dividing without combining at the same time does not work.", "explain": ["Duncker's radiation problem sets two demands against each other. A patient has an inoperable malignant tumor in his stomach. A kind of ray can destroy the tumor if it arrives all at once at a high enough intensity. Unfortunately, at that intensity the healthy tissue the rays pass through on the way will be destroyed too. At lower intensities the rays are harmless to the tissue, but they do nothing to the tumor either. The tumor needs high intensity and the tissue needs low intensity, so no single ray can satisfy both. Only 10% of participants reach the answer without help.", "The answer is to send several low-intensity rays from different directions so that they converge on the tumor at the same time. Each ray is weak enough to pass through healthy tissue safely, and where they meet, their combined strength destroys the tumor.", "The fortress problem has the same shape. A rebel general knows his whole army could take a dictator's fortress, but the roads are mined so that only small groups pass safely, and a large force would blow up the road and the villages around it. He divides the army into small groups, sends them along different roads, and has them arrive at the same time. The mines play the healthy tissue, the small groups are the weak rays, and the fortress is the tumor.", "This shows how hard it is to notice a good analogy. After the fortress story, only 30% solved the radiation problem, against 10% without it. Gick and Holyoak described three steps: noticing the analogy, which is the hardest, mapping the parts, and applying the mapping. When told to think about the story, success rose to 75%. The lightbulb problem, where a strong laser would break fragile glass around a broken filament, looks more like the radiation problem, and 81% of participants who knew the radiation problem solved it, against 10% of the control group."], "another": ["Imagine a chef who wants to melt and brown the sugar on one spot of a dessert, but the dish and the table underneath are delicate and would scorch under a hot flame. One strong burner aimed at the spot would do the job at the target and ruin everything it passes over. One gentle warming lamp keeps everything safe but never browns anything. The chef seems stuck, because the target needs lots of heat and the surroundings can take only a little.", "The way out is to stop asking one source to do everything. Set up several gentle lamps around the dessert, each aimed at the same spot from a different side. Anywhere along the way, only one lamp is shining, so the dish and the table feel something mild. At the one spot where all the beams cross, the warmth of every lamp adds up, and there it is strong enough to brown the sugar. The strong effect exists only where the weak parts meet.", "So you could explain it like this. When something needs a strong effect in one place and a weak effect everywhere else, split the strength into several weak pieces that travel along separate paths, and make sure they all point at the same spot together. If you aim only one lamp at it, you are back to a single gentle source and nothing browns. The target is the dessert spot, the weak pieces are the lamps, and the safe surroundings are the healthy tissue or the mined roads."]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Base rates and Jack", "plain": "Start with how common each category is, then adjust for the description, instead of ignoring the numbers.", "steps": ["Write the base rate (70 lawyers, 30 engineers).", "With no description, the chance of an engineer is 30 out of 100.", "With a description, remember the group still has more lawyers.", "Do not decide on fit alone."], "example": "Jack is conservative, careful, and enjoys home carpentry and math puzzles. People say engineer, yet the group is 70 lawyers.", "watchOut": "A good-fitting description does not settle the probability.", "explain": ["A base rate is simply how common each category is in the population. The idea behind this topic is that when you judge what category someone belongs to, the starting point should be those numbers, and the description should adjust them, not replace them. This is hard because a description is vivid and a base rate is a dry number, so the description tends to push the number out of the judgment completely.", "Walk through the setup slowly. A group has 100 people, 70 lawyers and 30 engineers, and one person is picked at random. With nothing else known, the chance of an engineer is 30%, and people get this right. People use base rate information if it is all that is available, but when a description is available they disregard the base rate.", "Now add Jack. He is a 45-year-old man, married with four children, generally conservative, careful and ambitious, and uninterested in political and social issues. He spends most of his free time on hobbies, including home carpentry, sailing and mathematical puzzles. People say that Jack is an engineer, because his careful nature and love of puzzles fit the engineer stereotype, and that fit outweighs the base rate. But look at what the description actually rules out. Nothing in it rules out a lawyer. A lawyer could be careful and conservative and enjoy puzzles, and the group still contains many more lawyers than engineers.", "The right idea is to keep both pieces of information in play. The 70 and 30 are still true after you read about Jack, so the description has to be weighed against them and cannot settle the question by itself. This is the representativeness heuristic at work, judging class membership by resemblance. The same happens with Robert, who wears glasses, speaks quietly and reads a lot: people say librarian and ignore that America has about 2.2 million farms and 120,000 libraries."], "another": ["Picture a party of 100 people where 90 belong to the chess club and 10 play on the basketball team. You are told that one person, picked at random, is tall, quick on their feet and loves jumping. Almost everyone would guess basketball, because the description sounds like a basketball player. But stop and look at the room. There are 90 chess club members, and with that many people, plenty of them are tall, quick and love jumping too.", "Before you heard anything about the person, the chance of basketball was 10 out of 100. That starting point does not vanish when the description arrives. The description should push your guess up from there, maybe quite a lot, but it is pushing from a low starting point, so it has to be very convincing to end up above even. A good fit tells you that basketball is more likely than it was a minute ago. It does not tell you that basketball is more likely than chess club.", "To say it aloud, the numbers about the crowd tell you where to begin, and the description tells you how far to move from there. The mistake is letting the vivid description replace the dull numbers entirely, as if the room only contained the type of person who was described. Jack is the same case with the groups reversed, since a careful, puzzle-loving man still has to be weighed against a group made mostly of lawyers."]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Conjunction rule (Linda)", "plain": "Adding a condition can only shrink the set, so a combination is never more likely than one part.", "steps": ["Name the two events (bank teller, feminist bank teller).", "See that feminist bank tellers are inside bank tellers.", "The smaller set cannot be more probable."], "example": "About 82% chose feminist bank teller, but bank teller alone must be at least as probable.", "watchOut": "A more detailed story feels more likely but is not.", "explain": ["The conjunction rule says that the probability of two events occurring together cannot be higher than the probability of either single event alone. A conjunction just means two things being true at once. The rule is easy to state and surprisingly easy to break, because a more detailed story feels more likely even though it is less likely. Each added detail narrows the possibilities, and narrower things are less probable.", "Here is the problem. Linda is described as 31 years old, single, outspoken and very bright, and she majored in philosophy. As a student she was deeply concerned with issues of discrimination and social justice, and she also took part in antinuclear demonstrations. People are asked which is more probable: that Linda is a bank teller, or that Linda is a bank teller who is active in the feminist movement. Because Linda sounds like a feminist, the second option feels more like her.", "Now reason about the groups rather than about Linda's personality. Picture everyone who is a bank teller. Some of those people are active in the feminist movement and many are not. The feminist bank tellers are a smaller group sitting inside the larger group of all bank tellers. Anyone who belongs to the smaller group also belongs to the larger one, so the larger group can never have fewer members, and a group contained inside another can never be more probable than the one containing it. Bank teller alone must be at least as probable as feminist bank teller.", "Eighty-two percent picked the second option, which breaks the rule. The sense of fit overrides the logic, which is the representativeness heuristic producing an error. Linda resembles a feminist, so people judge the resembling option as more likely, and they stop thinking about the sizes of the categories. The lesson is that how well a story fits a person tells you nothing about whether a combination can be more probable than one of its parts."], "another": ["Suppose a friend texts you that she is somewhere in Illinois. Later she texts that she is in a coffee shop in Chicago. Which of those two statements is more likely to be true, given only that you know she loves coffee and big cities? The second one sounds more like her, but it cannot be the more likely one. If she is in a Chicago coffee shop, then she is in Illinois, so the second statement is true only when the first one is.", "Think about it as nested regions on a map. Illinois is a large area, Chicago sits inside it, and a particular coffee shop sits inside Chicago. Each time you add a detail you draw a smaller region, and a smaller region can hold fewer of the possible places she might be. She could be in Illinois in a hundred ways that have nothing to do with Chicago, such as a small town or a farm, and all of those count toward the first statement but not the second.", "So when you explain it to a friend, say that every extra detail is like zooming in on the map. A detailed story can feel more believable because it paints a clearer picture, but it can only be equally likely or less likely than the broader version it lives inside. That is exactly the Linda situation: bank teller is the big region, feminist bank teller is the smaller region inside it, and fit with her personality cannot make the inside region larger than the one around it."]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Sample size (hospital question)", "plain": "Small samples swing more, so extreme proportions happen more often in them.", "steps": ["Note the sample sizes (about 15 versus 45 births a day).", "Note the true rate is about 50% boys in both.", "Ask which sample can stray more from 50%.", "Pick the smaller one."], "example": "The smaller hospital has more days over 60% boys. 56% wrongly said about the same.", "watchOut": "Do not confuse total boys with the share of days above 60%.", "explain": ["This problem tests whether you notice how much information a sample contains. A town has two hospitals. In the larger one about 45 babies are born each day, and in the smaller one about 15. About 50% of all babies are boys, but the exact percentage varies from day to day, sometimes higher and sometimes lower. For one year, each hospital recorded the days on which more than 60% of the babies born were boys. Which hospital recorded more such days: the larger, the smaller, or about the same?", "Most people get this wrong. Fifty-six percent said about the same, and the larger and the smaller hospital were each chosen by only 22%. The reasoning behind that answer is that both hospitals have the same true rate of about 50% boys, so both should have unusual days equally often. They fix on the 50% that applies to both and overlook that the question is about day-to-day variation, which depends on how many babies are born each day as well as on the average rate.", "Think it through slowly. With about 15 births in a day, chance can easily tilt a day heavily toward boys, so a day with more than 60% boys happens fairly often. With about 45 births, chance variations even out over more births, and the share of boys stays nearer 50%. So the smaller hospital records many more days over 60% boys, and the correct answer is the smaller hospital, which only 22% chose.", "The law of large numbers states that the larger the number of individuals randomly drawn from a population, the more representative the resulting group will be of the entire population. A small random sample can be thrown off by a few unusual cases, so it may differ a lot from the population. People often assume that representativeness holds for small samples, and this results in errors in reasoning. Those who answered about the same were expecting every sample to look like the population, which is the representativeness heuristic applied where sample size matters."], "another": ["Think of a basketball player who makes about half of her shots over a whole season. In one game she takes only six shots, and in another she takes sixty. In which game is she more likely to end up shooting better than 60%? Most of us feel she is a 50% shooter, so both games should look about the same. But a six-shot game needs just one or two lucky bounces to land well above half, and that happens in plenty of games.", "In the sixty-shot game, the lucky and unlucky shots keep canceling each other out. To finish above 60%, she would need to be luckier than usual for a long stretch, over and over, and that is rare. Her ability is the same in both games. What differs is how many chances there are for luck to even out, and a small number of chances lets luck dominate.", "Now you can tell it back like this. The true rate describes the long run, and it does not promise that every short stretch will look like it. The fewer attempts in a stretch, the more it can stray from the true rate in either direction, so extreme results show up much more often in small samples. The six-shot game is the small hospital with about 15 births a day, the sixty-shot game is the larger hospital, and the days above 60% are the unusually lucky games."]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Probability as a ratio (jellybeans)", "plain": "Chance of winning is winning items divided by total items, not the count of winning items.", "steps": ["Write each bowl as red out of total.", "Convert to a percentage.", "Compare the percentages."], "example": "1 out of 10 is 10%. 7 out of 100 is 7%. So the first bowl is better, although most picked the second.", "watchOut": "A bigger count of red beans hides the larger total.", "explain": ["This is a problem about what counts as your chance of winning. The idea is simple: chance is the number of winning items divided by the total number of items. It is not the number of winning items. The error comes from the fact that a count is easy to see and a proportion takes a little work, so people judge by what looks like more.", "The setup is the jellybean study. Participants could earn up to $7 by receiving $1 every time they drew a red jellybean, and each time they chose between two bowls. One bowl held 1 red jellybean out of 10, so the odds were 1 in 10. The other held 7 red jellybeans out of 100, so the odds were 7 in 100, or 0.7 in 10. The bowls differ in both the number of red beans and the proportion of red beans, and that is where the confusion starts.", "Do the arithmetic for each bowl. One in 10 is 10%, and 7 in 100 is 7%. So the first bowl gives the better chance of a red bean. Yet most participants chose the 7 in 100 bowl. The second bowl has more red beans, seven instead of one, so it seems to offer more chances, even though it also holds many more white beans that hide those reds.", "Their choice was influenced by considerations other than probabilities. People paid attention to the number of winning beans instead of their proportion, and by choosing by appearance instead of probability they lowered their real chance of winning. The way to avoid it is to write each bowl as red out of total, convert to a percentage, and compare the percentages, never the red counts."], "another": ["Say you are at a school fair choosing between two raffles. In the first, there are 20 tickets in the drum and 3 of them win. In the second, there are 100 tickets and 12 of them win. A lot of people lean toward the second drum because twelve winning tickets sounds far better than three. But your ticket is one single pull from the drum, and what matters is how crowded that drum is with losers.", "Work it out one drum at a time. Three winners out of 20 tickets is 15 out of every 100, which is 15%. Twelve winners out of 100 tickets is 12%. So the small drum with fewer winners is actually the better bet, because its winners make up a bigger share of what you might pull out. The big drum has more winners, but it also has many more losing tickets standing between you and them.", "To say this to a friend, explain that a count of winners only means something once you know how big the whole pile is. Always turn each option into winners out of total, convert both to percentages, and compare those. It is the same move as with the red jellybeans, where seven reds sounded better than one red but sat inside a much larger bowl."]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Expected utility", "plain": "Weigh how good each outcome is by how likely it is, then choose the best total.", "steps": ["List the alternatives.", "Rate the value of each outcome.", "Combine value with chance.", "Choose the maximum expected utility."], "example": "Casino gambling shows people choosing an activity despite knowing the odds are against them.", "watchOut": "People value things other than money, so the theory does not fully describe choices.", "explain": ["Decision making means making judgments that involve choices between different courses of action. To judge a choice you need a way of saying how good it is, and utility is the word for that. Utility refers to outcomes that are desirable because they are in the person's best interest, so an option with higher utility leads to a better outcome for that person. Expected utility theory builds on this by also bringing in chance.", "The theory begins from the claim that people are rational. It holds that if people have all the relevant information, they will make the decision that results in the maximum expected utility. Expected utility combines how good each outcome is with how likely it is. In its early form, utility meant maximum monetary payoff, so the best choice was the one that paid the most money on average.", "The procedure is clear. For each option, multiply the value of every possible outcome by its probability, add the products, and then pick the option with the largest total. That is why the approach has a real advantage: it offers specific procedures for determining the best choice and tells you what a fully rational decision maker would pick. It works as a benchmark, because you can compare real choices with its predictions.", "The comparison is where the theory shows its limits. Utility is not necessarily money, because people find value in other things, such as safety, fairness or how they feel about a choice. People are not utility maximization machines. People gamble in casinos all the time even though they know the odds are very much against them, because they value the thrill or the chance at a big win. Ritov and Baron (1999) found that 63% did not choose to send a convoy to the second refugee camp, although the utility of 900 more lives saved says to reroute, because actively causing harm weighs more than failing to prevent it. So the theory is a strong standard but a weak description of how people really choose."], "another": ["Consider deciding each morning whether to carry an umbrella. Suppose there is a 30% chance of rain. Carrying the umbrella costs you a small, certain annoyance, because it is one more thing in your hands. Leaving it at home costs you nothing if the sky stays dry, but a soaking and a ruined afternoon if it rains. You do not ask only whether it will rain. You ask how much each result would matter to you and how likely it is.", "If getting soaked bothers you a great deal, then even a 30% chance of it can outweigh the small nuisance of carrying the umbrella, so you carry it. If you are going to be indoors all day, the soaking matters much less and you leave it. Notice that nothing here is about money. The values are how good or bad each outcome feels to you, and the chances act as weights on those values. Add up the weighted outcomes for each choice and the larger total is the one the theory says to pick.", "When you explain it aloud, say that the theory is a way of weighing what you might get by how likely it is, and that what counts as good is whatever the person values, not just dollars. It tells you what a perfectly rational chooser would do, which makes it a handy yardstick. Real people do not always follow it, for example when how a choice is worded pulls them away from the larger total, which is why it is a standard to compare choices against and not a full description of them."]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Inductive evidence strength", "plain": "Inductive conclusions are probably true, and they are stronger with better observations.", "steps": ["Check how representative the observations are.", "Check how many there are.", "Check how good (compelling) each one is.", "Allow that one strong case can contradict a claim."], "example": "", "watchOut": "More observations is not the only factor. Quality counts.", "explain": ["Inductive reasoning is reasoning based on observations: you reach conclusions from evidence, going from particular cases to a general conclusion. Scientists do this deliberately, making observations, collecting data and proposing a general rule that fits the many cases they have seen. Ordinary people do it constantly too, predicting what will happen from what has happened in the past. The important thing to learn is that the conclusion is only probably true, never definitely true, so the real question is always how strong the support is.", "Three things decide the strength of an inductive argument. The first is the representativeness of the observations, meaning how well they stand for the whole category. Many observations from a narrow group can still lead to a wrong conclusion, so a big pile of evidence that all comes from one corner of a category does not help much. The second is the number of observations, because more observations give stronger support.", "The third is the quality of the observations. Careful and accurate observations count for more, and sometimes one very compelling, high-quality observation can carry a lot of weight. People often miss this one because they assume strength is just a matter of counting cases. But look at what an inductive conclusion is. It is a general claim built from particular cases, so a single clear counterexample can prove it false, however many cases supported it before.", "That is why conclusions reached this way are always open to revision. The conclusions are suggested with various degrees of certainty, and a new observation can always overturn a conclusion drawn from earlier ones. The right way to judge an inductive argument is therefore to check how representative the observations are, how many there are, and how good each one is, and to remember that quality can outweigh quantity."], "another": ["Say you are deciding whether a new pizza place is any good, and you are reading reviews. The conclusion you are heading toward is something like all their pizzas are great. You can never be completely sure of that, because it is a general claim built from the few pizzas other people happened to try. So the real question is how well the reviews support it, not whether they prove it.", "First, who wrote the reviews? If every review comes from people who ordered the same cheese pizza on Friday nights, a hundred of them still tell you little about the rest of the menu, because they do not stand for the whole category. Second, how many are there? Twenty reviews support the conclusion better than two. Third, how good are they? One detailed review from someone who tried many dishes, described them carefully and sounded trustworthy can count for more than a pile of one-line ratings.", "Then there is the catch you can explain to a friend. A general claim like all their pizzas are great can be knocked down by one clear case, such as one soggy, burnt pizza from that kitchen, however many happy reviews came before. That is why the strength of an inductive argument depends on how representative, how many and how good the observations are, and why the conclusion always stays open to revision."]}, {"module": 13, "title": "What a syllogism is and its parts", "plain": "A syllogism is the basic form of deductive reasoning, introduced by Aristotle. It has two premises and a conclusion.", "steps": ["1. Find the two premises: the statements the reasoning starts from.", "2. Find the conclusion: the third statement, what you arrive at.", "3. Ask only one thing: does the conclusion follow from the two premises?"], "example": "Premise 1: All birds are animals. Premise 2: All animals eat food. Conclusion: Therefore, all birds eat food. Birds are inside animals, animals are inside things that eat food, so birds are inside that group too.", "watchOut": "A categorical syllogism uses all, no, or some about two categories. Do not mix it up with the conditional syllogism, which starts with If p, then q.", "explain": ["Start with the word deductive. Deductive reasoning means working out whether a conclusion logically follows from some premises, where a premise is simply a statement that your reasoning starts from. Notice how narrow that job is. You are not asking whether the conclusion is likely, or whether you happen to believe it. You are asking only whether it follows, and the answer is either yes or no, with nothing in between.", "A syllogism is the basic form of this kind of reasoning, and it was introduced by Aristotle. It always has the same three-part shape: two statements called premises, and then a third statement called the conclusion, which is where the reasoning arrives. So the first thing to do with any syllogism is just to sort the statements: these two are what I am given, and this last one is what I am being asked to accept.", "The kind you meet first is the categorical syllogism, which describes how two categories relate to each other using the words all, no or some. Take the premises All birds are animals, and All animals eat food. The conclusion is Therefore, all birds eat food. The way to see why this works is to picture the categories as circles. The circle of birds sits completely inside the circle of animals, and the circle of animals sits completely inside the circle of things that eat food. If the small circle is inside the middle one and the middle one is inside the big one, then the small circle has to be inside the big one too. So every bird is inside the group of food eaters, and the conclusion follows. It follows because of how the categories fit inside one another, and that fit is the whole point.", "People mix this up with the conditional syllogism, which begins with If p, then q instead of all, no or some. For now, keep the habit simple. Find the two premises, find the conclusion, and ask only one question: given these two premises, does the conclusion have to follow?"], "another": ["Here is another way to see it. Think of a syllogism as a short chain of three links, where the first two are things you are simply handed and the third is the thing you are asked to accept. Say I tell you that all poodles are dogs, and that all dogs are mammals. Those two statements are the premises, the starting points. Then I say, so all poodles are mammals. That last sentence is the conclusion, the place the reasoning lands.", "Why does the landing hold? Imagine nesting boxes. The poodles fit in a box labeled dogs, and the dogs box fits inside a bigger box labeled mammals. If one box sits inside a second, and the second sits inside a third, then the first is automatically inside the third. You never had to inspect a single poodle. The conclusion follows purely from how the boxes sit inside each other, and that is what deductive means: if the premises are true, the conclusion has no choice.", "So if a friend asks what a syllogism is, you can say it is two given statements about categories, using words like all, no or some, plus a third statement that either has to follow from them or does not. Your whole job is to sort the three statements and ask whether the last one is forced. That is different from the if-then kind, which starts from a condition instead of from categories."]}, {"module": 13, "title": "How to check validity, step by step", "plain": "There is no easy method, so picture the situation the premises describe and try to break the conclusion (the mental model approach).", "steps": ["1. Build a picture (model) of the situation the two premises describe.", "2. Write a tentative conclusion, one that might hold.", "3. Look for an exception: a case where the premises are true but the conclusion is not.", "4. If you find one exception, the syllogism is not valid. If no model of the premises refutes the conclusion, it is valid."], "example": "All of the students are tired; Some tired people are irritable; so Some of the students are irritable. Everything sounds reasonable, but the conclusion does not follow from the premises, so the syllogism is not valid.", "watchOut": "Do not stop at the first model that fits. A conclusion is valid only if NO model of the premises refutes it.", "explain": ["Here is the uncomfortable part: there is no easy method for deciding whether a syllogism is valid. You cannot just glance at it and know. What people do instead is picture the situation the premises describe, and that is the idea behind the mental model approach of Johnson-Laird (1999). A mental model is one specific, concrete situation held in your mind that fits the premises, one way the premises could be true.", "Once you have that picture, you move through it in a particular order. You build a model of the situation. Then you write down a tentative conclusion, meaning a conclusion that might hold in that picture. Then comes the step people skip, which is to hunt for an exception, a way the premises could still be true while the conclusion is false. Finally you decide. If you can find even one model where the premises are true and the conclusion is not, the conclusion has been refuted, and the syllogism is not valid. It is valid only if no model of the premises can refute the conclusion.", "Walk through the students case slowly. The premises are All of the students are tired, and Some tired people are irritable. The conclusion offered is Some of the students are irritable. Picture a room of tired people. Every student is in it, because all the students are tired. But there are also tired people who are not students, and the second premise only says that some tired people are irritable. It does not say which ones. So imagine the irritable ones are all non-students. Every student is tired, some tired people are irritable, both premises are true, and yet no student is irritable. That is an exception, and one exception is enough.", "This is why everything in this syllogism can sound reasonable and the syllogism is still not valid. The word some leaves open which tired people are irritable, and being tired alone does not put any student among them. The danger is stopping at the first picture that happens to fit, where the irritable people are students. A tentative conclusion often fits the first model you build, so you have to deliberately try other ways the premises could be true before you trust it."], "another": ["Here is another way to see it. Suppose I tell you that all of my cats are hungry, and that some hungry animals are noisy, and then I conclude that some of my cats are noisy. It sounds fine. So do what a careful person does and try to build a scene where both premises are true and the conclusion is false. Put three animals in my kitchen, two cats and a dog, all hungry, and let the dog be the only noisy one. All my cats are hungry, yes. Some hungry animals are noisy, yes, the dog. But no cat is noisy.", "That one scene is the whole proof that the conclusion does not have to follow, because the word some never said which hungry animals make the noise. Now change the second premise to all hungry animals are noisy, and try to break the conclusion that all my cats are noisy. Every cat is hungry and every hungry animal is noisy, so there is no scene that keeps the premises and escapes the conclusion. That is what valid feels like: you hunt hard and every attempt fails.", "So when you explain it aloud, say that you picture a situation, guess a conclusion, and then try to wreck the guess by building a different situation that still fits the premises. One scene that wrecks it is enough to call it not valid. Only if every scene you can build keeps the conclusion standing is it valid."]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Validity versus truth", "plain": "Valid means the conclusion follows from the premises. True means the statement matches the world. They are different.", "steps": ["1. Ignore whether the statements sound true.", "2. Check only the form: does the conclusion follow from the two premises?", "3. Then separately ask whether each statement is actually true.", "4. Remember: valid plus true premises forces a true conclusion; valid with a false premise can give a false conclusion."], "example": "All birds are animals; All animals have four legs; All birds have four legs. It has the same form as the first example, so it is valid. The conclusion is false only because premise 2 is false.", "watchOut": "A syllogism can be invalid even if every statement and the conclusion seem reasonable (the students example). Valid does not guarantee a true conclusion.", "explain": ["Two words get used as if they meant the same thing, and they do not. Valid describes the reasoning: a syllogism is valid if its conclusion follows logically from its two premises. True describes a statement and the world: does it match how things actually are? Validity depends on the form of the argument, while truth depends on whether the statements are correct. Validity should not be confused with truth, and that confusion is exactly what makes it hard to judge whether reasoning is logical.", "The cleanest way to see the difference is to put two syllogisms side by side. The first is All birds are animals, All animals eat food, so all birds eat food. The second is All birds are animals, All animals have four legs, so all birds have four legs. Read them out loud and compare their shapes. They are built in exactly the same way, birds inside animals and animals inside a further group. Since the first has that shape and the conclusion follows, the second does too. Both are valid.", "What differs is the premises. In the first, both premises are true, so the conclusion is true as well. In the second, the claim that all animals have four legs is false, and that single false premise is the only reason its conclusion is false. So here is the rule that ties it together: if a syllogism is valid and its premises are true, the conclusion must be true. But a valid form fed a false premise can still deliver a false conclusion. Validity on its own does not guarantee a true conclusion.", "Truth and validity can come apart in the other direction too. In the students example, every statement sounds reasonable, yet the syllogism is not valid because the conclusion does not follow. So when you check a syllogism, do it in two separate passes. First ignore whether anything sounds true and ask only whether the conclusion follows from the premises. Then, separately, ask whether the statements are true. Keeping those two passes apart is the skill."], "another": ["Here is another way to see it. Think of validity as how well a machine is built, and truth as what you happen to feed into it. Imagine a machine designed so that when you give it all X are Y and all Y are Z, it hands back all X are Z. That machine works every time, whatever you put in. Now feed it silly things: all the pets in my house are robots, and all robots run on batteries. Out comes, all the pets in my house run on batteries. The machine did its job, so the reasoning is valid, but the conclusion is false, because my pets are not robots. The bad input is to blame, not the machine.", "Feed the same machine true things: all poodles are dogs, and all dogs are mammals, so all poodles are mammals. Same machine, true inputs, true output. That is the promise of validity: a good machine plus true premises gives a true conclusion, and nothing beyond that.", "Now picture a badly built machine. All dogs are animals, and some animals are pets, so some dogs are pets. Every sentence there happens to be true, even the conclusion, and still the reasoning is not valid, because you can picture a world where the only pets are hamsters. The premises would still hold and the conclusion would fail. A true ending does not rescue weak reasoning. Tell your friend that validity asks whether the conclusion has to follow, truth asks whether it matches the world, and you answer those two questions separately."]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Belief bias", "plain": "Belief bias is thinking a syllogism is valid just because its conclusion is believable.", "steps": ["1. Notice if your gut says the conclusion is true.", "2. Set that feeling aside.", "3. Check whether the conclusion follows from the premises (look for an exception).", "4. Judge validity from that, not from belief."], "example": "71% of participants judged invalid syllogisms with believable conclusions as valid. All of the students are tired; Some tired people are irritable; Some of the students are irritable sounds right but is not valid.", "watchOut": "Good reasoning and truth are not the same thing; do not accept reasoning just because the ending sounds true.", "explain": ["Belief bias is a very human mistake. It is the tendency to think a syllogism is valid when its conclusion is believable. You read the ending, it matches what you already think, it feels right, and so you wave the whole argument through without checking whether it actually earns that ending.", "The number shows how common this is: 71% of participants judged invalid syllogisms with believable conclusions as valid. That is most people. So if you catch yourself saying that one sounds right, treat that feeling as a warning rather than as evidence. Good reasoning and truth are not the same thing, and people make many errors in evaluation because of this.", "Go back to the syllogism that fools people. The premises are All of the students are tired, and Some tired people are irritable. The conclusion is Some of the students are irritable. Each sentence sounds fine, and the ending is believable, because tired people being irritable is easy to accept. But now slow down and picture it. All the students are in the group of tired people. The second premise says some tired people are irritable, and that word some does not say which tired people. The irritable ones could be entirely outside the students, in which case both premises are true and no student is irritable. The conclusion does not follow, so the syllogism is not valid, however believable it sounds.", "The fix is a procedure and not a feeling. Notice what your gut says about the conclusion, then set it aside on purpose. Check whether the conclusion follows from the premises by looking for an exception. Decide validity from that check and not from belief. A believable ending is exactly what makes this error easy to miss, so it is the case where you most need to run the check."], "another": ["Here is another way to see it. Imagine a friend reads you this: all of the cafe's pastries are sweet, and some sweet things are bad for your teeth, so some of the cafe's pastries are bad for your teeth. You nod before they finish, because you already believe sugary pastries are rough on teeth. That quick nod is belief bias at work. The ending matched what you think, so the argument got waved through.", "Slow it down and test the reasoning instead of the ending. Picture the pastries as sweet but gentle, say soft fruit tarts. Picture some hard candy elsewhere in the world that is sweet and bad for teeth. Both premises are true in that picture, and not one pastry from the cafe is bad for teeth. So the conclusion was never forced, even though it might well be true in real life. The premises just did not earn it.", "A handy question to ask yourself is what you would do if the ending were something you did not believe, with the very same shape. If you would hunt for an exception then, hunt for one now. The pull you feel toward a believable ending is a feeling about the world, while validity is about whether the conclusion follows. Pull those apart and you stop being fooled. Sounding right is not the same as following."]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Conditional syllogisms: the four forms cheat sheet", "plain": "All four start with If p, then q. p is the condition and q is what follows. They differ in the second premise.", "steps": ["1. Write the first premise as If p, then q.", "2. Identify the second premise: p, not q, q, or not p.", "3. Match it: p so q (valid); not q so not p (valid); q so p (not valid); not p so not q (not valid).", "4. Valid forms: go forward from p, or backward from not q."], "example": "If I study, I'll get a good grade. Form 1: I studied, so I'll get a good grade (97% correct). Form 2: I didn't get a good grade, so I didn't study (60%). Form 3: I got a good grade, so I studied (40%). Form 4: I didn't study, so I didn't get a good grade (40%).", "watchOut": "Two are valid and two are not. Both valid types were judged correctly more often than both invalid ones.", "explain": ["Conditional syllogisms are a second family, and they look different from the all, no and some kind. They have two premises and a conclusion, but the first premise is always of the form if p, then q. The letter p stands for a condition, and q stands for what follows when that condition is met. In the running example the first premise is If I study, I'll get a good grade, so p is studying and q is getting a good grade. Every one of the four types starts with that same sentence.", "The four types differ only in what the second premise says and what is concluded from it. In type 1 the second premise is p, I studied, and you conclude q, so I'll get a good grade. In type 2 the second premise is not q, I didn't get a good grade, and you conclude not p, so I didn't study. In type 3 the second premise is q, I got a good grade, and you conclude p, so I studied. In type 4 the second premise is not p, I didn't study, and you conclude not q, so I didn't get a good grade.", "Two of these four are valid and two are not. Types 1 and 2 are valid and types 3 and 4 are not. One handy way to remember the pattern is that the valid types either confirm p or deny q, while the invalid types either confirm q or deny p. Put another way, the valid ones go forward from the condition, or backward from a missing result, while the invalid ones are the reverse of that.", "What makes this interesting is how people actually did. Type 1 was judged correctly 97% of the time, type 2 only 60%, and types 3 and 4 only 40% each. So the two valid forms were both judged correctly more often than the two invalid forms, but the numbers also show that valid does not mean easy. Each pair is worth taking apart one at a time, so that you understand why each one is valid or not and not just which is which."], "another": ["Here is another way to see it, using a rule everyone trusts: if it rains, the ground is wet. Here p is it rains and q is the ground is wet. Say it rains. Then the ground is wet, no question, and that is the forward direction. Say the ground is dry. Then it cannot have rained, because rain would have left it wet, so that is the backward direction, and it works too. Those are the two valid forms.", "Now try the other two. Say the ground is wet. Did it rain? Not necessarily, because a sprinkler or a spilled bucket would do the same. The rule said what rain brings about, not that rain is the only cause. And say it did not rain. Is the ground dry? Not necessarily, for the very same sprinkler reason. Both wrong forms fail because they treat the rule as if it also said that rain is the only way to a wet ground.", "So when you explain it aloud, hang on to rain and wet ground. Rain happened, so wet. Not wet, so no rain. Those are safe. Wet, so rain? Not safe. No rain, so not wet? Not safe. The valid ones begin with p being true or with q being false, and the invalid ones begin with q being true or p being false."]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Modus ponens and modus tollens (the valid ones)", "plain": "Modus ponens: the condition is met, so the result follows. Modus tollens: the result did not happen, so the condition did not happen.", "steps": ["1. Modus ponens: If p, then q; p; therefore q.", "2. Modus tollens: If p, then q; not q; therefore not p.", "3. Test tollens in words: if studying always leads to a good grade, no good grade means no studying.", "4. Both are valid with any content."], "example": "If I study, I'll get a good grade. I didn't get a good grade. Therefore, I didn't study. Valid, though only 60% judged it correctly (vs 97% for modus ponens).", "watchOut": "Modus tollens is just as valid as modus ponens, yet people find it much harder.", "explain": ["These are the two valid conditional forms, and each has a Latin name. The first premise is the same in both: If I study, I'll get a good grade. What changes is the second premise.", "Modus ponens is the simple one. The second premise is I studied, so the condition is met, and the conclusion is Therefore, I'll get a good grade. In letters that is if p then q, p, therefore q. It moves forward, in the same direction as the rule. You know the studying happened, you know studying leads to a good grade, so the good grade has to follow. Nearly everyone gets this right, and 97% judged it correctly.", "Modus tollens is the same rule used the other way round. The second premise is I didn't get a good grade, and the conclusion is Therefore, I didn't study. In letters that is if p then q, not q, therefore not p. The way to see why it works is to think about what would have happened if the studying had happened. The rule says a good grade would then have had to follow. There was no good grade, so the studying cannot have happened. It is exactly as valid as modus ponens.", "And yet only 60% of participants judged it correctly, against 97% for modus ponens. The reason is about direction. Modus ponens runs along the rule, while modus tollens runs against it, and reasoning backward from something that did not happen is harder. So do not let the lower success rate trick you into thinking tollens is shaky. It is valid with any content, and the difficulty is in the people, not in the logic."], "another": ["Here is another way to see it. Think about a rule at a concert gate: if you have a ticket, you are let in. Modus ponens is the easy one. The guard sees you holding a ticket, so you go in. You start at the condition and walk forward along the arrow, and nobody finds that confusing.", "Modus tollens is the same rule read from the far end. Suppose the guard tells you that a certain person was not let in. What do you know about their ticket? They did not have one, because a ticket would have gotten them through. Notice that you reasoned without ever seeing their pockets. You only needed the rule and the fact that the promised result did not happen. A board game gives the same shape: if you roll a six, you move again. Your friend did not move again, so they did not roll a six.", "This feels harder only because you travel against the arrow, from a result that did not happen back to a condition that did not happen. The logic is just as solid as going forward. When you tell a friend, say that if the promised result is missing, the condition cannot have been there, because if it had been there, the result would have followed."]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Why syllogisms 3 and 4 are not valid", "plain": "If p, then q says what happens when p is true. It does not say q happens only then, and it says nothing about when p is false.", "steps": ["1. Syllogism 3 (q so p): the premise does not say studying is the only way to get a good grade.", "2. Swap in other content to test it: If I live in Tucson, then I live in Arizona; I live in Arizona; so I live in Tucson fails.", "3. Syllogism 4 (not p so not q): the premise says nothing about not studying, and a good grade could come another way.", "4. If you can find a case where the premises hold but the conclusion fails, it is not valid."], "example": "Syllogism 4: If I study, I'll get a good grade; I didn't study; therefore I didn't get a good grade. A person could get a good grade for some other reason, so the conclusion does not follow.", "watchOut": "Only 40% got each of these right. The first premise does not support either conclusion.", "explain": ["Once you accept that modus ponens and modus tollens are valid, the natural temptation is to think the other two directions work as well. They do not, and the reason is that the first premise says less than it seems to say. If p, then q tells you what happens when p is true. It does not say q happens only then, and it says nothing about what happens when p is false. Both invalid syllogisms come from reading more into the sentence than it contains.", "Syllogism 3 has the premises If I study, I'll get a good grade, and I got a good grade, and it concludes Therefore, I studied. This runs from the result back to the condition. The first premise says what happens when someone studies, but it never says that studying is the only way to get a good grade. A good grade could have come from somewhere else, so the conclusion does not follow. A trick that works nicely is to put the same form with other content. If I live in Tucson, then I live in Arizona. I live in Arizona. Therefore, I live in Tucson. Now the problem is obvious, because a person can live in Arizona without living in Tucson. The form was never reliable, and Tucson just makes that visible.", "Syllogism 4 has the premises If I study, I'll get a good grade, and I didn't study, and it concludes Therefore, I didn't get a good grade. This runs from a missing condition to a missing result. The first premise says nothing about what happens when someone does not study, and a good grade could come about some other way. It is the same kind of error as syllogism 3, only from the other side.", "Both checks come down to the same question you have used all along. Can you find a case where the premises hold but the conclusion fails? If so, it is not valid. Only 40% judged each of these correctly, which makes sense, because the conclusions feel natural while the first premise supports neither of them."], "another": ["Here is another way to see it. Take a rule so plain that nobody can argue with it: if it is a golden retriever, then it is a dog. Now try the two shaky moves. It is a dog, so it is a golden retriever? Clearly not, it could be a beagle. It is not a golden retriever, so it is not a dog? Again clearly not, because that same beagle is not a golden retriever and is still a dog. These feel silly here, but they are exactly the two invalid forms, with content that makes the flaw impossible to miss.", "Why do they fail? The rule only says where golden retrievers end up, which is inside the dog group. It says nothing about the dog group containing only golden retrievers, and nothing about animals outside the retriever group. When a conclusion feels right in another case, it is usually because you quietly added the word only to the rule. Try a cafe: if you show a student card, you get a discount. Someone got a discount, so they showed a card? Maybe they had a coupon. Someone showed no card, so they got no discount? Maybe they had the coupon.", "So when a form feels shaky, put in something like retrievers and dogs, where you already know the answer, and see whether the same shape gives nonsense. If it does, the shape is invalid wherever it shows up. The first premise supports going forward from the condition and backward from a missing result, and nothing else."]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Mental models (Johnson-Laird, 1999) and the pool table", "plain": "A mental model is a specific situation pictured in your mind that you use to judge a syllogism.", "steps": ["1. Create a model of the situation.", "2. Generate tentative conclusions about the model.", "3. Look for exceptions that falsify the model.", "4. Decide validity from whether an exception exists."], "example": "Pool table: a black ball directly above the cue ball, the green ball on the right of the cue ball, a red ball between them. The slide's answer is that the cue ball is to the left of the line of sight. Most people solve it by imagining the arrangement.", "watchOut": "One model that fits the premises but not the conclusion is enough to reject the conclusion.", "explain": ["Mental models are the answer to the problem that there is no easy method for judging validity. The idea is that people can imagine situations, and so they can reason by picturing what the statements describe. To see that you already do this, the pool table problem shows it, and it is worth working through it slowly in your head.", "The setup is this. A black ball is directly above the cue ball, and the green ball is to the right of the cue ball. A red ball is between them, meaning between the cue ball and the green ball. Then the speaker moves so that the red ball is between the speaker and the black ball. The question is which side of the speaker's line of sight the cue ball is on. Most people do not solve this with a rule. They imagine the arrangement of the balls on the table.", "Here is the picture. The black ball sits above the cue ball, and the red ball and then the green ball lie off to the cue ball's right. Now stand so the red ball blocks your view of the black ball, which means you are looking past the red ball toward the black ball. From that spot, the cue ball sits off to the left. So the answer is that the cue ball is to the left of the line of sight. You got there by holding a picture in mind, not by applying a formula.", "Johnson-Laird (1999) proposed that people use the same ability to solve deductive reasoning problems. A mental model is a specific situation represented in the mind, one concrete way the premises could be true. You create a model, generate tentative conclusions about it, and then look for exceptions that would falsify it. A conclusion is valid only if no model of the premises can refute it, so a single model that fits the premises but not the conclusion is enough to reject the conclusion."], "another": ["Here is another way to see it. Imagine three pets on a shelf. I tell you the cat is to the left of the dog, and the fish tank is to the right of the dog. Where is the cat compared with the tank? You do not need a rule. You picture cat, dog, tank in a row, and the cat is plainly to the left of the tank. That little picture is a mental model, one concrete way the statements could be true, the same kind of picture you hold when you work out the pool table.", "Now make it trickier. The hamster is to the left of the dog, and the fish tank is also to the left of the dog. Is the hamster to the left of the tank? Picture hamster, tank, dog, and it seems so. But a careful person tries another arrangement that fits the same statements: tank, hamster, dog. Both statements are still true, and now the hamster is to the right of the tank. That single picture falsifies the tentative conclusion, because the statements never decided the order. In the first shelf puzzle every arrangement you can build gives the same answer, which is why that one holds up.", "That is the whole method in plain words. Picture a situation, guess what follows, then try to build a different picture that still fits but breaks your guess. One picture that breaks it is enough. And just as with the pool table, you are not applying a formula, you are using the ability you already have to picture where things are."]}, {"module": 13, "title": "The Wason four-card problem (abstract)", "plain": "To test a rule, look for cards that could break it. This is the falsification principle.", "steps": ["1. Rule: if there is a vowel on one side, then there is an even number on the other.", "2. Ask for each card: could its hidden side break the rule?", "3. E: a non-even number behind it would break the rule, so turn it. K: not a vowel, cannot break it. 4: cannot break the rule whatever is behind it. 7: a vowel behind it would break the rule, so turn it.", "4. Answer: turn E and 7."], "example": "Cards E, K, 4, 7. 46% wrongly chose 4; only 7% chose the correct 7.", "watchOut": "The 4 feels right because it fits the rule, but it cannot break the rule; most people fail to look for falsifying cases.", "explain": ["The Wason four-card problem is a famous puzzle about testing a rule, and the difficulty is that the natural thing to do is the wrong thing. You see four cards: E, K, 4 and 7. The rule is, if there is a vowel on one side, then there is an even number on the other side. The task is to decide the minimum number of cards you must turn over to test the rule.", "The principle that rescues you is the falsification principle: to test a rule, you must look for situations that falsify it. To falsify a rule is to prove it false. So instead of asking which cards fit the rule, ask for each card whether anything on its hidden side could break the rule. Go through them one at a time. The E is a vowel, so if there is an odd number behind it, the rule is broken, which means you have to turn it. The K is not a vowel, and the rule only talks about what is behind vowels, so nothing behind K can break it. The 4 is even, and an even number fits the rule whether there is a vowel or a consonant on the back, so nothing can break it. The 7 is odd, and if there is a vowel on the other side, the rule is broken, so you have to turn the 7.", "The correct answer is therefore E and 7. Now look at how people did. 46% of participants said the 4 needed to be turned over, which is an error, and only 7% said the 7 did, which is the correct choice. The 4 is tempting because an even number is what the rule seems to be about, and finding a vowel behind it would feel like a success. But a success does not test anything, since the rule could not have failed there.", "The point is that most participants fail to look for falsifying cases. They look for evidence that agrees with the rule instead of the one situation that would break it. That is why so few picked the 7."], "another": ["Here is another way to see it. Suppose someone hands you four cards and says: if a card has a circle on one side, then the other side is blue. You can see a circle, a square, a blue card and a green card. Which ones could prove the rule wrong? Forget which cards agree with the rule, and ask of each one whether its hidden side could embarrass the rule.", "The circle could, because if the back is any color other than blue, the rule is broken, so you turn it. The green card could too, because if a circle is hiding on its back, that is a circle with a non-blue side, exactly what the rule forbids, so you turn it. The square tells you nothing, since the rule only speaks about circles. And the blue card cannot hurt the rule either: a circle behind it is fine, and a square behind it is fine, because the rule never said only circles may sit behind blue. So you turn the circle and the green card.", "The blue card is the tempting one, because finding a circle there would feel like a win. But a win that could never have been a loss proves nothing. When you explain it aloud, say that to test an if-then rule you hunt for the case that would break it, which is a circle with no blue, and you check only the cards that could be hiding that case: the circle itself, and the card that is not blue."]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Concrete Wason version and permission schemas", "plain": "A permission schema is the idea that if A is satisfied, B can be carried out. Familiar rules like a drinking age call it up and make the problem easier.", "steps": ["1. Rule: If drinking beer, then 21 years old.", "2. Find the case that breaks it: someone drinking beer who is under 21.", "3. Turn over beer (check the age) and 16 years old (check the drink).", "4. Soda and 21 years old cannot break the rule."], "example": "Cards Beer, Soda, 16 years old, 21 years old. 73% answered correctly. Being 21 is the condition; drinking beer is what can then be done.", "watchOut": "The form is the same as the vowel rule. Familiarity is not always important, so the improvement does not come from familiarity alone; context that calls up a permission schema matters.", "explain": ["The same puzzle gets dramatically easier when it is stated in everyday terms. In the concrete version, the rule is if drinking beer, then 21 years old, and the four cards read beer, soda, 16 years old and 21 years old. 73% of participants answered this version correctly, compared with 7% who found the 7 in the vowel version. When the problem is stated in concrete everyday terms, correct responses greatly increase.", "Apply the same falsification question you used on the letters. For each card, could its hidden side break the rule? The only case that breaks the rule is someone drinking beer who is under 21. So the beer card must be turned, because the person's age might be under 21. The 16 years old card must be turned too, because the drink might be beer. The soda card cannot break the rule, since the rule says nothing about people drinking soda. And the 21 years old card cannot break it either, because someone who is 21 is allowed to drink anything. So you turn beer and 16 years old.", "Why is this easier? The explanation is the permission schema, which is the idea that if A is satisfied, B can be carried out. A schema is a familiar pattern of knowledge, and a permission schema is the pattern of a rule that grants permission once a condition is met. In the beer rule, being 21 years old is the condition, and drinking beer is what can then be carried out. People are familiar with rules like this, and the rule tells them exactly what to check: if someone is drinking beer, check their age, and if someone is 16, check what they are drinking. The abstract rule about vowels and even numbers does not call up this familiar pattern, so people do not know what to look for.", "The logical form is the same in both problems. Two cautions apply. Context is important, because the same problem becomes easier in a familiar setting. But familiarity is not always important, so familiarity alone does not explain the improvement. What helps is a context that brings up a permission schema."], "another": ["Here is another way to see it. Picture a library with this rule: if someone is borrowing a book to take home, then they must have a library card. Four people stand at the desk, and you can only see one side of each. One is carrying a book out the door. One is just reading at a table. One shows a library card. One has no card. Who do you need to check?", "You probably answered without effort. Check the person leaving with a book, to see whether they have a card. Check the person with no card, to see whether they are taking a book. The reader at the table breaks nothing, because the rule never limited reading. The person with a card breaks nothing either, because a card holder is allowed to borrow or not borrow. Nobody had to explain falsification to you. You just knew this is a permission rule, a condition that must be met before an action is allowed, so the cheating to look for is the action without the permission.", "Notice that this has the same shape as the vowel and even number puzzle, so the logic did not change. What changed is that the rule matched a familiar pattern, the permission schema, and it told you what to check. The beer and 21 rule works the same way. Keep the caution in mind, though: it is not familiarity alone that helps, it is a setting that calls up the idea of permission."]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Framing effect: gain versus loss", "plain": "The same facts worded as gains or as losses change what people choose.", "steps": ["1. Gain frame (saved): sure option vs gamble; people avoid risk.", "2. Loss frame (die): sure option vs gamble; people take the risk.", "3. Check that A equals C (200 saved = 400 die) and B equals D (same gamble).", "4. Conclude that only the wording changed."], "example": "600 people at risk. A: 200 saved for certain (72% chose it); B: 1/3 chance all saved, 2/3 none. C: 400 die for certain (22%); D: 1/3 nobody dies, 2/3 all die (78%).", "watchOut": "It is a switch in strategy caused by wording alone, not a difference in the outcomes.", "explain": ["The framing effect is the finding that decisions are influenced by how a decision is stated. The wording of a choice can highlight one aspect of the situation, such as what might be gained or what might be lost, and the facts can stay exactly the same while the wording changes what people focus on. Tversky and Kahneman (1981) studied how framing changes the amount of risk people accept. When situations are framed in terms of gains, people tend toward a risk-aversion strategy, which means they prefer the sure option. When they are framed in terms of losses, people tend toward a risk-taking strategy, which means they prefer the gamble.", "The disease problem shows this. A disease is expected to kill 600 people, and two programs have been proposed. In the first version the outcomes are described in terms of people saved, which makes it the gain frame. Program A: 200 people will be saved for certain. Program B: a 1/3 probability that 600 people will be saved and a 2/3 probability that no people will be saved. 72% of people chose Program A, the sure option, and 28% chose Program B. So most people avoided the risk, even though the gamble could save everyone.", "In the second version the same disease is expected to kill 600 people, but the outcomes are described in terms of people who will die, which makes it the loss frame. Program C: 400 people will die for certain. Program D: a 1/3 probability that no one will die and a 2/3 probability that 600 people will die. This time 78% of people chose Program D, the gamble, and 22% chose Program C.", "Now compare the two versions carefully, because this is the heart of it. If 200 of 600 people are saved, then 400 die, so Program C has the same outcome as Program A. And Program D is the very same gamble as Program B. Nothing about the outcomes changed. Only the wording changed, yet most people switched from the sure option to the gamble. A statement about lives saved points to what can be gained, while a statement about deaths points to what can be lost, and that shift in attention is what moved people."], "another": ["Here is another way to see it. Suppose a pizza party has ten slices that might get ruined by a leaky oven, and you must pick a plan. Put it as saving. Plan one: five slices will be saved for certain. Plan two: a half chance that all ten are saved and a half chance that none are. Put it as losing. Plan three: five slices will be ruined for certain. Plan four: a half chance that none are ruined and a half chance that all ten are ruined.", "Now look under the words. Five saved for certain means five ruined, so plans one and three are the same plan. A half chance of saving all ten is a half chance of ruining none, so plans two and four are the same gamble. Four plans, only two real options. The framing effect says people lean toward the sure option when the choices talk about gains and toward the gamble when they talk about losses, even though nothing about the slices has changed.", "Why would wording do that? Saved points your attention at what you keep, and a sure thing feels good. Ruined points your attention at what you lose, and a sure loss feels worse than a chance of dodging it, so the gamble looks more attractive. This pizza case is only my own illustration of the pattern, not a result from anyone. What you can say aloud is that the facts stayed the same, the words changed, and the choice changed with them, so the switch comes from the wording and not from the outcomes."]}]};
